hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3601","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Jarir narrated to us, from Ubayd al-Mukattib, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Abbas or Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, said, the doubt is from Ubayd: Allah Blessed and Almighty veiled Himself from His creation with four: light, then darkness, then fire, then darkness; or fire, then darkness, then light, then darkness."
"3602","Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubadah al-Qaysi informed us, Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated to us, from Abu Hazim Salamah ibn Dinar, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Below Allah Blessed and Almighty are seventy thousand veils of light; none hears the sense of any of those veils except his soul departs. This is a weak chain."
"3603","Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Zimmani narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Umar ibn al-Hakam, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, and from Abu Hazim, from Sahl, may Allah be pleased with him, with it."
"3604","Abd Allah narrated to us, Abdah narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: Hassan ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, recited verses, and he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I."
"3605","Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Umayyah ibn Shibl, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrate from Musa, peace be upon him, upon the pulpit. He said: It occurred to Musa, peace be upon him: Does Allah Blessed and Almighty sleep? Allah Almighty sent him an angel and kept him awake three days, then gave him two flasks, a flask in each hand, and commanded him to guard them. He said: He would sleep, his hands almost meeting, then wake and hold one from the other, until he slept a sleep; his hands clashed and the flasks broke. He said: Allah Almighty struck a parable for him: If Allah Almighty slept, the heavens and earth would not hold."
"3606","Chapter: Encouragement to disobey whispering in acts of obedience. al-Harith said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Mubarak ibn Sa'id, he said: I heard Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Iblis sat for the son of Adam on his paths; he sat for him on the path of Islam and said: Do you accept Islam and leave your religion, your family, your child and your birthplace? He disobeyed him and accepted Islam. He sat for him on the path of emigration and said to him: Do you emigrate, when emigration is like a horse on a long tether that does not depart? He disobeyed him and emigrated. He sat for him on the path of jihad and said to him: Do you strive, when jihad is as its name, straining wealth and soul, so you fight and are killed, and the wife is married and the wealth divided? He disobeyed him and strove. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has these qualities in him is guaranteed by Allah Almighty, if he dies or is killed or drowns or burns, that Allah Almighty admit him to Paradise. This is mursal or mu'dal."
"3607","Book of Knowledge. Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shu'ayb informed us, Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn narrated to me, he said: It reached me that Luqman the Wise used to say: O my son, do not learn knowledge to boast with it before scholars, dispute with it the foolish, and show off with it in gatherings; nor abandon knowledge disdaining it and desiring ignorance. O my son, when you see people remembering Allah Almighty, sit with them; if you are learned your knowledge will benefit you, if ignorant they will teach you, and perhaps Allah Almighty will look upon them with His mercy and it will reach you with them."
"3608","Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Harun ibn Antarah narrated to us, from his father, he said: We entered upon Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: No man travels a path seeking knowledge therein except Allah Almighty facilitates for him a way to Paradise; and whom his deed delays, his lineage does not hasten."
"3609","Chapter: The virtue of the scholar. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, al-Sa'q ibn Hazn al-Bakri narrated to me, Aqil al-Ja'di narrated to me, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, from Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: O Ibn Mas'ud, do you know which bonds of faith are firmest? I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah; Allah and His Messenger know best. Until he said to me three times. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The firmest bonds of faith are love for Allah Almighty and hate for Allah Almighty. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ibn Mas'ud. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know which people are best? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. Until he said it three times. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of them in knowledge when they understand their religion. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ibn Mas'ud. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know which people are most learned? Until he said it three times. I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most learned see truth clearest when people differ, though deficient in deed, though crawling upon his buttocks."
"3610","Al-Tayalisi said: al-Sa'q narrated to us, and he mentioned it in brief."
"3611","Abu Ya'la said: Shayban narrated to us, al-Sa'q narrated to us, likewise."
"3612","Chapter: The protection of consensus from misguidance. Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-Shaybani, from Ibn Yusayr ibn Amr, he said: I heard my father narrate that Abu Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned the hadith, and in it: Allah Almighty would not gather the community of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon misguidance."
"3613","Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Sharik narrated to us, from Qays ibn Yusayr ibn Amr, from his father, he said: I met Abu Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the hadith, and said: Allah Blessed and Almighty would not gather the community of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon misguidance. It will come, if Allah wills, complete in the fitan."
"3614","al-Harith said: Isma'il ibn Abi Isma'il narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ubayd Allah al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty has spared you three: that you all gather upon misguidance, that the people of falsehood prevail over the people of truth, and that I supplicate against you so you perish; and He replaced this for you with the Beast, the Dajjal and the Smoke."
"3615","Chapter: Seeking the chain. Ishaq said: Sa'id ibn Amir al-Duba'i informed us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: I said to Ibrahim: You narrate to me, so chain it for me. He said: Whatever I say to you: Abd Allah said, more than one narrated it to me from Abd Allah; and when I name, it is whom I named."
"3616","Chapter: Taking by the disagreement of the Companions. Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: Nothing pleases me as the disagreement of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) like red camels; for if we take the word of these we are right, and if we take the word of these we are right. Sahih maqtu'."
"3617","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Ibn Mas'ud and Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with them both, differed about a man praying. Ubayy, may Allah be pleased with him, said: He prays in one garment. Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In two garments. That reached Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he sent to them and said: Two men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed in one fatwa; by which word shall people issue? Then he said: The word is what Ubayy, may Allah be pleased with him, said; and Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, did not fall short. Sahih mawquf."
"3618","Chapter: Rebuke against asking about what has not occurred. Ishaq said: Abu Hisham al-Makhzumi informed us, Wuhayb narrated to us, Dawud informed us, from Amir, that is al-Sha'bi, he said: Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about a matter and said: Was this after? They said: No. He said: Leave us until it is; when it is we will discuss it for you. This is mawquf, its men trustworthy, sahih if al-Sha'bi heard from Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3619","Abu Khalid Sulayman ibn Hayyan informed us, Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Tawus, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Do not hasten the trial before its descent; if you do not, the Muslims will not cease to have among them one who, when he speaks, is guided or, he said, made right. If you hasten the trial before its descent, the path will take you here and here."
"3620","Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Salt ibn Rashid, he said: I asked Tawus about a thing and he said: Was this? I said: Yes. He said: Our companions informed us, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Do not hasten the trial before its descent, and he mentioned the like, and did not raise it. This is a hasan chain."
"3621","Chapter: Brevity in fatwa. Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Thumamah ibn Hazn, he said: I was with Ubayy when a man came and said: I have not seen Abd Allah ibn Amr yesterday, and I fear to miss him, so I wish you ask him for me about something. He said: Go and ask him yourself. He said: Abd Allah was standing before his tent when a man came to the open space, came to him, then returned. He said: He informed us when he came. He said: I said: O Abd Allah ibn Amr, give me fatwa; O Abd Allah ibn Amr, give me fatwa; O Abd Allah ibn Amr. He said: Say only truth with this, pointing to his tongue; work only righteousness with this, meaning his hand; you will enter Paradise without reckoning nor punishment. He said: I said: You were brief in fatwa. He said: You came while I intended the Ka'bah, my cloak or suit spread; if I said that, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given the middle of his affair, and it was said to him: Stand and be brief. He stood and was brief, the briefest before him and after him. He said: I said: O Abd Allah ibn Amr, from every sin does Allah accept repentance? He said: Yes. Sahih mawquf."
"3622","Chapter: Prohibition of writing other than the Qur'an. Ishaq said: Ata' ibn Muslim al-Halabi informed us, he said: I said to Amr ibn Qays al-Mula'i: Write this hadith for me. He said: No; Ibrahim al-Nakha'i said: Do not write lest you rely. Then Ibrahim said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exited to us while we wrote something of hadith. He said: What is this, O Mu'adh? We said: We heard it from you, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This Qur'an suffices you besides it. We wrote nothing after. This is disconnected."
"3623","Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from Abu Burdah, he said: I wrote a book from my father, and my father said: Were it not that in it is a verse of the Book of Allah Almighty I would burn it. Then he called for a basin or tub, himself, then said: From me, from me; whatever you heard from me, for I did not write a book from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: You nearly ruined your father."
"3624","Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Khalifah ibn Qays, from Khalid ibn Urfutah, he said: I was sitting with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man of Abd al-Qays residing in al-Sus was brought. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Are you so-and-so son of so-and-so al-Abdi? He said: Yes. He struck him with a staff with him. The man said: What is mine, O Commander of the Faithful? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Sit. He sat, and he recited to him: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: Alif Lam Ra, those are the verses of the clear Book; We sent it down an Arabic Qur'an that you may understand; We narrate to you the best of stories, the verse. He recited it to him three times and struck him three times. The man said: What is mine, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: Are you the one who copied the book of Daniel? He said: Command me your command I follow. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Go and erase it with hot water and white wool; then do not read it yourself nor teach it to any Muslim. If it reaches me that you read it or taught it to any Muslim, I will destroy you with punishment. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Sit. He sat before him. He said: I went and copied a book from the People of the Book, then came with it in leather. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: What is this in your hand, O Umar? He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, a book I copied to increase knowledge to our knowledge. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry until his eyes reddened, then prayer was called in congregation. The Ansar said: Your Prophet is angry; weapons, weapons. They came until they surrounded the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I have brought it to you white pure; do not be confused, nor let the confused deceive you. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I stood and said: I am pleased with Allah as Lord, Islam as religion, and you as messenger. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended."
"3625","Chapter: Permission to write. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Abd al-Wahid ibn Qays, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I love to retain your hadith, but my heart does not retain it; shall I seek help with my right hand? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish."
"3626","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Surayj ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Mu'ammal narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I tie down knowledge by writing? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes."
"3627","Hushaym narrated to us, Juwaybir narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: We would not write in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything of the hadiths except the tashahhud and the istikhara. Juwaybir is abandoned (matruk), and al-Dahhak did not hear from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3628","Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb and Sadaqah ibn Khalid narrated, from Utbah ibn Abi Hakim, from Yazid al-Raqashi, who said: When we pressed Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) much about hadith, he would bring us some of his bags and throw them to us, and say: These are hadiths I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I wrote them and memorized them."
"3629","Al-Harith said: al-Sakan ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Imran ibn Hudayr narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz: Bashir ibn Nahik narrated to me, saying: I was with Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and I would write down some of what I heard from him; when I wanted to leave him, I brought the books and read them to him, and I said: Is this what I heard from you? He said: Yes."
"3630","Ishaq said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, Muhammad ibn Amr narrated, Abu Salamah narrated, saying: I wrote a book from her mouth, meaning Fatimah bint Qays."
"3631","Chapter: encouragement to believe what has come from Allah, Blessed and Exalted. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Bazi' Abu al-Khalil narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is informed of a virtue from Allah, Blessed and Exalted, and does not believe in it, he will not attain it. Bazi' is very weak."
"3632","Chapter: prohibition of looking into the books of the People of the Book. Ishaq informed us: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated, from al-Zuhri, that Hafsah brought a book to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the stories of Yusuf; she began reading it while the face of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) changed color, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if Yusuf were to come to you and you followed him and left me, you would go astray."
"3633","Chapter: good demeanor is from Allah, Exalted. Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) - I do not know whether he raised it to the Prophet or not - who said: Part of a man's understanding is his walk, his entrance, and his exit."
"3634","Chapter: reminding oneself with a thing. Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, al-Hayyaj ibn Bistam narrated to us, Anbasah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Salim Abu al-Ala', from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared he would forget something, he would tie a thread on his hand to remind himself by it."
"3635","Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, al-Harith ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya, from a man of Banu Tamim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you fears he will forget, let him say: Praise be to Allah, the One Who reminds the forgetful."
"3636","Chapter: putting dust on the book. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abbad ibn Abbad narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Yazid, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put dust on the book, for it is more likely to succeed."
"3637","Chapter: prohibition of concealing knowledge. Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, al-Hajari narrated to us, from Abu Iyad, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of knowledge that is not spoken of is like a treasure from which nothing is spent in the way of Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"3638","Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is asked about knowledge and conceals it will come on the Day of Resurrection bridled with a bridle of fire; and whoever speaks about the Qur'an without knowledge will come on the Day of Resurrection bridled with a bridle of fire. Sahih."
"3639","Chapter: encouragement of sincerity in knowledge. Ishaq said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd informed us, al-Salt ibn Bahram narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Do not learn knowledge to boast thereby before the scholars, nor to argue thereby with the foolish, nor to attract people's eyes; whoever does that is in the Fire."
"3640","Chapter: encouragement of conveying knowledge. Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Ibn Abi Sabrah narrated to us, from Abbas ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ashja'i, from Salim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar, from his father (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A servant will be asked on the Day of Resurrection about the surplus of his deed, just as he will be asked about the surplus of his wealth."
"3641","Chapter: dislike of pretension in knowledge. Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Ibn al-Had, from al-Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The religion will spread until it crosses the seas, and the seas will be traversed with horses in the way of Allah, Exalted; then after them will come peoples who recite the Qur'an and say: We have recited the Qur'an, who recites better than us, who understands better than us, who knows better than us? Then he turned to his Companions and said: Is there any good in those? They said: No. He said: Those are from you, from this ummah, and those are the fuel of the Fire."
"3642","Ishaq said: Ja'far ibn Awn informed us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, and he mentioned something similar."
"3643","Chapter: dislike of lying and breaking promises. Al-Harith said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu Hilal informed us, from Humayd, from Yunus ibn Jubayr, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: Prepare my gear, for I am leaving on such-and-such day. That day came while some of his gear remained, and he said: Have you finished? I said: A little remains. He said: I am leaving. I said: May Allah set the commander right, if only you stayed until the rest of your gear is finished. He said: No, I dislike telling my family a lie so that they lie to me, and breaking my promise to them so that they break theirs to me, and betraying them so that they betray me."
"3644","Chapter: warning against lying and prompting. Al-Harith said: Dawud narrated to us, Maysarah narrated to us, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever draws out a false hadith is like one who narrates it. It was said: How does one draw it out? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a man meeting a man and saying: Such-and-such happened, prompting him; so let none of you be a key to evil and falsehood. Hadith of Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him): Whoever says about me something I did not say - already mentioned in the sacrifices chapter."
"3645","Abu Ya'la said: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, saying: I prompted Salamah ibn Alqamah with a hadith, and he narrated it to me, then he came back and said: If you want to make your companion lie, prompt him."
"3646","Chapter: etiquette of the muhaddith. Musaddad said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Sirin would converse and laugh, but when the hadith came, he would become subdued. Sahih, mawquf."
"3647","Chapter: etiquette of the student. Hadith of Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) from the beginning of the hadiths of the Prophets, peace be upon them. Abu Ya'la said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Ja'far narrated to us, from Thabit who said: When I would come to Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he would call for perfume and wipe his hands and cheeks."
"3648","Chapter: scrupulousness in giving fatwa. Al-Harith said: al-Sakan ibn Nafi' narrated to us, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Abu Mijlaz who said: Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to say: O people, leave me alone; I was with one who knew more than me, and if I knew I would live until people needed me, I would have learned for you; leave me alone."
"3649","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yusuf ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Abu Khalid al-Madani, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Good news is brought by a righteous man, and bad news is brought by an evil man."
"3650","With the same isnad he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man must not deceive and wheedle."
"3651","Chapter: acting on weak hadith in virtues of deeds. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Bazi' Abu al-Khalil narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is informed of a virtue from Allah, Exalted, and does not believe in it, he will not attain it."
"3652","Chapter: verification in hadith. Abu Ya'la said: Shayban narrated to us, Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Humayd, from Imran ibn al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadiths that I heard and memorized; nothing prevents me from narrating them except that I see my companions differing from me about them."
"3653","Chapter: mutual study. Musaddad said: Isma'il narrated to us, al-Jurayri and Abu Salamah informed us, from Abu Nadrah, who said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Narrate to one another, for hadith reminds of hadith. Sahih, mawquf."
"3654","Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Abu Nadrah, who said: We would come to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), and he would say: Narrate, for hadith stirs up hadith."
"3655","Al-Harith said: Rawh narrated to us, from Kahmas ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Nadrah, who said: I said to Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him): Dictate to us in writing. He said: I will never write for you; take from me as we used to take from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Narrate, for hadith reminds of one another."
"3656","Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: Narrate hadith at length so that it does not become obsolete. Sahih, maqtu'."
"3657","Chapter: condemnation of giving fatwa by opinion. Abu Ya'la said: al-Hudhayl ibn Ibrahim al-Himmani narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This ummah will act for a while by the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, then will act for a while by the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then will act by opinion; and when they act by opinion they will have gone astray and led astray."
"3658","Chapter: narrating by meaning. Abu Dawud said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, who said: While Ubayd ibn Umayr was narrating, with Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) present, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of the hypocrite is like a sheep between two folds: when it comes to these they butt it, and when it comes to those they butt it. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: It is not so; he only said: between two flocks. Ubayd ibn Umayr said: Between two folds and two flocks is one. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying it, I would not have said it."
"3659","Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Bahili narrated to us, Ibn Dawud narrated to us, Asim ibn Raja' ibn Haywah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Malik, and Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, and Makhul, who said: When Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he would say: Like this, or of this form."
"3660","Abu Abd Allah al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us with the same."
"3661","Muhammad ibn Qudamah narrated to us, Ma'n ibn Isa narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid with the same."
"3662","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Asbagh ibn Zayd al-Warraq narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Kathir, from Khalid ibn Durayk, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says about me what I did not say, or claims something from me, or affiliates himself to other than his masters, let him take his seat between the two eyes of Hell. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, does it have two eyes? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; have you not heard the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: When it sees them from a distant place, they will hear its raging and roaring? So we refrained from narrating until he noticed that in our conduct, and he said to us: Why do I not hear you narrating? We said: O Messenger of Allah, how can we narrate when you have said what you said, while we do not render the hadith exactly, putting forward and back, adding and omitting? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I did not mean that; I only meant one who intends to fault me and to disgrace Islam."
"3663","Chapter: vastness of knowledge. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from a man of Banu Abs who said: I was with Salman (may Allah be pleased with him), and we passed by the Tigris, and he said: O brother of Banu Abs, dismount and drink. So I dismounted and drank. Then he said: Dismount and drink. So I dismounted and drank. He said: O brother of Banu Abs, how much did your drinking diminish the Tigris? I said: How could my drinking diminish the Tigris? He said: Such is knowledge; it does not perish, so take from it what benefits you."
"3664","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, and I think he raised it to the Prophet, who said: Two greedy ones never satisfy their greed: one greedy in seeking knowledge never satisfies his greed, and one greedy in seeking wealth never satisfies his greed. I say: Layth is weak, but it has a witness from Ibn Mas'ud with al-Tabarani, and from Anas with Ibn Adi, both raising it to the Prophet, and from al-Hasan as mursal, and its chain to al-Hasan is sahih."
"3665","Chapter: encouragement of spreading knowledge. Ishaq said: Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid al-Dimashqi informed us, from al-Awza'i: Abu Kathir narrated to me that he heard his father say: I came to Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was at the middle jamrah with people gathered asking him for fatwas; a man came and stood before him and said: Did not the Commander of the Believers forbid you from giving fatwa? He raised his head to him and said: Are you a watcher over me? If you placed the sword on this - and he pointed to his neck - then I thought I could carry out a word I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before you could execute me, I would carry it out. This is a sahih hadith; al-Bukhari cited part of it in the Book of Knowledge."
"3666","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated, from Abu A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and mentioned a hadith, in which: Whoever learns knowledge, is humble in knowledge, and teaches it to the servants of Allah, Exalted, seeking thereby what is with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, there will be none in Paradise with better reward or greater rank than him, and there will be no rank or lofty precious degree in Paradise but he will have therein the fullest share and the fullest stations. Verily knowledge is the best worship and the pivot of religion is scrupulousness; the scholar is only one who acts on his knowledge, even if his knowledge is little."
"3667","Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Abu Sinan Dirar ibn Murrah narrated, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from a shaykh of Nakha' who said: I entered the mosque of Iliya' and prayed two rak'ahs by a pillar; a man came and prayed nearby, and people inclined toward him, and behold it was Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with them both). Then a messenger of Yazid ibn Mu'awiyah came, saying: Answer. He said: This one wants to forbid me from narrating, just as his father used to forbid me, while I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, and he mentioned a hadith."
"3668","Chapter: meanings of the letters. Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hafs ibn Abd Allah al-Ifriqi narrated, Hakim ibn Nafi' narrated, from al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked, and he mentioned a hadith raised to the Prophet, in which he said: As for Abu Jad: the ba' is the splendor of Allah, the jim is the beauty of Allah, the dal is the religion of Allah which He chose for Himself, His angels, His prophets, His messengers, and His righteous creation. As for Hawwaz: the ha' is the disgrace of the people of the Fire, the zay is the roaring of Hell upon the enemies of Allah and the people of sins. As for Hutti: the sins of the sinners are cast off by seeking forgiveness. As for Kalaman: the kaf is the perfection of the people of Paradise, and they said: Praise be to Allah Who fulfilled His promise to us. As for the nun: it is the fish whose liver they will eat before entering Paradise. As for Sa'fas: a measure for a measure and retaliation for retaliation, and as you judge you will be judged. As for Qarashat: they will be presented for reckoning."
"3669","Chapter: the Qur'an confirming the Sunnah. Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated, Isra'il narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Arqam ibn Shurahbil, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When I narrate to you a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and you do not find its confirmation in the Book of Allah, Exalted, nor find it good in people's morals, then it is false."
"3670","Chapter: encouragement of holding to the Sunnah. Al-Harith said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from Awf, from a man he named - I think he said: Sa'id ibn Khuthaym - from a man of the Ansar among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah be pleased with them) who settled in Syria, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) exhorted us with an admonition from which skins grew sick, eyes shed tears, and hearts trembled; so we said: As if this is a farewell admonition, O Messenger of Allah, what do you enjoin upon us? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That you fear Allah, Exalted, and follow my Sunnah and the Sunnah of the rightly-guided rightly-guiding caliphs after me; bite upon it with the molar teeth, for every innovation is misguidance."
"3671","Affan narrated to us, Abu al-Ashhab narrated, Sa'id ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, from a man of the people of Syria who said: A man of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) narrated to him, saying: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, and he mentioned something similar."
"3672","Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Walid al-Qurashi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, Zuhayr ibn Muhammad informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam who said: He used to see Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) with the button of his shirt undone; he was asked about that, and he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing it."
"3673","Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Kathir ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Awf narrated to me, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever revives a sunnah of mine and people act upon it, he will have the reward of whoever acts upon it without diminishing anything from their rewards; and whoever innovates and it is acted upon, upon him will be the burdens of whoever acts upon it without diminishing anything from the burdens of those who acted upon it."
"3674","Abd said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us with the same."
"3675","Chapter: traveling in search of knowledge. Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated, saying: I heard Abu Sa'd al-A'ma narrating from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, saying: Abu Ayyub went out to Uqbah ibn Amir while he was in Egypt to ask him about a hadith he heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the story."
"3676","Abu Ya'la said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated, Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated, Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Farigh who said: Abu Sayyad narrated to him that he was once with Maslamah at midday when a man entered upon him on his mount and sought permission to see Maslamah, saying: O Maslamah; Maslamah ordered a slave-girl of his, saying: See who this is. She said: An old man who has come on his mount. He said: Call Maslamah for me. I said: Shall I call the commander for you? He said: Go back to him and ask him who you are. He said: I am so-and-so. Maslamah rose quickly; the man was among the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them). He said: I heard a hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the closest of the people to him that day was Uqbah ibn Amir (may Allah be pleased with him), so I wished to ask him about it to verify; so come with me, O Maslamah, to him. He said: Rather I will send for him and he will come to me. He said: Your authority has pleased you; order Abu Sayyad to go with me to Uqbah. When Uqbah (may Allah be pleased with him) saw him, he welcomed him and took his hand. The man said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, and he mentioned the hadith."
"3677","Al-Harith said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated, Yahya Abu Hisham al-Dimashqi narrated, saying: A man of the people of Madinah came to Egypt and said to the chamberlain of its commander: Tell the commander to come out to me. The chamberlain said: No one has said this to us since we settled here except you; it used to be said: Ask permission for us to see the commander. He said: Go to him and tell him: So-and-so is at the door. The commander came out to him, and he said: I have only come to you to ask you about a hadith concerning covering the nakedness of a Muslim."
"3678","Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated, from Abu Imran al-Jawni: Jundub narrated, saying: I came to Madinah seeking knowledge, and people were in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in circles conversing. I kept going to the circles until I came to a circle containing a pale man wearing two garments as if he had just come from travel; I heard him saying: The people of knots have perished, by the Lord of the Ka'bah; I feel no sorrow for them - he said it three times. I sat with him, and he spoke what was decreed for him, then stood up; when he stood I asked about him: Who is this? They said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), the chief of the Muslims. So I followed him until he reached his house, and he was of shabby appearance and shabby dress, one resembling the other. I greeted him and he returned the greeting, then asked me: From where are you? I said: From the people of Iraq. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The most questioning of things. When he said that, I became angry and knelt on my knees, faced the qiblah, raised my hands, and said: O Allah, we complain of them to You: we spend our wealth, tire our bodies, and send our mounts seeking knowledge, and when we meet them they frown at us and say to us. Ubayy (may Allah be pleased with him) wept and began to appease me, saying: Woe to you, go over there. Then he said: O Allah, I pledge to You that if You keep me alive until Friday, I will speak what I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and will not fear for Your sake the blame of any blamer. Then I think he stood. When he said that, I left him and kept waiting for Friday to hear his speech. When Thursday came, I went out for some need, and the streets were crowded with people; I would not take a street but people met me. I said: What is the matter with the people? They said: Do you suppose yourself a stranger? I said: Yes. They said: The chief of the Muslims, Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), has died. Then I met Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) in Iraq and narrated the hadith to him, and he said: O grief, would that he had lived to convey to us the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3679","Chapter: encouragement of seeking knowledge. Musaddad said: Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, saying: I heard my father saying: Knowledge is better than gold, and a righteous soul is better than pearls. Mawquf, sahih."
"3680","Abu Ya'la said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Karim, from Abu Ammar, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The seeker of knowledge - or the possessor of knowledge - everything seeks forgiveness for him, even the fish in the sea."
"3681","Hudhayl al-Himmani narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, from Hammad, from Abu Wa'il, from Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim."
"3682","Al-Hudhayl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Mujashi' ibn Yusuf narrated, Yazid ibn Rabi'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to me, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever seeks knowledge and attains it, Allah, Exalted, gives him two shares of reward; and whoever seeks knowledge and does not attain it, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gives him one share of reward. He explained it, saying: Whoever seeks knowledge and attains it, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, gives him the reward of what he learned and the reward of what he acted upon; and whoever seeks knowledge and does not attain it, Allah, Exalted, gives him the reward of what he learned, and the reward of what he did not learn falls away from him."
"3683","Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Ja'far ibn Maymun: al-Raqashi narrated to us, saying: Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: They would, when they prayed the morning prayer, sit in circles reciting the Qur'an and learning the obligations and the sunnahs."
"3684","Ibn Abi Umar said: Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Maslamah al-Qa'nabi, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, Exalted, is not worshipped with anything better than understanding of religion."
"3685","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Yazid ibn Iyad narrated, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is not worshipped with anything better than understanding of religion; and one learned man is harder upon Satan than a thousand worshippers."
"3686","With the same isnad he said: Everything has a pillar, and the pillar of this religion is understanding."
"3687","Al-Harith said: Isma'il narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, Humayd ibn Abi Suwayd narrated, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Teach and do not be harsh, for the teacher is better than the harsh one."
"3688","Al-Tayalisi said: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi', from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque while some people were remembering Allah, Blessed and Exalted, and some were studying jurisprudence; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Both gatherings are upon good: as for those who remember Allah, Exalted, and ask their Lord, if He wills He gives them and if He wills He withholds from them; and these teach people and learn; I was only sent as a teacher, and this is better. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with them."
"3689","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated with the same."
"3690","Abu Ya'la said: Uqbah narrated to us, Mas'adah ibn al-Yasa' narrated, from Shibl ibn Abbad, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him): A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Which people know most? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who joins the knowledge of people to his knowledge; and every possessor of knowledge is still hungry for more."
"3691","Al-Harith said: Abd Allah ibn Awn narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Fadl narrated, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Ja'far al-Abdi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The superiority of the scholar over the worshipper is like my superiority over my ummah."
"3692","Abu Ya'la said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Abd Allah al-Rumi narrated, saying: I heard al-Khalil ibn Murrah narrating from Mubashshir, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The scholar is preferred over the worshipper by seventy degrees, between every two degrees as between heaven and earth."
"3693","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Teach, make easy and do not make hard - he said it three times - and when you become angry, be silent."
"3694","Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Ibn Ulathah narrated, Khusayf narrated, from Mujahid, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever memorizes for my ummah forty hadiths of what benefits them in their religion will be raised on the Day of Resurrection among the scholars; and the scholar is preferred over the worshipper by seventy degrees, Allah knows best what is between every two degrees."
"3695","Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Shami al-Abbadani narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated, from Nuh ibn Dhakwan, from his brother Ayyub, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not inform you about the most generous? The Most Generous is Allah, Exalted; and I am the most generous of the children of Adam, and the most generous of them after me is a man who learned knowledge and spread it - he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone - and a man who strove with himself in the way of Allah until he was killed."
"3696","Hammam narrated to us, al-Walid narrated, from a man, from Uthman ibn Abi Sawdah, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned a hadith, in which he said: The death of a scholar is a calamity that cannot be repaired, a breach that cannot be closed; he is a star that has been effaced; the death of a tribe is easier than the death of a scholar."
"3697","Chapter: the hadith clarifying the general statements of the Qur'an. Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan who said: While Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) was sitting with companions narrating to them, a man said: Narrate to us only from the Qur'an, or: we want only the Qur'an. He said: What would you say if you and your companions were left to the Qur'an alone - would you find the noon prayer four rak'ahs, the afternoon prayer four, the sunset prayer three with recitation in the first two rak'ahs? What would you say if you and your companions were left to the Qur'an alone - would you find in every two hundred five, and in camels so-and-so, and in cattle so-and-so? What would you say if you and your companions were left to the Qur'an alone - would you find circumambulation of the House seven times, and between al-Safa and al-Marwah so-and-so?"
"3698","Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari narrated, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated, from al-Hasan, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps a man reclining on his couch will belie me when a hadith reaches him from me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not say this - leave this and bring what is in the Qur'an. Isma'il said: I narrated it to Amr ibn Ubayd, and he said: Al-Hasan narrated to us, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him). I said: Come with us to al-Hasan. So we came to al-Hasan and asked him about the hadith, and he said: Yazid al-Raqashi narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3699","Chapter: the Qur'an comprising all rulings in general and in detail. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah: Abu Ishaq narrated, from Murrah who said: Abd Allah said: Whoever wants knowledge should stir up the Qur'an, for in it is the knowledge of the first and the last."
"3700","Chapter: warning against lying. Abu Ya'la said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya and Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Mawsili both said: Sayf ibn Harun al-Burjumi narrated to us, from Ismah ibn Bashir who said: al-Faza' narrated to me who said: al-Munaqqa' narrated to me who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the charity of our camels, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the charity of our camels. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered concerning it and it was distributed. I said: O Messenger of Allah, among it is what is a gift for you and charity. So I separated the gift from the charity, and I stayed some days; people rumored that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) to the slaves of Egypt to collect their charity. I said: We have wealth, and my family has no wealth with them - should I not collect from them before he comes to me? So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was upon a she-camel with a black man who had drawn level with the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I never saw anyone taller. When I drew near him, he lunged at me, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) restrained him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, people have rumored that you will send Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) to the slaves of Egypt to collect their charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands until we saw the whiteness of his armpits, then said: O Allah, I do not permit them to lie about me. Al-Munaqqa' said: I never narrated a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except a hadith uttered by a book or reported by a sunnah; he is lied about in his life, so how after his death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"3701","Book of Knowledge, continued. Chapter: the Qur'an comprising all rulings in general and in detail. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah: Abu Ishaq narrated, from Murrah who said: Abd Allah said: Whoever wants knowledge should stir up the Qur'an, for in it is the knowledge of the first and the last."
"3702","Chapter: warning against lying. Abu Ya'la said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya and Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Mawsili both said: Sayf ibn Harun al-Burjumi narrated, from Ismah ibn Bashir: al-Faza' narrated, al-Munaqqa' narrated, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the charity of our camels, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the charity of our camels. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered concerning it and it was distributed. I said: O Messenger of Allah, among it is what is a gift for you and charity. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Separate it. So I separated the gift from the charity, and I stayed some days; people rumored that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) to the slaves of Mudar to collect their charity. I said: We have wealth and my family has no wealth with them - should I not collect from them before he comes to my family? So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was upon a she-camel with a black man who had drawn level with the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I never saw anyone taller. When I drew near him, he lunged at me, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) restrained him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, people have rumored that you will send Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) to the slaves of Mudar to collect their charity. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands until we saw the whiteness of his armpits, then said: O Allah, I do not permit them to lie about me. Al-Munaqqa' said: I never narrated a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except a hadith uttered by a book or reported by a sunnah; he is lied about in his life, so how after his death (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"3703","Chapter: refreshing the hearts so they comprehend. Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Imran ibn Hudayr, from Qasamah ibn Zuhayr who said: Refresh the hearts, they will comprehend remembrance."
"3704","And from Hammad, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Converse with these hearts, for they quickly fade."
"3705","Abu Ya'la said: Sulayman ibn Umar ibn Khalid Abu Ayyub al-Raqqi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from al-Kalbi, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Qur'an was recited and poetry was recited before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, Qur'an and poetry in one gathering? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes."
"3706","Chapter: warning against lying about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Harun, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"3707","Fudayl narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Talhah, from Abu Ammar, from Amr ibn Shurahbil (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies about me deliberately to mislead people thereby, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"3708","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, Ibn Hubayrah narrated, saying: I heard a shaykh of Himyar mentioning that he heard Qays ibn Sa'd - while he was over Egypt - saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take a resting place or a house in Hell."
"3709","Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Abu Muhammad ibn Ma'bad who said: I heard Ma'bad ibn Ka'b narrating that Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to them and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever says about me something I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"3710","Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Zimmani narrated to us, Abd al-Samad reported, Dujayn ibn Thabit al-Yarbu'i narrated, saying: I entered the mosque and there was an old man sitting beside the pulpit, called Salim or Aslam, who said: I used to travel with Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and recite rajaz poetry for him; he would not narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we said to him: Would that you narrated to us. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"3711","Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, Muslim narrated, from al-Dujayn, from Aslam the freedman of Umar, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the same."
"3712","Sufyan, meaning Ibn Waki', narrated to us, my father narrated, from al-Dujayn with the same."
"3713","Al-Fadl ibn Sukayn narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Isa ibn Musa ibn Talhah - a trustworthy Kufan - narrated, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever lies about me deliberately - the hadith."
"3714","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, Jariyah ibn Harim narrated, Abd Allah ibn Darim narrated to me, Abd Allah ibn Busr al-Hubrani narrated, saying: I heard Abu Kabshah al-Anmari - who had companionship - narrating from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies about me deliberately, or rejects something I commanded, let him take a house in Hell."
"3715","Shabab ibn Khayyat narrated to us, Salm ibn Qutaybah narrated, Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah al-Fazari narrated, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsajah, from al-Bara' (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"3716","Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated, Sadaqah ibn al-Muthanna narrated, Riyah ibn al-Harith narrated to me, saying: We were with al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was in the mosque with the people of Kufah; Sa'id ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and al-Mughirah (may Allah be pleased with him) made room for him, saying: Here, sit. So he seated him with him on the couch. Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Lying about me is not like lying about anyone; whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"3717","Book of Heart-Softeners. Chapter: the usual lifespan. Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When a servant reaches sixty years, Allah, Exalted, has removed excuses from him by age - or he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has conveyed to him the limit of age. Al-Tabarani narrated it in his Large Mu'jam from Yusuf al-Qadi, from Sulayman; and al-Ruyani narrated it in his Musnad from al-Saghani, from Khalaf ibn Hisham; and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated it in his Musnad from Arim, all of them from Hammad ibn Zayd with it. This is a sahih isnad, but it has a defect: more than one narrated it from Abu Hazim, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him); and from this wording al-Bukhari cited it as a mu'allaq report. So if Hammad ibn Zayd preserved it, it may be that he heard it from two wordings."
"3718","Chapter: mention of death and short hopes. Musaddad said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ghaylan ibn Bishr, from Ya'la ibn al-Walid, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: It was said to him: What do you wish for one you love? He said: Death. He said: If he does not die? He said: That his wealth and children be few."
"3719","Abu Dawud said: al-Hasan ibn Abi Ja'far narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) said to me: O Muhammad, live as you wish, for you will die; love whom you love, for you will part from him; and do as you wish, for you will meet it."
"3720","Abd said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The gift of the believer is death."
"3721","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hammam narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to me, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated with the same."
"3722","Abu Bakr said: Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Wonder at one heedless while he is not neglected; wonder at a seeker of the world while death seeks him; wonder at one laughing heartily while not knowing whether Allah is pleased with him or angry with him."
"3723","Al-Harith said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Yunus narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Urwah, from Urwah who said: A woman died while the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were laughing about her; so-and-so said: Woe to her, she has rested. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only one who is forgiven rests. Its isnad is mursal, its men trustworthy."
"3724","Al-Khalil ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Habib ibn al-Shahid informed us, al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan narrated, from Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of the believers is most astute? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The most astute of the believers are those who remember death most and are best prepared for it."
"3725","Yahya ibn Hashim narrated to us, Abu Khalid Amr ibn Khalid informed us, from Zayd ibn Ali, from his forefathers (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which people are most astute? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most astute of people are those who remember death most and are best prepared for Allah."
"3726","Chapter: beneficial counsels. Abu Bakr said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Thawban the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or other than him among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with them), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Let one of you take a remembering tongue, or a grateful heart, or a believing wife who helps him upon his faith, or helps one of you upon his faith. I cited it because of the doubt in it; al-Tirmidhi and Ibn Majah narrated it through Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) alone, with a more complete wording."
"3727","Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Ali ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Abd Allah informed us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us; he said: And Ibrahim ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, from Hibban ibn Abi Jabalah who said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: You give birth for death, build for ruin, covet what perishes, and leave what remains; how beloved to you of these three: death, sickness, and poverty. I say: Abu Nu'aym narrated it in the biography of Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) in al-Hilyah, through Abd Allah ibn Wahb, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ubayd Allah ibn Zahr, saying: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said, and he mentioned it, but he said at its end: How beloved are the two disliked ones: death and poverty; and he did not mention anyone between Ubayd Allah and Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3728","Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated, Duwayd narrated, from Abd al-Wahid who said: Isa ibn Maryam (peace be upon him) said: O children of Adam, give birth for death and build for ruin; your souls perish and your houses remain."
"3729","Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, al-Thawri narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abis: Abu Iyas narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to say in his sermon: The truest speech is the Speech of Allah; the firmest handhold is the word of piety; the best religion is the religion of Ibrahim (peace be upon him); the best stories are this Qur'an; the best ways are the way of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the noblest speech is the remembrance of Allah, Exalted; the best matters are their firmest; the worst matters are their invented ones; the best guidance is the guidance of the Prophets, peace be upon them; the noblest death is the killing of martyrs; the most misguided of misguidance is misguidance after guidance; the best deed or knowledge - Bishr doubted - is what benefits; the best guidance is what is followed; the worst blindness is blindness of the heart; the upper hand is better than the lower hand; what is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts; a soul you save is better than authority you cannot encompass; the worst expiation is expiation at the presence of death; the worst regret is the regret of the Day of Resurrection; among people are those who do not come to Friday or prayer except at the end, and do not remember Allah, Exalted, except with foul speech; the greatest of sins is the lying tongue; the best wealth is wealth of the soul; the best provision is piety; the head of wisdom is fear of Allah, Exalted; the best cast into the heart is certainty; doubt is from disbelief; wailing is from the deeds of ignorance; misappropriation is from the embers of Hell; hoarded treasure is branding with the Fire; poetry is the flutes of Satan; wine is the gatherer of sin; women are the snares of Satan; youth is a branch of madness; the worst earnings are the earnings of usury; the worst food is eating the wealth of orphans; the fortunate is one admonished by others; the wretched is one wretched in his mother's womb; one of you needs only what his soul is content with; he will only end up in a place of four cubits; the best matter is its immediate; the most controlling of deeds are their endings; the worst narrations are narrations of lies; all that is coming is near; reviling a Muslim is sinfulness, fighting him is disbelief, eating his flesh is among disobediences to Allah, Exalted, and the sanctity of his wealth is like the sanctity of his blood; whoever swears presumptuously about Allah, Exalted, Allah belies him; whoever forgives, Allah forgives him; whoever pardons, Allah pardons him; whoever swallows anger, Allah, Exalted, rewards him; whoever is patient over calamities, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, aids him; whoever knows affliction is patient over it; whoever does not know it denies it; whoever denies it, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, abandons him; whoever seeks reputation, Allah makes him heard of; whoever intends the world, it defeats him; whoever obeys Satan disobeys Allah, Mighty and Majestic; and whoever disobeys Allah, Exalted, He punishes him."
"3730","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yusuf ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, Abu Hamzah - al-A'war, whose name is Maymun - narrated, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to preach every Thursday evening with this sermon; he said: And we used to think it was the sermon of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O people, the truest speech is the Book of Allah, Exalted; the best guidance is the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the worst matters are their invented ones; every invented matter is an innovation, every innovation is misguidance. Verily, O people, you will be stopped on one plain; sight will encompass you and the caller will make you hear; the wretched is one wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is one admonished by others."
"3731","Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated, from Irak ibn Malik, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from al-Bahili, who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stood among the people preaching upon their entry into Syria at al-Jabiyah, saying: Learn the Qur'an, you will be known by it; act by it, you will be of its people; none who is obeyed in disobedience to Allah, Exalted, will attain the rank of one deserving right; know that speaking truth and great admonition neither brings an appointed time near nor keeps provision away; know that between a servant and his provision is a veil: his provision appears to him, and if he plunges through, the veil is torn and he does not attain beyond his provision; discipline horses, practice archery, wear sandals, use the tooth-stick, adopt the ways of Ma'add; beware the manners of the non-Arabs, neighboring tyrants, that a cross be seen among you, that you sit at a table upon which wine is drunk, that you enter the bath without a waist-wrapper, that you leave your women to enter baths, for that is not lawful; beware acquiring from the non-Arabs after settling in their lands what keeps you in their land, for you are about to return to your lands; beware the yellow dye, putting it on your necks; upon you is the wealth of the Arabs: livestock, moving with it wherever you move; know that drinks are made from raisins, honey, and dates: what is aged of it is wine, not lawful; know that Allah, Exalted, will not purify three persons, nor look at them, nor bring them near on the Day of Resurrection: a man who gave his pledge to a leader seeking thereby the world - if he attains it he fulfills to him, and if he does not attain it he does not fulfill to him; a man who went out with merchandise after the afternoon prayer and swore he was offered so-and-so for it, and it was bought for his word; reviling a Muslim is sinfulness and fighting him is disbelief; it is not lawful for you to abandon your brother for more than three days; whoever comes to a sorcerer, soothsayer, or fortune-teller and believes him in what he says has disbelieved in what was revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3732","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Ala' Abu Muhammad al-Thaqafi informed us, saying: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I served the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was eight years old; the first thing he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me was his saying to me: O my son, perfect your ablution - and he mentioned the hadith, at the end of which: Do not spend night or morning with deceit toward any of the people of Islam in your heart, for this is of my Sunnah; whoever takes my Sunnah has loved me, and whoever loves me is with me in Paradise. O my son, if you act by this and memorize my counsel, let nothing be more beloved to you than death, for in it is your rest."
"3733","Abu Ya'la said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi Yazid narrated, Abbad al-Minqari narrated, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned the like, more complete than it."
"3734","Khalid ibn Mirdas narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Badr narrated, from Abu Hazim, from Hafs ibn Ubayd Allah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned it, adding in it: Greet in your house, Allah, Exalted, will increase your blessings; honor the elder of the Muslims and have mercy on their young; I and you will come like these two. And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined his fingers together."
"3735","Mansur ibn Abi Muzahim narrated to us, Umar ibn Abi Khalifah narrated, from Dirar ibn Muslim who said: I heard him mentioning it, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counselled me, and he mentioned the hadith, at the end of which: If you sleep pure, then die - you die a martyr. O Anas, honor the elder and have mercy on the young."
"3736","Abd said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ziyad, that is Abu al-Miqdam, informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi who said: I knew Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was our governor in Madinah, a young man of thick build and full body; when he became caliph and endured of work and worry what he endured, his condition changed, and I kept looking at him, hardly turning my gaze from him. He said: O Ibn Ka'b, you look at me with a look you did not look at me with before. He said: I said: You please me. He said: What pleases you? He said: What has changed of your color, what has gone of your hair, and what has thinned of your body. He said: How would it be if you saw me after three days, when my eyes flow upon my face, and my nostrils and mouth flow with pus and worms - would you find me more unrecognizable? Repeat to me a hadith you narrated to me from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both). He said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to me, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Everything has a nobility, and the noblest gatherings are those faced toward the qiblah; one is only sat with in trust; kill the snake and the scorpion even if you are in your prayer; whoever loves to be the noblest of people, let him fear Allah, Exalted; whoever loves to be the strongest of people, let him rely upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic; whoever loves to be the richest of people, let him be more confident in what is in Allah's Hand, Blessed and Exalted, than in what is in his own hand. Shall I not inform you of your worst? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who alights alone, withholds his help, and beats his servant. Shall I not inform you of worse than this? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One whom people hate and who hates them. Shall I not inform you of worse than this? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who does not overlook a slip, nor accept an excuse, nor forgive a sin. Shall I not inform you of worse than this? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One whose good is not hoped for, and whose evil is not safe from. Isa son of Maryam (peace be upon him) stood among his people and said: O Children of Israel, do not speak wisdom to the ignorant, lest you wrong it; nor withhold it from its people, lest you wrong them; do not wrong, nor repay a wrongdoer with wrong, lest your merit with your Lord be nullified. O Children of Israel, matters are three: a matter whose rightness is clear, so follow it; a matter whose error is clear, so avoid it; and a matter disputed about, so refer it to its scholar."
"3737","Al-Harith said: Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad narrated, Abu al-Miqdam narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b who said: I knew Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was our governor in Madinah in the time of al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik, a young man, and he mentioned the same. I say: In the Sunan is something of its beginnings."
"3738","Abu Ya'la said: Rawh ibn Hatim narrated to us, Hushaym narrated, from al-Kawthar ibn Hakim, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out one day to the mosque, and there were people conversing whose talk made them laugh; he stood, greeted, and said: Remember the destroyer of pleasures: death. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out after that another time, and there were people conversing and laughing, and he said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also came out, and there were people conversing and laughing; he greeted, then said: Verily Islam began strange and will return strange; blessedness for the strangers on the Day of Resurrection. It was said to him: Who are the strangers, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Those who are righteous when people are corrupt."
"3739","Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated, from a man of Nakha' who said: I witnessed Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) when death came to him, saying: I will narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Worship Allah, Exalted, as if you see Him; if you do not see Him, He sees you; count yourself among the dead; fear the supplications of the oppressed, for they are answered; whoever of you can attend the last evening prayer and the morning prayer in congregation, let him do so, even crawling. Sahih, were it not for the unnamed man."
"3740","Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated, from Mansur, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Worship Allah, Exalted, as if you see Him - and he mentioned it mawquf up to his saying: the oppressed. And he added: Know that little that suffices you is better than much that distracts you; religion does not wear out, and goodness is not forgotten."
"3741","Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Zakariyya al-Kufi, from a man who narrated to him, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man three things and counselled him with three: as for those he forbade him, he said: Do not break a covenant, nor help in breaking it, nor commit transgression, for whoever is transgressed against, Allah, Exalted, will surely aid him; beware the scheming of evil, for evil scheming encompasses none but its people, and for them is a Seeker from Allah, Exalted. As for those he counselled him with: Remember death much, for it consoles you from all else; upon you is supplication, for you do not know when it will be answered for you; upon you is gratitude, for it is increase. Then Sufyan recited: If you are grateful, I will surely increase you."
"3742","Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated, saying: I heard Uqbah ibn Muhammad al-Madani narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, narrating from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: With Allah, Blessed and Exalted, are treasuries of good and evil whose keys are men."
"3743","Chapter: good character. Ishaq said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, from Talhah ibn Amr, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The best of you are the best of you in character."
"3744","Chapter: guarding the religion and sacrificing wealth and life for it. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated, from Qatadah, from Yunus ibn Jubayr who said: We escorted Jundub to the fortress of al-Mukatab, and we said to him: Counsel us. He said: Upon you is the Qur'an, for it is the light of the dark night and the guide of day; act by it whatever effort and poverty there be. If a trial comes, offer your wealth before yourself; if the trial exceeds, offer your wealth and yourself before your religion; for the deprived is one deprived of his religion, the plundered is one plundered of his religion; no wealth avails after the Fire, no poverty impoverishes after Paradise; the Fire - its captive is not freed, its poor is not enriched. Sahih, mawquf."
"3745","Abu Dawud said: Abd al-Hakam ibn Dhakwan narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Among the worst of people in station is one who sacrificed his Hereafter for the world of another."
"3746","Chapter. Abu Ya'la said: Mansur ibn Abi Muzahim narrated to us, Umar ibn Shabib narrated to us, from Yusuf ibn al-Sabbagh, from al-Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) - I know it only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever witnesses a matter and dislikes it is like one absent from it; and whoever is absent from a matter and is pleased with it is like one who witnessed it."
"3747","Chapter: straitness upon the believer in the world. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Musa Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Harawi and Abu Ma'mar narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Muhammad al-Warraq narrated, Musa al-Juhani narrated, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Atiyyah ibn Amir al-Juhani who said: I heard Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O Salman, the world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever."
"3748","Chapter. Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated, Isa ibn Sabrah narrated to me, narrating from one who heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), raising it to the Prophet, saying: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gives the world upon the intention for the Hereafter, but does not give the Hereafter upon the intention for the world."
"3749","Chapter: merit of righteous deeds in evil times. Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri' narrated to us, Haywah narrated, Shurahbil ibn Sharik informed us, that he heard Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli narrating that he heard Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: A good deed I do today is more beloved to me than its like with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerned with the Hereafter and not concerned with the world, while today the world has inclined us."
"3750","Chapter: affliction befalling the perfect believer as trial. Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla saying: So-and-so, a man among the Companions of the Prophet (may Allah be pleased with them), narrated to us that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying - and he mentioned a hadith; he said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The angels of night and the angels of day gather at the afternoon prayer and the morning prayer; the angels of day ascend at the afternoon prayer, and the angels of night remain among you; the angels of night ascend at the morning prayer, and the angels of day remain among you. And they say: We came to them while they prayed and left them while they prayed; and we left among them a man whom no good or affliction ever befell but he knew it was from You. So He says: Test My servant, or: Increase for My servant - Sufyan said: I do not know with which he began - He said: So they test him, then He says: Test him, and he is tested; then He says: Test him - while He knows best - and they say: The trial has ended, O Lord. So He says: Increase for him, and he is increased; then He says: Increase for him, and he is increased; then He says: Increase for him, and he is increased; then He says: Increase for him - while He knows best - and they say: The increase has ended, O Lord. So He says: How did you leave My servant in trial, and how did you see him in ease? They say: O Lord, the most patient and most grateful servant. So He says: Write My servant among those who are not changed nor altered until he meets Me."
"3751","Chapter: encouragement of patience. Abu Bakr said: Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, which faith is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Patience and generosity. Its isnad is hasan. He narrated it from a long hadith that they narrated separately except this sentence."
"3752","Abu Ya'la said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated, from Abu Raja' Muhriz ibn Abd Allah, from Furat ibn Salman, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No household is patient three days upon hardship but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, brings them provision."
"3753","Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn al-Antaki narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated, from Sadaqah ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Wahb, from Makhul, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People today are like a tree with fruit, and people will soon become like a thorny tree: if you oppose them, they oppose you; if you leave them, they do not leave you; if you flee from them, they seek you. He said: I said: How is the way out, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Lend them from your honor for the day of your poverty."
"3754","Chapter: condemnation of anger. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulaym narrated, Abu Amr the freedman of Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, that he heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever guards his tongue, Allah, Exalted, covers his faults; whoever restrains his anger, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, restrains His punishment from him; whoever apologizes to Allah, Exalted, He accepts his excuse."
"3755","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"3756","And Abu Musa narrated to us, Isa ibn Shu'ayb al-Darir Abu al-Fadl informed us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulaym al-Numayri narrated, from Abu Umayr ibn Anas, from his father, something similar."
"3757","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Hashim al-Bazzar narrated, Khalid ibn Burd al-Ijli narrated to me, from his father, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), with the same."
"3758","Chapter: merit of one who leaves disobedience from fear of Allah, Exalted. Abu Ya'la said: Sulayman ibn Umar ibn Khalid Abu Ayyub al-Raqqi narrated to us, Isa, meaning Ibn Yunus, narrated, from Bishr ibn Numayr, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is able to attain the greed of the world while able not to fulfill it, Allah, Exalted, marries him to the houris wherever he wishes; and whoever is invited by an immoral woman to herself and leaves her from fear of Allah, Blessed and Exalted, Allah, Exalted, marries him to the houris wherever he wishes."
"3759","Chapter: hastening to obedience. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Sufyan narrated, my father narrated to me, Abu Wa'il narrated, from Shurayh: A man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me, before these hadiths became confused, that he said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: O son of Adam, stand toward Me, I walk toward you; walk toward Me, I hasten toward you. Sahih, mawquf."
"3760","Chapter: warning against evil deeds. Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Isma'il ibn Ubayd, that is Ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): Gather your people for me. So he gathered them and they were at the door. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Verily my allies among you are the God-fearing; beware lest people come with deeds while you come with burdens carried upon your backs."
"3761","Al-Tayalisi said: Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, Qatadah narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every man has three friends: as for one friend, he says: What you spent is yours, and what you withheld is not yours - that is his wealth. As for one friend, he says: I am with you, but when you come to the door of the king I leave you and return - that is his family and retinue. As for one friend, he says: I am with you wherever you enter and wherever you exit - that is his deed. So he says: You were the least of the three to me."
"3762","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, Furat ibn Salman narrated, from Abu al-Muhajir, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I heard my beloved Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Just as grapes are not gathered from thorns, the wicked will not attain the stations of the righteous; they are two paths - whichever you take will bring you to its people."
"3763","Abu Ya'la said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, Mukabbir ibn Uthman narrated, al-Wadin ibn Ata' narrated, from Yazid ibn Marthad al-Madhhiji, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, built His religion upon four pillars; whoever is not patient upon them and does not act by them will meet Allah, Exalted, among the corrupt. He said: What are they, O Abu Dharr? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: He submits Allah's lawful to Allah, Allah's unlawful to Allah, Allah's command to Allah, and Allah's prohibition to Allah; none is trusted with them except Allah. He said: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Just as grapes are not gathered from thorns, so the wicked do not attain the stations of the righteous."
"3764","Chapter: frightening about the Day of Resurrection. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Furat narrated, saying: Two men disputed before Muharib ibn Dithar; a man testified against one of them. The one testified against said: By Allah, I know him to be a truthful man; if you asked about him, he would be praised or commended; yet he testified against me falsely, and I do not know what emboldened him to that. Muharib said: O man, fear Allah, for I heard Abd Allah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, while admonishing a man that day - and he mentioned the hadith, saying: And the birds on the Day of Resurrection will beat with their wings and cast out what is in their bellies, having no pursuit."
"3765","Al-Harith said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Furat narrated with the same, abridged, and at its end: And they have no pursuit."
"3766","Chapter: encouragement of deeds. Ishaq said: al-Muqri' informed us, Nuh ibn Ja'wanah al-Khurasani narrated, from Muqatil ibn Hayyan, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and mentioned the hadith, in which: Verily the deed of the Hereafter is grief upon a hill - three times - and the deed of the Fire is ease with desire; and the fortunate is one protected from trials."
"3767","Chapter: the life of the predecessors. Abu Ya'la said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Isa narrated, Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out at midday and found Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) in the mosque, saying: What brought you out at this hour? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What brought you out brought me out, O Messenger of Allah. And Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What brought you out, O son of al-Khattab? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What brought you both out brought me out, O Messenger of Allah. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) sat, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began narrating to them, then said: Have you two strength to go to these palms and obtain food, drink, and shade? He said: We said: Yes. He said: Take us to the house of Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan al-Ansari. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went ahead of us, greeted, and sought permission three times, while Umm al-Haytham (may Allah be pleased with her) was behind the door hearing the speech and wanting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to increase her greeting. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to leave, Umm al-Haytham (may Allah be pleased with her) came out after them, saying: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I heard your greeting, but I wanted you to increase us of your greeting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said good to her, saying: Where is Abu al-Haytham? I do not see him. She said: He is near; he went to fetch us sweet water. Enter, for he will come shortly, if Allah, Exalted, wills. She spread for them a mat under a tree; Abu al-Haytham came, rejoiced at them, his eyes delighted with them; he climbed a palm and cut a cluster. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enough, O Abu al-Haytham. He said: O Messenger of Allah, eat of its ripe dates, its unripe dates, and its dried dates. Then he brought them water and they drank upon it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is of the blessings you will be asked about. Umm al-Haytham (may Allah be pleased with her) stood kneading and baking for them; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar laid their heads for the midday rest; they awoke with their food ready; the food was placed before them, they ate, were satisfied, and praised Allah, Exalted. Umm al-Haytham (may Allah be pleased with her) returned to them the rest of the cluster, and they ate of its ripe and dried dates; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted them and supplicated for them."
"3768","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Abu Khalaf Abd Allah ibn Isa narrated, and he mentioned it in full, except that he said: Until we came to the house of Malik ibn al-Tayyihan Abu al-Haytham al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him); and he added at its end: Then he supplicated good for them, then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu al-Haytham: When it reaches you that captives have come to us, come to us. Abu al-Haytham (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When it reached me that captives were brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I came to Madinah, and he gave me a head; I contracted him for forty thousand dirhams, and I never saw a head of greater blessing than him. Abd Allah ibn Isa said: I narrated it to Isma'il ibn Muslim al-Makki, and he narrated to me something similar, adding in it: Umm al-Haytham said to him: Would you supplicate for us? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May the fasting break fast with you, the righteous eat your food, and the angels bless you."
"3769","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, al-Muharibi narrated, Yahya ibn Ubayd Allah narrated, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: I missed the evening meal one night and came to my family, saying: Have you any evening meal? They said: No, by Allah we have no evening meal. So I lay upon my bed, and sleep did not come to me from hunger. I said: If I went out to the mosque and prayed, passing time until morning. So I went out to the mosque and prayed what Allah willed, then leaned against a side of the mosque. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) appeared, saying: Who is this? I said: Abu Bakr. He said: What brought you out at this hour? So I told him the story. He said: By Allah, nothing brought me out but what brought you out. He sat beside me; while we were thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us, noticed us, and said: Who is this? Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) hastened before me, saying: This is Abu Bakr and Umar. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What brought you two out at this hour? Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I went out, entered the mosque, and saw the figure of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), saying: Who is this? He said: Abu Bakr. I said: What brought you out at this hour? He mentioned what had happened. I said: By Allah, nothing brought me out but what brought you out. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, nothing brought me out but what brought you two out; come with us to al-Waqifi Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan, perhaps we will find with him something to feed us. So we went out walking until we reached the garden in the moonlight; we knocked on the door. The woman said: Who is this? Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr, and Umar. She opened for us and we entered. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is your husband? She said: He went to fetch us sweet water from the well of Banu Harithah; he will come to you now. He came carrying a waterskin until he brought it to a palm and hung it on a stump of its stumps, then turned to us, saying: Welcome and greetings; never has anyone visited people like one who visited me. Then he cut for us a cluster and brought it to us; we began picking from it in the moonlight and eating. Then he took the knife and went among the sheep. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Beware the milking one - or he said: Beware the one with milk. He (may Allah be pleased with him) took a sheep, slaughtered and skinned it, saying to his wife: Rise. She ground, baked, cut meat into the pot, and kindled under it until the bread and meat were done; he crumbled bread, ladled over it of the broth and meat, then brought it to us and placed it before us; we ate until satisfied. Then he went to the waterskin, which the wind had cooled, poured into a vessel, and handed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who drank; then handed to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), who drank; then handed to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), who drank. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Praise be to Allah, Who brought us out - nothing brought us out but hunger - then we returned having obtained this; you will surely be asked about this on the Day of Resurrection; this is of the blessings. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to al-Waqifi: Have you no servant to draw water for you? He said: No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When captives come to us, come to us that we may order a servant for you. It was only a little while until captives came to him; al-Waqifi came to him, saying: What brought you? He said: O Messenger of Allah, your promise which you promised me. He said: These are captives, so stand and choose from them. He said: You choose for me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take this boy and treat him well. He took him and went with him to his wife. She said: What is this? He told her the story. She said: What did you say to him? He said: I said to him: You choose for me. She said: You did well; he said to you: Treat him well, so treat him well. He said: What is treating him well? She said: That you free him. He said: He is free for the sake of Allah, Exalted."
"3770","Musaddad said: Bashir ibn Surayj narrated to us, from Nufay' ibn al-Harith who said: I heard Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) saying: Flour was never sifted for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at all."
"3771","3156/1 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrate to you, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, who said: Hafsah bint Umar said to Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: If only you would wear softer clothes than your clothes, and eat softer food than your food. So he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I dispute with you against yourself; do you not know of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, such-and-such? Until she wept. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I have told you, but I share with them in their harsh living, hoping I may share with them in their pleasant living? So he affirmed it and said: Yes. 3156/2 - Al-Nasa'i transmitted it in al-Sunan al-Kubra, from Suwayd ibn Nasr, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Isma'il, from his brother al-Nu'man. If Mus'ab heard it from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with them both, then it is sahih; otherwise it is mursal with a sahih isnad."
"3772","3157/1 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Yazid ibn Ziyad narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, he is al-Qurazi, who said: One who heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating, narrated to me, he said: I went out on a wintry morning from my house hungry and famished, the cold had pained me, so I took a damaged hide that we had, cut an opening in it, put it over my neck, then tied it over my chest to warm myself with it. By Allah, there was nothing in my house for me to eat, and had there been anything in the house of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) it would have reached me. So I went out to one of the outskirts of Madinah, and I looked over at a Jew in a garden through a gap in its wall. He said: What is wrong with you, O Bedouin? Will you take a date for every bucket? I said: Yes, so open the garden. He opened for me, I entered, and I kept drawing a bucket while he gave me a date, until I filled my palm. I said: Enough from you for now. I ate them, then I lapped water, then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sat with him in the mosque while he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was among a group of his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, when Mus'ab ibn Umayr, may Allah be pleased with him, came upon us wearing a patched cloak of his. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, he remembered the luxury he had been in, and saw the state he was in, so his eyes shed tears and he wept, then he said: How will you be when one of you goes out in the morning in one suit and returns in the evening in another, and your houses are draped as the Ka'bah is draped? We said: On that day we shall be better; our burden will be sufficed and we shall devote ourselves to worship. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You today are better than you on that day."
"3773","3157/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated, the like of it. I say: Al-Tirmidhi transmitted it through Ibn Ishaq with this chain, abridged. 3157/3 - Ahmad transmitted, from the hadith of Mujahid, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, part of the story of the dates."
"3774","3157/4 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Hisham ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Abu Rafi' narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi narrating that the people of Iraq were struck by hardship, so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood among them and said: O people, rejoice, for by Allah I hope that only a short while will pass over you until you see what pleases you of ease and prosperity. I remember I wept three days of my life, finding nothing to eat until I feared hunger would kill me, so I sent Fatimah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask him for food for me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O my daughter, by Allah there is no food in the house for any living creature to eat except what you see - for a small thing before him - but return, and Allah, blessed and exalted, will provide for you. When she came to me and told me, I turned and went until I came to Banu Qurayzah, and there was a Jew at the edge of a well. He said: O Ali, will you water some date-palms of mine and I feed you? I said: Yes, so I struck a bargain with him that for every bucket I draw I get a date. I kept drawing, and each time I drew a bucket he gave me a date, until my hands filled with dates. I sat and ate, then drank from the water, then I said: O belly, you have met good today. Then I drew likewise for the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then I laid it down, and turned back returning, until when I was partway along the road I came upon a dinar lying there. When I saw it I stood looking at it, debating with myself whether to take it or leave it, but I insisted on taking it, and I said: I shall consult the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I took it. When I came to her I told her the news. She said: This is provision from Allah, exalted. Go and buy flour for us. So I went until I came to the market, and there was a Jew of the Jews of Fadak selling barley flour. I bought from him, and when I had measured out he said: What are you to Abu al-Qasim? I said: His cousin, and his daughter is my wife. He gave me the dinar back, so I came to her and told her the news. She said: This is provision from Allah, mighty and majestic. Go with it and pawn it for eight qirats worth of gold in meat. I did so, then I brought it to her; she cut it for her, set it up, kneaded and baked, then we prepared food and sent for the Messenger of Allah, so he came to us. When he saw the food he said: What is this? Did you not come to me just now asking me? We said: Yes indeed; sit, O Messenger of Allah, we shall tell you the news; if you see it as wholesome, eat, and we shall eat. We told him the news, so he said: It is wholesome, so eat in the name of Allah. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose and went out, and there was a Bedouin woman hurrying as though her heart had been torn out. She said: O Messenger of Allah, my father entrusted me with a dinar and it fell from me; by Allah I do not know where it fell, so by my father and mother, see if it is mentioned to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call Ali ibn Abi Talib for me. I came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Go to the butcher and say to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says to you: My qirats are owed to you, so send the dinar. He sent it, so he gave it to the Bedouin woman and she went away."
"3775","3158 - Abd said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu al-Atuf al-Jarrah ibn Minhal informed us, from al-Zuhri, from a man, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until we entered one of the gardens of the Ansar, and we began picking up dates and eating. He said to me: O Ibn Umar, why do you not eat? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not desire it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: But I desire it, and this is the morning of the fourth since I have not tasted food nor found it. Had I wished, I would have called upon my Lord and He would have given me the like of the kingdom of Khosrow and Caesar. How will it be with you, O Ibn Umar, when you remain among a people who store up a year's provision while certainty grows weak? By Allah, we had not moved nor gone away before there was revealed: {And how many a creature does not carry its provision; Allah provides for it and for you, and He is the Hearing, the Knowing}. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, has not commanded me to hoard the world nor to follow desires. Whoever hoards it seeking thereby an enduring life, then life is in the hand of Allah, mighty and majestic. Indeed, I do not hoard a dinar nor a dirham, nor do I store up provision for tomorrow."
"3776","3159 - Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, al-Mustalim ibn Sa'id narrated, from Sulayman ibn Muhammad, from Abu Hazim, who said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, prepared food for Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and kept lifting dish after dish. He said: So she, may Allah be pleased with her, turned her face to the wall weeping. Urwah, may Allah be pleased with him, said to her: You have spoiled it for us. She said: By the One who sent him with the truth, he never saw sieves since Allah, blessed and exalted, sent him until he passed away."
"3777","3160 - Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar narrated, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slept on a bed stuffed with fiber and a pillow stuffed with fiber. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose and it had marked his skin, so I wept. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umm Salamah, what makes you weep? I said: What I see of the mark of this. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not weep, for by Allah, had I wished, the mountains would walk with me. And the hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, about the story of the bowl in the house of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, is in her virtues."
"3778","3161 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, who said: When death came to him, he said: I die upon a matter which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not lay down as practice, nor Abu Bakr, nor Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Yet I have left neither gold nor silver except what is in this sword of mine, so sell it and shroud me with it."
"3779","3162 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from al-Hasan, who said: Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Arabs used to say: Whoever eats bread becomes stout. When we conquered Khaybar we drove them off from some bread of theirs, so I sat on it and ate until I was full, then I kept looking at my sides to see if I had become stout."
"3780","3163 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad, he is al-Muqaddami, narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated, Fa'id, freedman of Abadil, narrated to me, Ubaydullah ibn Ali narrated to me, he said: His grandmother Salma informed him, she said: Al-Hasan ibn Ali, and Abdullah ibn Abbas, and Abdullah ibn Ja'far, may Allah be pleased with them, came to me and said: Prepare for us food from what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to like to eat. I said to al-Hasan: O my son, we do not desire it today. Then she took barley, ground it, winnowed it, made a loaf from it, made its condiment oil, sprinkled pepper over it, and brought it near to them, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to like this and eat it well. Al-Tirmidhi transmitted it in al-Shama'il."
"3781","3164 - Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Mahdi narrated, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: A nephew of ours got married and prepared food, so Ibn Sirin said: A man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go days without eating; when he found a skin he sufficed himself with it, and if he did not find, he tied a stone over his belly."
"3782","3165 - Al-Harith said: Ya'qub ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated, Abu Salih, freedman of Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, narrated, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent us on the expedition of Nakhlah, and with us was Amr ibn Suraqah, a man slender in the belly and tall. He grew hungry and his back bent, so he could not walk, and he fell upon us. We took a flat stone and tied it upon his belly, then fastened it to his back, so he walked with us. We came to a clan of the Arabs and they hosted us, and he walked with us. Then he said: I used to think the two legs carry the belly, but now the belly carries the two legs."
"3783","19 - Chapter on the rebuke of boasting in food and clothing. 3166/1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Ifriqi narrated, Umarah ibn Rashid, of the people of Damascus, narrated, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The worst of my nation are those nourished on luxury, whose bodies grow upon it."
"3784","3166/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated, Abd al-Rahman narrated, Umarah narrated with it."
"3785","3166/3 - Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yazid, he is Abu Abd al-Rahman, he is al-Muqri, narrated with it. And he said: Umarah is alone in it, and Abd al-Rahman narrated rejected reports from unknown narrators."
"3786","20 - Chapter on wariness of the trial of wealth and abundance of property. 3167/1 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith informed us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated, Humayd ibn Hilal al-Adawi narrated, Zuhayr ibn Hayyan al-Adawi narrated, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, called me, and before him was a leather mat with gold scattered upon it like the scattering of chaff. Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: And chaff is straw. He said: Come and divide among your people. Allah knows best whether He withheld this from His Prophet and from Abu Bakr seeking good or evil. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, began to weep, saying in his weeping: By the One in whose hand is my soul, He did not withhold it from His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, intending evil for them, and give it to me intending good for me. This is a hasan hadith; al-Haytham ibn Kulayb al-Shashi transmitted it in his Musnad."
"3787","3167/2 - Ibn al-Munadi narrated to us, Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated, from Humayd ibn Hilal, Zuhayr ibn Hayyan narrated, he used to attend Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and heard from him, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, called me, and he mentioned it. Its men are those from whom Muslim transmitted, except Zuhayr, and he is not disparaged."
"3788","3168/1 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-Mughirah al-Dabbi, from a man of Banu Amir, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I am more fearful for you of the trial of prosperity than I am of the trial of adversity. You were tested with the trial of adversity and were patient, and the world is green and sweet."
"3789","3168/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir narrated with it."
"3790","3169 - Abu Ya'la said: Bishr ibn Sayhan narrated to us, Harb ibn Maymun narrated, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, and he brought out to him heaps of dates. He said: What is this, O Bilal? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Dates I stored up, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you not fear to hear for it a sizzling in Hell? Spend, O Bilal, and do not fear from the Lord of the Throne any lessening."
"3791","3170 - Abu Hammam narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Maslamah ibn Ali informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has little wealth, many dependents, good prayer, and does not backbite the Muslims, will come on the Day of Resurrection with me like these two."
"3792","3171 - Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from a man of Banu Abs, who said: I was with Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned a story. He said: Then he mentioned the treasures of Khosrow. He said: The One who gave you this, entrusted it to you, and opened it for you, holds back His treasuries, while Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alive; they would wake in the morning with neither dinar nor dirham, nor a mudd of food. What then is this, O brother of Banu Abs? Then we passed threshing floors being winnowed, and he said: The One who gave you this, entrusted it to you, and opened it for you, holds back His treasuries, while Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alive; they would wake in the morning with not a mudd of food. What then is this, O brother of Banu Abs?"
"3793","21 - Chapter on the excellence of taking little of the world and praise of the ascetics among them. 3172 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit al-Jumahi, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, called a man of Banu Jumah called Sa'id ibn Amir ibn Hidhayam, and said to him: I am appointing you over such-and-such land. He said: Will you excuse me, O Commander of the Faithful? He said: By Allah, I shall not; you put it on my neck then leave me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Shall we not assign you a stipend? He said: You have already put in my stipend what suffices me without it, and surplus over what I want. He said: When his stipend came out he would buy his family's sustenance and give the rest in charity. His wife would say to him: Where is the surplus of your stipend? He would say: I have lent it. Some people came to him and said: Your family has a right over you, and your in-laws have a right over you. He said: I shall neither favor others over them nor seek anyone's pleasure in seeking the wide-eyed maidens of Paradise; if an excellent one of the excellences of Paradise were to look out, the earth would shine for her as the sun shines. Nor shall I lag behind the first emancipation after I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: People will be gathered for reckoning, then the poor believers will come pressing forward as doves press forward. It will be said to them: Stand at the reckoning. They will say: We have no reckoning, nor did you give us. Their Lord, majestic and exalted, will say to them: My servants have spoken truly. A gate of Paradise will be opened for them and they will enter it seventy years before people."
"3794","3173 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Shadhakuni narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Aban narrated, Ali ibn al-Hazawwar narrated, he said: I heard Abu Maryam; I heard Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The righteous have not adorned themselves in the world with the like of asceticism in it."
"3795","3174 - Isma'il ibn Sayf narrated to us, Umar ibn Harun al-Balkhi narrated, from Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn Ja'far, from his father, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see one who is ascetic in the world, draw near to him, for wisdom is cast to him."
"3796","3175 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Wahhab, meaning Ibn Ata, narrated to us, from Awf, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like the first hadith before it: Richness is not from abundance of goods; richness is only richness of the soul."
"3797","3176 - Bishr ibn Abi Bishr narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, Hayyan ibn al-Basri narrated, from Ishaq ibn Nuh, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), turning to Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: O Usamah, beware of every hungry liver that will dispute against you before Allah, exalted, on the Day of Resurrection. Beware of the supplication of servants who have melted flesh, burned skins with winds and scorching heat, and thirsted livers until their eyes were covered. If you wish, look at them and the angels rejoice at them; through them earthquakes and tribulations are turned away. Then he wept until his sobbing intensified, then he said: Woe to this nation for what one who obeyed his Lord meets from them; how they kill him and belie him because he obeyed Allah, blessed and exalted? Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Messenger of Allah, will people on that day be upon Islam? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. He said: Over what will they then fight one another? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umar, the people have left the path and mounted beasts, worn the softest clothes, and the sons of Persia serve them, adorning themselves for them as a woman adorns herself for her husband. When the allies of Allah, exalted, speak, cloaks are upon them, their backs bent, having slaughtered themselves with thirst. When one of them speaks he is belied, and it is said to him: You are the companion of Satan and the head of misguidance; you forbid the adornment of Allah, exalted, and the good things of provision. They recite the Book of Allah, exalted, upon other than religion. They humiliated the allies of Allah. Know, O Usamah, that the nearest of people to Allah, exalted, on the Day of Resurrection are those whose grief, thirst, and hunger were longest in the world: the elect, the righteous, who when they attend are not brought near, and when they are absent are not missed. The expanses of the earth know them; they are known among the people of heaven and hidden from the people of earth, and the angels surround them. People live in luxury while they live in luxury through hunger and thirst. People wore soft clothes while they wore the coarsest clothes. People spread bedding while they spread foreheads and knees. People laughed while they wept. O Usamah, Allah, mighty and majestic, does not combine hardship upon them in the world and the Hereafter; for them is Paradise. Would that I had seen them, O Usamah? For them is glad tidings in the Hereafter, the earth is merciful to them, and the Almighty is pleased with them. People neglected the deeds and character of the Prophets while they preserved them. The desirous is one who desires toward Allah, exalted, like their desire, and the loser is one who opposes them. The earth weeps when it loses them, and Allah, exalted, is angry with every town in which there is none like them. O Usamah, when you see them in a village, know they are a security for that village; Allah, exalted, does not punish a people among whom they are. Take them for yourself, hoping you may be saved through them. Beware of leaving what they are upon, lest your foot slip and you fall into the Fire. They were denied the lawful that Allah made lawful for them, seeking bounty from the Hereafter, abandoning food and drink though able, not clinging to the world as dogs cling to carrion. People busied themselves with the world while they busied themselves with obedience to Allah, blessed and exalted. They wore rags and ate hard dry bread. You see them disheveled and dusty; people think they have a disease, but they do not; people think their minds are gone, but they are not gone. Rather they looked with their hearts at what took their minds away from the world, so in the world, among worldly people, they walk as though mindless. O Usamah, they understood when the minds of people departed; for them is glad tidings in the Hereafter."
"3798","3177 - Musaddad said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Those before you were destroyed only by this dinar and this dirham, and they will destroy you. Sahih, halted."
"3799","22 - Chapter on the excellence of provision in one's homeland. 3178 - Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Ishaq ibn Abi Ya'qub al-Madani narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn al-Hasan, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Four are of a man's happiness: that his wife be agreeable, his children righteous, his brothers upright, and his provision be in his own land."
"3800","23 - Chapter on the exposure of a servant's deed even if he hides it. 3179 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Awf, Ma'bad al-Juhani narrated, from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: If a man entered a house within a house and persisted there in a deed, people would soon speak of it. There is no worker who does a deed except Allah, exalted, clothes him in the garment of his deed; if good, then good, and if evil, then evil."
"3801","3180 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Maslamah ibn Mukhallad: To proceed, when a servant acts in obedience to Allah, mighty and majestic, Allah, blessed and exalted, loves him, and when He loves him He makes him beloved to His creation. And when a servant acts in disobedience to Allah, exalted, Allah, mighty and majestic, hates him, and when Allah, exalted, hates him He makes him hateful to creation."
"3802","24 - Chapter on the permissibility of securing sustenance along with righteous deed. 3181 - Al-Harith said: Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Saffar narrated, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Ayzar, who said: I met an old Bedouin shaykh at al-Raml and said to him: Did you meet anyone of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. I said: Who? He said: Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both. I said to him: What did you hear him say? He said: I heard him, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Secure for your world as though you will live forever, and act for your Hereafter as though you will die tomorrow."
"3803","25 - Chapter on encouragement to ease in worldly matters. 3182 - Abu Ya'la said: Mus'ab ibn Abdullah al-Zubayri narrated to us, my father narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not tell you upon whom the Fire will be forbidden tomorrow? Upon every gentle, mild, near, easy one."
"3804","3183 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham narrated to us, Hafs narrated, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever."
"3805","3184 - Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umarah narrated, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'mar, from a man of Banu Fahm, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had the world weighed with Allah, blessed and exalted, the wing of a gnat, He would not have given a disbeliever a drink of water from it."
"3806","3185 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Amr, he is Ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, who said: Some Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Rejoice, O Abu Abdullah, you will come to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the Pond. He said: But what of this? And he pointed to the top of the house and its bottom. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What suffices one of you of the world is like the provision of a rider."
"3807","3186 - Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to us, Abu Sa'id, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated, from Sadaqah ibn al-Rabi', from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'id, I think from his father - the narrator doubted - who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while upon the pulpit, saying: Little that suffices is better than much that distracts."
"3808","3187 - Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn al-Antaki narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad al-A'war narrated, from Uthman ibn Ata, from his father, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were a servant to flee from his provision as he flees from death, his provision would come to him as death comes to him."
"3809","3188/1 - Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The world is but like a puddle whose clear part is gone and whose turbid part remains."
"3810","3188/2 - Khalid narrated to us, Yazid narrated, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The clear part of the world is gone and nothing of it remains but murkiness, and death today is a gift for every Muslim."
"3811","3188/3 - Al-Harith said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Juhayfah, with it, but he did not mention Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3812","26 - Chapter on the excellence of mixing with people and patience over their harm. 3189 - Musaddad said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Fear Allah, mighty and majestic, and fear people."
"3813","3190/1 - Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated, from Yahya ibn Waththab, from Abu Salih, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The believer who mixes with people and is patient over their harm is greater in reward than the believer who does not mix with people nor is patient over their harm."
"3814","3190/2 - Al-Harith said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated, from al-A'mash, with it."
"3815","3191 - Abu Ya'la said: Rawh ibn Hatim narrated to us, Hushaym narrated, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Accustom yourselves to patience, for tribulation is about to descend upon you, just as what befell us when we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will not befall you."
"3816","3192 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Mansur, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Sa'sa'ah ibn Suhan said to Ibn Zayd, meaning Ibn Suhan: I was dearer to your father than you, and you are dearer to me than my son. Two traits I enjoin upon you: be sincere to the believer, and deal with the wicked with good character, for the wicked is pleased with you for good character, and it is a duty upon us to be sincere to the believer."
"3817","27 - Chapter on seeking blessing through relics of the righteous. 3193 - Abu Ya'la said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abd al-A'la narrated, from al-Walid ibn Ali, from Muhammad ibn Suqah, from his father, who said: I came to Amr ibn Hurayth to rent from him a house in his residence. He said: Rent, for it is blessed for its owner, blessed for whoever dwells in it. I said: From what is that? He said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a camel had been slaughtered and he had ordered its distribution. He said to the one distributing it: Give Amr a share of it. He did not give me and overlooked me. The next day I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with dirhams before him. He said: Did you take the share I ordered for you? I said: O Messenger of Allah, he gave me nothing. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took from the dirhams and gave me. I brought them to my mother and said: Take these dirhams which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took with his hand then gave me; keep them until we see in what to put them. Then time struck its blows until I bought this house. My mother said: When you want to pay its price, do not pay until you call me to supplicate blessing for you. I called her when I had prepared it, and she said to me: Take these dirhams and mix them into it. I mixed them, then mingled them, and she said: Go with them."
"3818","3194 - Abu Ya'la said: Surayj ibn Yunus Abu al-Harith narrated to us, Hushaym narrated, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from his father, who said: Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We performed umrah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in an umrah he performed. He shaved his hair and people raced to his hair. I raced to the forelock and took it, made a cap, and put it in the front of his cap, and he never faced it toward any front but it was opened for him."
"3819","3195 - Hashim ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I asked Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her, for some of the perfume she kneaded with the sweat of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She gave me some of it, and when Muhammad died he was embalmed with that perfume."
"3820","28 - Chapter on the excellence of constancy in deed. 3196 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Suwayd narrated, Abu Fakhatah al-Taymi of Kufah narrated to me, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my nephew has striven in worship and exhausted himself. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the zeal of Islam; for every zeal there is a lull, so watch him at his lull: if he draws near, then perhaps, and if he perishes, then woe to him."
"3821","29 - Chapter on mention of the Abdal. 3197/1 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Safwan ibn Abdullah, or Abdullah ibn Safwan, who said: A man said on the day of Siffin: O Allah, curse the people of Syria. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do not curse the people of Syria en masse, for among them are the Abdal - he said it three times. Ahmad transmitted it in the Musnad of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, raised."
"3822","3197/2 - al-Nadr informed us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from Ibn Shihab, from Safwan ibn Abdullah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. 3197/3 - al-Dhuhli transmitted it in the defects of al-Zuhri's hadith, from Muhammad ibn Kathir, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Safwan ibn Abdullah ibn Safwan, with it. It has a witness from the hadith of Ibn Zurayr al-Ghafiqi, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, halted also; Ibn Yunus transmitted it in the history of Egypt."
"3823","30 - Chapter on the blessing of obedient people. 3198/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Khuthaym ibn Irak narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Gently with Allah, exalted, gently. Were it not for humble youths, bowing elders, suckling children, and pasturing beasts, punishment would have been poured upon you in torrents."
"3824","3198/2 - Al-Bazzar said: al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musa narrated, Ibrahim ibn Khuthaym narrated with it. And he said: We know it only with this isnad."
"3825","31 - Chapter on what honors a righteous man receives. 3199 - al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Suqah narrated, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, who said: Allah, exalted, preserves the righteous man in his child, his grandchild, the small house in which he was born, and the small houses around him; they remain in preservation. Sufyan said: And I see in it: and covering."
"3826","32 - Chapter on what is reported about storytellers and preachers. 3200 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Mutarrif, from al-Qasim ibn Kathir, from one of his companions, who said: Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, used to tell stories. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: None tells stories but a commander, or one commanded, or an arrogant pretender. He said: It was said to Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him: May your mother be bereaved of you, this is Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, saying such-and-such, so he left storytelling. Then Mu'awiyah ordered him to tell stories, so he deemed it lawful thereby."
"3827","3201 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, who said: I heard my father narrating that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by a storyteller and said: What does he say? They said: He tells stories. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: No, rather he says: Know me."
"3828","3202 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah narrated, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivered a sermon to us and exhorted us in it with an exhortation from which eyes shed tears, hearts feared, skins shivered, and entrails shook. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, who called: Prayer in congregation. He mentioned the hadith at length, and in it: Whoever prefers the world over the Hereafter, for him is the Fire. Whoever magnifies a worldly man and praises him coveting his world, Allah, exalted, is angry with him, and he will be in the rank of Qarun in the lowest Hell. Whoever builds a building for show and repute will be laden on the Day of Resurrection with seven earths, collared with fire kindled on his neck, then cast with it into the Fire. It was said: How does one build a building for show and repute? He said: He builds beyond what suffices him and builds it in rivalry. Whoever learns the Qur'an and does not act by it, preferring over it the debris of the world and its adornment, incurs the wrath of Allah, exalted, and will be in the rank of the Jews and Christians who cast the Book of Allah, exalted, behind their backs and bought with it a small price. Whoever is discontent with his provision and spreads his complaint, no good deed of his is raised to Allah, exalted, and he meets Allah, mighty and majestic, while He is angry with him. Whoever marries a lawful woman with lawful wealth seeking thereby pride and show, Allah, exalted, increases him thereby only in disgrace and humiliation, and Allah, exalted, will station him, for what he enjoyed of her, upon the edge of Hell, then he will fall into it for seventy autumns. Whoever humiliates a poor Muslim for his poverty and belittles him has belittled the right of Allah, exalted, and remains in Allah's, exalted, loathing and anger until he pleases him. Whoever honors a poor Muslim will meet Allah, exalted, on the Day of Resurrection while He smiles at him. Whoever is offered the world and the Hereafter and chooses the world over the Hereafter will meet Allah, exalted, with no good deed to shield himself with from the Fire. If he chooses the Hereafter over the world he will meet Allah, exalted, while He is pleased with him. Whoever's eyes shed tears from fear of Allah, blessed and exalted, will have for every drop of his tears the like of Uhud in his scale, and for every teardrop in Paradise, upon its edge, cities and palaces such as no eye has seen, no ear heard, nor occurred to a describer's heart."
"3829","3203 - Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: Storytelling is an innovation."
"3830","3204 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: When Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, fell ill with the illness in which he died, Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, came to visit him while he was governor of Kufah. Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, began to weep, and he mentioned the hadith, then he said: As for you, O man, fear Allah, exalted, at your concern when you intend, at your hand when you distribute, and at your tongue when you judge."
"3831","3205 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated, Sufyan narrated, from al-Ajlah, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the Children of Israel perished, they told stories."
"3832","3206 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from one of his companions, from Yazid al-Raqashi, who said: People disputed about storytelling; some deemed it good and some disliked it. They came to Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned that to him, and asked him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent with fighting and was not sent with storytelling."
"3833","33 - Chapter on dislike of adorning houses with drapes and excess in adornment. 3207 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Abu Ja'far al-Khatmi narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, who said: Abdullah ibn Yazid was invited to food. When he came he saw the house draped, so he sat outside weeping. It was said to him: What makes you weep? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sent off an army and reached the Pass of Farewell, would say (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I entrust to Allah your religion, your trusts, and the seals of your deeds. One day he saw a man who had patched his cloak with a piece of fur. He said: He faced the rising sun and said thus with his hand - and Hammad described with his hands the insides of the palms, stretching his hands: The world has loomed over you - meaning it has come - until we thought it would fall upon us, and one of you will go out in the morning in one suit and return in the evening in another, and you will drape your houses as you drape the Ka'bah. Abdullah ibn Yazid said: Should I not weep when I see you draping your houses as you drape the Ka'bah. I say: Abu Dawud and al-Nasa'i transmitted the story of the words at farewell only, and their isnad is hasan."
"3834","35 - Chapter on censure of miserliness. 3208 - Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Ali ibn Abi Sarah narrated, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing has obliterated Islam like miserliness obliterates it."
"3835","3209 - Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Husayn narrated, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, who said: When it is the middle of the night an angel looks down and says: Glorify the Holy King. Then another angel looks down and says: Glorify the Holy King. At that the birds move their wings. Then another angel looks down and says: O seeker of good, come forth. Then another angel looks down and says: O seeker of evil, desist. Then another looks down and says: O Allah, give a spender a replacement. Then another looks down and says: O Allah, give a withholder ruin."
"3836","3210 - Abu Bakr said: Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated, from Abu Abdullah al-Asadi, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave what puts doubt in you for what does not put doubt in you."
"3837","36 - Chapter on the excellence of one who loves meeting Allah, exalted. 3211 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, while at a funeral - that was the first day I knew him - I heard him saying: So-and-so, a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever loves meeting Allah, mighty and majestic, Allah, exalted, loves meeting him; and whoever hates meeting Allah, exalted, Allah, mighty and majestic, hates meeting him. The people wept and said: O Messenger of Allah, which of us does not hate death? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not mean that, but Allah, blessed and exalted, said: {But if he is of those brought near, then rest and bounty and a garden of bliss}. When he is at that, he loves meeting Allah, exalted, and Allah, mighty and majestic, loves meeting him even more. {And if he is of the deniers, the astray, then a lodging of scalding water}. When that is so, he hates meeting Allah, exalted, and Allah, mighty and majestic, hates meeting him even more."
"3838","37 - Chapter on warning against show and supplication removing it. 3212/1 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from one who narrated to him, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, said, testifying it upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned association and said: It is more hidden among you than the creeping of ants. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Messenger of Allah, is association anything but to set up another god with Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May your mother be bereaved of you, O Abu Bakr; association is more hidden among you than the creeping of ants. I shall point you to something which, when you do it, the minor and major of association - or the small and great of association - departs from you. Say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from associating with You while I know, and I seek Your forgiveness for what I do not know. I say: Layth is weak for his poor memory and confusion, and his shaykh is unnamed."
"3839","3212/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, he is Ibn Abi Isra'il, narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, on His, exalted, saying: {Or have they set up for Allah partners who created like His creation}, Layth ibn Abi Sulaym informed me, from Abu Muhammad, from Hudhayfah, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Either Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, attended that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned it."
"3840","3212/3 - Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abu Muhammad, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, who said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, or Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Association is more hidden among you than the creeping of ants. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not point you to what removes from you its small and great? Say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from associating with You while I know, and I seek Your forgiveness for what I do not know."
"3841","3212/4 - Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Rawh ibn Aslam narrated, and Fahd, both said: Abd al-Aziz narrated with it."
"3842","3213 - Ishaq said: Abu Mu'awiyah informed us, Ibrahim ibn Muslim al-Hajari narrated, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever beautifies his prayer where people see him and mars it when alone, that is but contempt with which he contemns his Lord. This is a hasan hadith."
"3843","3214 - al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, al-Ju'ayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, he said: We were with al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid when al-Zubayr ibn Sahl ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with them, came to him with a mark of prostration on his face. He said: Who is this? We said: al-Zubayr ibn Sahl. He said: By Allah, this is not the mark Allah, mighty and majestic, named. I have prostrated on my face for eighty years and prostration has not marked between my eyes. This is a sahih halted isnad."
"3844","3215 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid, he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, al-Faraj ibn Fadalah informed us, from Abu al-Hasan, from Jabalah al-Yahsabi, who said: We were with a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of what he narrated to us he said: A speaker among the Muslims said: O Messenger of Allah, what is salvation tomorrow? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not deceive Allah, exalted. He said: How is Allah, mighty and majestic, deceived? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: To act by what Allah commanded you, seeking thereby other than Him. So beware of show, for it is association with Allah, mighty and majestic. The show-off will be called on the Day of Resurrection before all creatures by four names: O wicked, O disbeliever, O loser, O treacherous; your deed is astray and your reward void; you have no share today with Allah, exalted, so seek your reward from the one for whom you used to work, O deceiver. He said: I said to him: By Allah besides whom there is no god, did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, I heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), unless it be something I did not intend. Yazid said: And I think he recited verses of the Qur'an: {So whoever hopes for the meeting with his Lord, let him do righteous deed}, the verse, and: {The hypocrites deceive Allah while He deceives them}, the verse."
"3845","3216 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated, from al-Rabi' ibn Subayh, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The son of Adam will be brought on the Day of Resurrection as though a young camel - or perhaps he said: as though a camel - and Allah, mighty and majestic, will say: O son of Adam, I am the best apportioner; look at your deed you did for Me and I shall reward you for it, and look at your deed you did for other than Me, and let him reward you for what you did for him."
"3846","38 - Chapter on warning against belittled deeds. 3217/1 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us. 3217/2 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, Zayd narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, Satan has despaired of being worshiped in this land of yours at the end of time, but he is content with belittled deeds from you, so beware of him for your religion, the hadith."
"3847","3218/1 - al-Tayalisi said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Sa'd, from Abadah al-Nahdi, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, exalted, has not forbidden any sanctity but He knew that some intruder among you would violate it. Indeed, I hold your waist-wrappers lest you plunge into the Fire as flies plunge."
"3848","3218/2 - Abu Bakr said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from al-Mas'udi, with it, except he said: Taking hold of your waist-wrappers, and added: As moths, flies, and gnats plunge."
"3849","3218/3 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Yazid narrated with it."
"3850","39 - Chapter on rebuke of amassing the world. 3219 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Hasan ibn Musa narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, Huyayy ibn Abdullah narrated to me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, put me over something I may live by. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Hamzah, is a soul you give life to dearer to you or a soul you kill? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A soul I give life to. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stick to yourself."
"3851","40 - Chapter on the rest of warning against show. 3220 - Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Husayn ibn Muhammad ibn Arabi narrated, from a man I know only as Sa'id al-Azraq, from Muhammad ibn Wasi', who said: Uways, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a man praying, standing and sitting, and said: What is wrong with you? He said: I stand and Satan comes saying: You show off, so I sit. Then my soul contends with me toward prayer and I stand. Then he says: You show off, so I sit. He said: Had you been alone, would you pray this prayer? He said: Yes. He said: Pray, for you are no show-off."
"3852","3221 - Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated, from Mujahid, who said: A man came to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said to him: How are you and al-Dahhak ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: When we meet him we say to him what he likes, and when we turn from him we say to him otherwise. He said: That is what we, while with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to count as hypocrisy."
"3853","3222 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated, Abdullah ibn Budayl ibn Warqa narrated to me, he said: We came to al-Zuhri and he ordered us expelled. Then he sent for us and we came, and he narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Tamim narrated to us, from his uncle, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: O heralds of the Arabs, thrice: The most fearful thing I fear for you is show and hidden lust."
"3854","3223/1 - Al-Harith said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Hiql, he is Ibn Ziyad, narrated, from al-Awza'i, al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Urwah, who said: I said to Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, we enter upon the ruler who judges with a judgment we see as injustice, and we say: May Allah, exalted, grant you success, and we look at one of us praising him. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for we, the company of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), no. 3223/2 - Al-Bukhari transmitted it through Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Abdullah ibn Umar, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, abridged."
"3855","41 - Chapter on the excellence of hunger. 3224 - Abu Ya'la said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus, he is Ibn Bukayr, narrated, al-Hajjaj ibn Abi Zaynab narrated, from Talhah, freedman of Ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died while his belly was empty."
"3856","42 - Chapter on the excellence of the content poor. The hadith of Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, has preceded in the chapters of remembrance that removes doors of illness, in the chapters of medicine. 3225 - Al-Harith said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among my nation are those who, were one to stand at the door of one of you and ask him for a dinar he would not give him, or a dirham he would not give him, or a fils he would not give him. Were he to ask Allah, exalted, for the world He would not give him, and He withholds only for his honor with Him. Were he to ask Allah, exalted, for Paradise He would give him, and were he to swear upon Allah He would fulfill it."
"3857","43 - Chapter on censure of arrogance. 3226 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Sinan narrated, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated, from Muhammad ibn al-Qasim, who said: Abdullah ibn Hanzalah claimed that Abdullah ibn Salam passed in the market carrying a bundle of firewood. It was said to him: Has Allah, exalted, not sufficed you from this? He said: Yes indeed, but I wanted to crush arrogance. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: None shall enter Paradise in whose heart is a mustard seed's weight of arrogance."
"3858","3227 - Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed on a road and a black woman passed. A man said to her: The road. She said: What of the road? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave her, for she is overbearing."
"3859","3228 - Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us, Yazid, he is Ibn Harun, narrated, al-Azhar ibn Sinan informed us, Muhammad ibn Wasi' narrated, he said: I entered upon Bilal ibn Abi Burdah and said to him: O Bilal, your father narrated to me, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: In Hell is a valley called Habhab; it is binding upon Allah, blessed and exalted, to lodge in it every tyrant. Beware, O Bilal, of being among those who dwell in it."
"3860","3229/1 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us. 3229/2 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, both said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man attains the rank of the fasting, standing one through good character, and he is recorded as a tyrant though he owns nothing but his household."
"3861","3230 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated, Abbad ibn Abbad ibn Alqamah narrated, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: The companions of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, were bitten by cold, and they were shy to come in cloaks and woolen garments, so he missed them. It was said to him: Their affair is such-and-such. Abu Abd al-Rahman woke in a cloak. They said: Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, has woken in a cloak. Then the second day came, then the third day came. When they saw him in the cloak they came in their garments, and he recognized faces he had missed. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: None shall enter Paradise in whose heart is a mustard seed's weight of arrogance - or he said: an atom's weight of arrogance."
"3862","44 - Chapter on silence. 3231 - Abu Dawud said: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Makhul, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the hadith to Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him: As long as you are silent you are safe; when you speak, it is for you or against you."
"3863","3232 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam Abu al-Ash'ath narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated, Uthman ibn Sa'd narrated, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Silence is wisdom, and few practice it."
"3864","3233 - Harun ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated, from Umar ibn Hafs, from Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased to be safe, let him keep to silence."
"3865","3234 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Abdullah said: O my son, let your house contain you, weep over your sin, and restrain your tongue. And the hadith of Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, on that is at the beginning of the hadiths of the Prophets, upon them prayer and peace."
"3866","3235/1 - Abu Ya'la, Baqi ibn Makhlad, and al-Baghawi all said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated, from Abdullah ibn Nusayb, from Muslim ibn Abdullah ibn Sabrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I forbid you three: hearsay, wasting wealth, and excessive asking. 3235/2 - Al-Bukhari said: Qays ibn Hafs narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated with it; he mentioned it in al-Tarikh. Ibn al-Sakan said: I know no other for Abdullah ibn Sabrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3867","3236/1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Hanafi narrated to us, from al-Hajari, from Abu Iyad, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man speaks a word to make those around him laugh, yet it has come greater than Ukaz, and he does not perceive."
"3868","3236/2 - Husayn al-Ju'fi narrated to us, Za'idah narrated, from al-Hajari, with it."
"3869","3237 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Shurahbil narrated, Layth ibn Sa'd narrated, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn Abbas, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), addressing people, saying: Whoever guards what is between his jaws and guards what is between his legs, he is in Paradise."
"3870","3238 - Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Harb narrated, Ibrahim ibn Ishaq narrated, Iqal ibn Shabbah narrated, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Guard what is between your jaws and your legs. He said: I rose saying: Enough for me."
"3871","45 - Chapter on preferring others. 3239 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, from Mis'ar ibn Kidam, Thabit ibn Ubayd narrated, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Mughaffal al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has two shirts, let him clothe one or give one in charity."
"3872","46 - Chapter on short hope. 3240 - Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, who said: A man used to sit with Masruq, and was among the last to bid him farewell. He said: O Abu A'ishah, you are the chief of reciters and their master; your adornment is adornment for them, and your disgrace is disgrace for them. Do not entertain yourself with poverty nor long life."
"3873","47 - Chapter on safety in seclusion. 3241 - Musaddad said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated, from Makhul, who said: If there is excellence in congregation, safety is in seclusion."
"3874","48 - Chapter on grief. 3242/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Nashit narrated to us, Abu al-Mughirah narrated, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Damrah ibn Habib, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah, mighty and majestic, loves every grieving heart."
"3875","3242/2 - Al-Bazzar said: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated, from Damrah, with it. And he said: We know it only with this isnad. Al-Hakim declared it sahih."
"3876","49 - Chapter on the excellence of sharp temper. 3243/1 - Abu Bakr said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Layth ibn Sa'd narrated, from Duwayd ibn Nafi', from Abu Mansur al-Farisi, in whom was sharp temper and it was mentioned to him, so he said: I would not like it to miss me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sharp temper befalls the best of my nation."
"3877","3243/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' al-Ataki narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Aban narrated, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, with it."
"3878","3244 - Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Sallam al-Tawil narrated, from al-Fadl ibn Atiyyah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sharp temper befalls the best of my nation."
"3879","50 - Chapter on seeking affection. 3245/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Uthman narrated to us, Waki' narrated, my father narrated, from a shaykh called Tariq, from Amr ibn Malik al-Ruwasi, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, be pleased with me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from me thrice. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, the Lord, blessed and exalted, is entreated and He is pleased. He said: So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was pleased with me."
"3880","3245/2 - Al-Bazzar said: Ibrahim ibn Ziyad al-Sa'igh narrated to us, Waki' narrated with this. And he said: We know for him only this hadith, meaning Amr ibn Malik."
"3881","51 - Chapter on the best of companions. 3246/1 - Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Mubarak ibn Hassan narrated, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, which of our companions is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One whose seeing reminds you of Allah, whose speech increases you in knowledge, and whose deed reminds you of the Hereafter."
"3882","3246/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Ali ibn Hashim ibn al-Barid narrated, from Mubarak ibn Hassan, with it."
"3883","52 - Chapter on the excellence of dwelling near graves. 3247 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib narrate to you, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: It was said to Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: Why have you left neighboring the grave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and neighbored the graves, meaning al-Baqi'? He said: I found them truthful neighbors; they expiate evil and remind of the Hereafter? Abu Usamah affirmed it and said: Yes."
"3884","53 - Chapter on the excellence of abandoning indecencies. 3248 - Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated, from Yusuf ibn Maymun, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased to outstrip the untiring striver, let him refrain from sins."
"3885","3249 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hisham, from Hafsah, from al-Rabi' ibn Ziyad, from Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Praise does not settle for a servant on earth until it settles in heaven."
"3886","55 - Chapter on the excellence of weeping from fear of Allah, exalted. 3250 - Abd said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Zuhri narrated to us, my father, he is Ibn Sa'd, narrated, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant weeps and his eyes shed tears from fear of Allah, exalted, then Allah, mighty and majestic, admits him to the Fire ever, until the rain of the sky returns. And it is said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon the pulpit when people returned from Mu'tah with a piece of bread in his hand. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned their affair his eyes overflowed, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped his face and said: Indeed I am but a man; I seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan. A man sees himself as many through his brother. Who has a promise with me? Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I, O Messenger of Allah, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave it to him. He said: And Barakah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) attended his daughter while she was dying, she being under Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, his eyes, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), overflowed, and Barakah, may Allah be pleased with her, wept and tore her hair. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked her, so she said: Do you weep, O Messenger of Allah, while we are silent? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What you saw from me is mercy for her. I am but a man; the believer is at every good station from Allah, exalted, upon hardship or ease."
"3887","3251 - Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Affan narrated to us, Aban al-Attar narrated, Abu Imran al-Jawni narrated, that it reached him that Jibril, upon him prayer and peace, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What makes you weep? He, upon him prayer and peace, said: By Allah, my eye has not dried since Allah, exalted, created the Fire, fearing to disobey Him lest He cast me into it."
"3888","56 - Chapter on repentance and seeking forgiveness. 3252 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah narrated, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivered a sermon to us, and he mentioned the hadith at length. He said: Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended, and a group of the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them, hastened to him before he descended from the pulpit, saying: Our selves for you as ransom, O Messenger of Allah, who can bear these hardships, and how is life after this day? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: And you, may my father and mother be ransom for you; I contended with my Lord, blessed and exalted, over my nation, and He said to me: The door of repentance is open until the Trumpet is blown. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever repents a year before his death, Allah turns to him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A year is much; whoever repents a month before his death, Allah turns to him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A month is much; whoever repents a week before his death, Allah turns to him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A week is much; whoever repents a day before his death, Allah turns to him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A day is much; whoever repents an hour before his death, Allah turns to him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever repents before death-rattle, Allah turns to him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended. It was the last sermon he delivered. Dawud and his shaykh are known for fabrication."
"3889","3253 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, he is al-Ansari, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Turn to your Lord, mighty and majestic, for I turn to Him every day seventy times or more than seventy."
"3890","3254/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Huraym ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated, from his father, Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I repent in the day seventy times."
"3891","3254/2 - Abu al-Ash'ath narrated to us, he said: I heard Mu'tamir with it."
"3892","3254/3 - Al-Bazzar said: Ahmad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Bahr narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it, but he said: A hundred times."
"3893","3255 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated, from Burayd ibn Abdullah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, exalted, is more joyful at the repentance of His servant who has been extravagant against himself than a man who lost his mount, sought it right and left until weary or despairing of it, and when ruined, looked and found it in a place he had not hoped to find it; Allah, mighty and majestic, is more joyful at the repentance of the extravagant servant than that man with his mount when he found it."
"3894","3256 - Abu Hammam narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to me, Ibn Abi al-Muhajir or Abu Abd Rabb narrated to me - al-Walid doubted - he said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A man among those before you met a learned or worshiping man and said: The other killed ninety-nine souls, all killing them unjustly; do you find repentance for me? He said: No, so he killed him. Then he met another and said: The other killed a hundred souls, all killing them unjustly; do you find repentance for me? He said: If I told you that Allah, blessed and exalted, does not turn to one who repents, I would have lied. Here is a monastery with people worshiping; go to them and worship Allah, mighty and majestic, with them, hoping Allah, exalted, turns to you. He set out to them but died before reaching them. The angels of punishment and the angels of mercy disputed. Allah, exalted, sent an angel: Measure between the two places; to whichever he was nearer, he is of them. They measured and found him nearer to the monastery of the repentant by a fingertip, so Allah, mighty and majestic, forgave him."
"3895","3257 - Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um narrated, Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat one day with us with him and said: No sin He forgives is too great for Allah, exalted. A man before you killed ninety-eight souls, came to a monk and said to him: I killed ninety-eight souls; do you find repentance for me? He said: No, so he killed him. Then he came to another monk and told him he killed ninety-nine souls; do you find repentance for me? He said: No, so he killed him. Then he came to another and told him he killed a hundred souls; do you find repentance for me? He said: You have been extravagant and I do not know, but here are two villages, one called Nadrah, whose people do the deed of the people of Paradise and none but them persists in it, and the other called Kafrah, whose people do the deed of the people of Hell and none but them persists in it. Go to the people of Nadrah; if you do their deed and repent, do not doubt your repentance. He set out seeking it until, between the two villages, his term overtook him. The angels, upon them peace, asked their Lord about him. He, majestic and exalted, said: See which of the two villages he was nearer and record him of its people. They found him nearer to Nadrah by a fingertip, so they recorded him of its people."
"3896","3258/1 - Abu Bakr said: Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Sharik, from Uthman ibn Abi Zur'ah, from Abu Sadiq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paradise has eight gates: seven closed, and a gate open for repentance until the sun rises from its direction."
"3897","3258/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"3898","3259 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abd al-Malik, from Ali ibn Rabi'ah, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, carried me behind him, then rode with me through a cemetery, then raised his head to the sky and said: O Allah, forgive me my sins, for none forgives sins but You. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, turned to me laughing. I said: O Commander of the Faithful, your seeking your Lord's forgiveness and your turning to me laughing? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put me behind him, then rode with me beside al-Harrah, then raised his head to the sky and said: O Allah, forgive me my sins, for none forgives sins but You. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me laughing. I said: O Messenger of Allah, your seeking your Lord's forgiveness and your turning to me laughing? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I laughed at my Lord's, mighty and majestic, laughing at His servant's wonder that he knows none forgives sins but Him."
"3899","3260 - Abd said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Mughirah ibn Abi al-Hurr al-Kindi narrated, from Sa'id ibn Abi Burdah, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while we were sitting and said: Never have I woken in a morning but I sought Allah's, exalted, forgiveness in it a hundred times."
"3900","3261 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhriz ibn Awn narrated to us, Uthman ibn Matar narrated, Abd al-Ghafur narrated, from Abu Nusayrah, from Abu Raja, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Upon you is: There is no god but Allah, and seeking forgiveness; increase in them both. For Iblis said: I destroyed people with sins, but they destroyed me with: There is no god but Allah, and seeking forgiveness. When I saw that, I destroyed them with desires while they think they are guided."
"3901","3262 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad narrated, Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr narrated, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No people sit in a sitting, engage in talk, then seek Allah's, blessed and exalted, forgiveness before dispersing, but Allah, mighty and majestic, forgives them what they engaged in."
"3902","3263 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah narrated, from Abu A'ishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivered a sermon to us, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it: The learned is only one who acts by his knowledge though his knowledge be little. Do not belittle any sin though it be small in your eyes, for there is no small with persistence nor great with seeking forgiveness. Indeed Allah, blessed and exalted, will ask you about your deeds, even about one of you touching his brother's garment. Know that a servant is raised on the Day of Resurrection upon what he died upon. Allah, exalted, created Paradise and Hell; whoever prefers Hell over Paradise, Allah, exalted, banishes him. Indeed Allah, mighty and majestic, left nothing He forbade but He made it clear to you, that whoever lives may live upon proof and whoever perishes may perish upon proof, the hadith."
"3903","3264 - Abd said: Yazid, he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Dukayn narrated, Qays al-Masir narrated, from Dawud al-Basri, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every believer has a sin he is accustomed to time after time, or a sin he will not leave until he dies or the Hour stands. The believer is created sinful, tried, erring, forgetful; when reminded, he remembers."
"3904","57 - Chapter on prohibition of extremism. 3265/1 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Mis'ar narrate to you? He said: Ma'n ibn Abd al-Rahman brought out to me a book and swore to me it was his father's handwriting, and in it: Abdullah said: By the One besides whom there is no god, I never saw anyone more fearful for the extremists than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more fearful than Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. And I see Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was most fearful for them or over them? Abu Usamah affirmed it and said: Yes."
"3905","3265/2 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated to us with it."
"3906","3265/3 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with it."
"3907","3266 - Ishaq said: Amr ibn Muhammad and Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us, both said: Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated, from his brother Abdullah ibn Ubaydah, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were teaching one another, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Praise be to Allah; the Book of Allah is one; among you are the red and the black. Recite - thrice - before there come people who set up its letters as an arrow is set up, hastening it and not deferring it. This is a weak isnad."
"3908","3267/1 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This Qur'an has a zeal, then people have a lull from it. Whoever's lull is toward moderation, then what a good state; whoever's lull is toward turning away, those are ruined."
"3909","3267/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated with this."
"3910","58 - Chapter on dislike of building beyond need. 3268 - al-Tayalisi said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab, from Abu al-Aliyah, who said: al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, built a chamber. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Demolish it. He said: Or shall I give its price in charity? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Demolish it, thrice."
"3911","3269 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Zakariyya narrated, from Abu Ammar, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a quarter of the residences of the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them. He raised his head and saw a built dome. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Anas, whose dome is this? I said: So-and-so's. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every building is a woe upon its owner on the Day of Resurrection except a building of sufficiency. That reached the Ansari man, may Allah be pleased with him, so he demolished it. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed after that and did not see it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Anas, what happened to the dome? I said: Your words reached its owner, so he demolished it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah forgive him."
"3912","59 - Chapter on dislike of dwelling in the desert and rebuke of seclusion without cause. 3270 - Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Yunus, Ma'mar narrated, from Musa ibn Shaybah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever goes to the desert more than two months, it is Bedouinism. This is mursal with a weak isnad."
"3913","3271 - Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Iyas ibn Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Desert life is two months; what exceeds is becoming Bedouin. This is halted, sahih."
"3914","3272/1 - Abu Bakr said: Sharik narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hakam, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever goes to the desert becomes coarse."
"3915","3272/2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this. And the hadith of As'as ibn Salamah has passed in jihad."
"3916","3273 /1 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibn Abdullah ibn Badr ibn Wasil ibn Abdullah ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Sa'd used to go out to his companions in Tustar to visit them, staying the day of his arrival and the second day, and leaving on the third. They would say to him: If only you stayed. He would say: I heard my father say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me - or I heard him forbid - settling permanently, so whoever stays three days in the land of kharaj has settled, and I dislike staying."
"3917","3273 /2 - Al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said in his Musnad: Wasil ibn Abdullah ibn Badr narrated to us, my father Abdullah ibn Badr narrated to me the same report."
"3918","3273 /3 - Abu Nu'aym said: Abu Amr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hamdan narrated to us, al-Hasan narrated the same."
"3919","Chapter 60 - Love of the believer for meeting Allah the Exalted. 3274 - Musaddad said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Uthman, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: He used to sit with him in Kufa, and one day while he was in his porch with two women of rank and beauty under his care, and he had a child by them like the finest of children, a sparrow dropped on his head then discharged its belly contents, so he wiped it off with his hand, then said: By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Abdullah, that the family of Abdullah die and then I follow them is dearer to me than that this sparrow die."
"3920","Chapter on the virtue of suppressing anger. 3274 - Ishaq said: al-Muqri informed us, Nuh ibn Ja'wanah al-Khurasani narrated, from Muqatil ibn Hayyan, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out ... then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: There is no draught dearer to Allah than a draught of anger that a servant suppresses for Allah; no servant suppresses it for Allah except that Allah fills his heart with faith."
"3921","Chapter 2 - Giving precedence to deeds of the Hereafter over deeds of this world. 3275 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-A'mash who said: And Habib ibn Abi Thabit said, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib who said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) was in a company of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man passed by them and asked them, and they answered him. Then he came to Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was laying his head on his saddle talking to himself. He said: What did you ask them? He said: I asked them about such and such and they said such and such. Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Two words, if you hold to them you hold to the good of what they said, and if you abandon them you abandon the good of what they said: if you begin with your share of this world, your share of the Hereafter will escape you, and you may not attain what you want between them; and if you begin with your share of the Hereafter, your share of this world will pass by you and become fully ordered for you, then it will turn with you wherever you turn."
"3922","3276 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Deliberation in everything is good except in deeds of the Hereafter."
"3923","3277 /1 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatadah, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah the Exalted loves a servant, He protects him from this world, just as one of you keeps water away from his sick person. 3277 /2 - Ibn Lahi'ah differed from him and narrated it from Umarah, from Asim, from Mahmud, from Uqbah ibn Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3924","Abu Ya'la said: Kamil ibn Talhah narrated to us, from him. 3277 /3 - Al-Tirmidhi narrated it from the hadith of Ismail ibn Ja'far, from Umarah, making the Companion Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man (may Allah be pleased with him). 3277 /4 - Among them are those who sent it as mursal from Ismail, not mentioning anyone above Mahmud. Likewise Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated it from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3925","3278 /1 - Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not give you glad tidings, O company of the poor? The poor of the believers will enter Paradise half a day, five hundred years, before their rich."
"3926","3278 /2 - Al-Bazzar said: al-Walid ibn Amr ibn Sukayn narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated the same, and added: And Musa recited: And a day with your Lord is like a thousand years of what you count. Al-Bazzar said: We do not know it from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) except through this chain."
"3927","3279 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Zimmani narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated, al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah narrated, Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This world is green and sweet; whoever is God-fearing in it and does good therein, otherwise he is like one who eats and is not satisfied. The distance between people is like the distance between the two stars, one rising from the east and the other setting in the west."
"3928","3280 - Shuja' ibn Makhlad Abu al-Fadl narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated, al-Junayd ibn al-Ala ibn Abi Wahrah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Sa'id, from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Free yourselves from the worries of this world as much as you can, for whoever makes this world his greatest concern, Allah the Exalted will scatter his affairs and place his poverty before his eyes; and whoever makes the Hereafter his greatest concern, Allah the Mighty and Majestic will gather his affairs for him and place his richness in his heart. No servant turns with his heart to Allah the Mighty and Majestic except that Allah makes the hearts of the believers incline to him with love and mercy, and Allah the Exalted hastens to him with every good."
"3929","3281 - Al-Harith said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Subayh informed, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends by his intention to seek the Hereafter, Allah the Exalted will gather his affairs for him, and this world will come to him humbled; and whoever intends by his intention to seek this world, Allah the Mighty and Majestic will place poverty before his eyes, scatter his affairs, and nothing will come to him of it except what was decreed for him."
"3930","3282 - Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, Usamah ibn Zayd narrated, from Ibn Labibah, from Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The best of provision is what suffices, and the best of remembrance is what is hidden."
"3931","3283 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Rabi' narrated, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abu Murrah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Two vicious hungry wolves among sheep, one separating at its beginning and the other at its end, are not quicker in causing corruption than a man who loves the honor and wealth of this world in his religion."
"3932","3284 /1 - Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Hajjaj ibn Furafisah, from Makhul, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever seeks this world unlawfully, out of rivalry, boasting and showing off, will meet Allah the Blessed and Exalted while He is angry with him; and whoever seeks this world lawfully, to refrain from begging, striving for his family and showing kindness to his neighbor, will meet Allah the Mighty and Majestic with his face like the moon on the night of full moon."
"3933","3284 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated, Hajjaj ibn Furafisah narrated the like. This is disconnected between Makhul and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"3934","3285 - Abu Ya'la said: Husayn ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated, Umar ibn Hamzah narrated, Nafi' ibn Malik narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no god but Allah protects from the wrath of Allah the Mighty and Majestic so long as they do not prefer their world over their religion; but if they do that, then say There is no god but Allah, Allah the Mighty and Majestic will say: You have lied."
"3935","3286 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Thawban the freedman of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what suffices me of this world? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What fills your hunger and covers your nakedness; and if you have a house to shade you or an animal to ride, then excellent, excellent."
"3936","3287 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Adhrami narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abi Qays, from Sulayman ibn Habib who said: I heard Abu Umamah al-Bahili (may Allah be pleased with him) say: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sent, his troops came to Iblis and said: A prophet has been sent and a nation has been brought forth. He said: Do they love this world? They said: Yes. He said: If they love it, I do not care if they do not worship idols; they will not escape me, for I will come to them morning and evening with three things: taking wealth without right, spending it without right, and withholding it from its right; and all evil follows this."
"3937","Chapter 3 - Commanding good. 3288 - Ishaq said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: I heard my father say: Abu Nadrah informed, from Abu Sa'id freedman of Abu Usayd who said: Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) forbade hoarding, and a man kept interceding until his freedman was left. Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) entered the market and found the freedmen of Banu Umayyah hoarding, so he set upon them beating them. While he was so, Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) came on a mule or beast, so he walked to him, took the bridle of the mule and shook it hard, and spoke harshly to him. Then he left him, and when he dismounted a cushion was placed for him and he sat on it. Al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) came, greeted him, and said: By Allah, O Commander of the Faithful, I know you have a right over me, but I am a man who when I see evil cannot be patient. Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Sit down, and seated him on the cushion beside him."
"3938","3289 - Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed me, Hisham narrated from Ibn Sa'd, from Uthman ibn Hani, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me one day, and I knew something had happened to him. He did not speak to anyone, performed ablution, then went out, and I heard from the rooms; I heard him say: Allah says: O people, command good and forbid evil before you supplicate to Him and He does not answer you, and you ask Him and He does not give you, and you seek His help and He does not help you. I said: I did not know Uthman ibn Hani."
"3939","3290 - Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, Khalid ibn Yazid al-Fazari narrated to me, from al-Hasan who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) rejected a verse recitation of Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him). Ubayy (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you, O Umar, were distracted by bargaining in al-Baqi'. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: You have spoken truthfully; I only wanted to test you whether among you is one who speaks the truth. There is no good in a ruler before whom truth is not spoken and who does not speak it. This is disconnected."
"3940","3291 - Abu Usamah informed us, Abu Sinan Isa ibn Sinan narrated, from Ya'la ibn Shaddad ibn Aws who said: Mu'awiyah mentioned fleeing from the plague in a sermon, so Ubadah ibn al-Samit (may Allah be pleased with him) said: You have lied; your mother Hind knows better than you. Then he prayed, then he sent for Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him), and the Ansar rose with him and he detained them, and Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him) entered. Mu'awiyah said to him: Did you not fear Allah the Exalted and feel shame before your imam, declaring me a liar on the pulpit? Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Did you not know that I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of al-Aqabah that I would not fear the blame of a blamer for Allah; how then if I lied about Allah the Exalted? Then Mu'awiyah went out at Asr, prayed, then took hold of the pulpit post and said: O people, I mentioned a hadith to you on the pulpit and Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him) declared me mistaken. I entered the house and asked, and the hadith is as Ubadah narrates to me, so take knowledge from him, for he is more learned than me."
"3941","3292 - Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, I think from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from al-Rabi' ibn Umaylah who said: Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said: There will be tribulations and tribulations; it suffices a man when he sees a matter he cannot change that it be known from him that his heart hates it."
"3942","3293 - Al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Khalid, from Ibrahim ibn Bashir, from Khalid ibn Sa'd freedman of Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) visited Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was ill, and propped him up. Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: Advise us. He said: True misguidance is that you approve what you used to disapprove, and disapprove what you used to approve; and beware of changing colors in the religion of Allah the Exalted."
"3943","3294 - Yazid narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from one who informed him, he said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: You shall command good and forbid evil, or Allah the Mighty and Majestic will set your worst over you, then your best will supplicate and will not be answered for them."
"3944","Chapter 4 - Sincerity is part of religion. 3295 /1 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Religion is sincere advice. They said: To whom, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: To the Book of Allah the Exalted, to His Prophet, and to the leaders of the Muslims."
"3945","3295 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"3946","3296 - Abu Ya'la also said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah narrated to us, Salm ibn Qutaybah narrated, al-Hasan ibn Ali narrated, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) commanded me with sincerity."
"3947","3297 - Abu Ibrahim al-Tarjuman narrated to us, Salih al-Murri narrated, he said: I heard al-Hasan narrate from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he narrates from his Lord the Mighty and Majestic who said: Four qualities, one of them is Mine, one is yours, one is between Me and you, and one is between you and My servants. As for what is Mine: you worship Me and associate nothing with Me. As for what is yours: whatever good you do I will reward you for it. As for what is between Me and you: from you is supplication and upon Me is answering. As for what is between you and My servants: be pleased for them with what you are pleased with for yourself."
"3948","Chapter 5 - Encouraging commanding good and forbidding evil even if one does not fully practice it. 3298 /1 - Abu Bakr said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated, Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him) came from Abyssinia to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What is the strangest thing you saw? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I saw a woman with a basket of food on her head; a horseman passed galloping and scattered it, so she sat gathering her food, then turned to him and said: Woe to you on the day the King sets His Throne and takes for the oppressed from the oppressor. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said confirming her words: No nation is sanctified - or how can a nation be sanctified - whose weak cannot take his right from its strong without being troubled. His saying without being troubled means without being harmed in a way that distresses or disturbs him."
"3949","3298 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated the same."
"3950","3298 /3 - Al-Ruyani said: Ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated the same. Its chain is good. Al-Bazzar said: We do not know for it a chain other than this. And I do not know whether Mansur heard from Ata after his confusion or before. 3298 /4 - Amr ibn Abi Qays followed him, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Muharib; al-Hakim brought it. The hadith of Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) on that will come in the Book of the Day of Resurrection. In the chapter are reports from Abu Sa'id, Ibn Mas'ud and Aishah (may Allah be pleased with them)."
"3951","3299 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Abi Ubaydah narrated to us, from his father, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No nation is sanctified whose weak is not given his right therein without being troubled. I think Ibn Majah brought it within a hadith, so it should be looked at in the Book of Judgments of his book."
"3952","3300 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ismail ibn Abi Hakim who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Allah the Exalted does not punish the common people for the deeds of the elite; but if sins appear and are not denounced, the common and the elite are both seized."
"3953","3301 - Al-Harith said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Wuhayb narrated, Ayyub narrated, from Amr ibn Sa'id, from Abu Zur'ah ibn Amr ibn Jarir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) demanding dates owed to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him for respite, but he refused to grant him respite. The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked him, so he said: I adjure you by him not to leave Medina while I seek something from him, for by Allah I will not return to my land until more is plundered from it than what I seek from you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to a woman of Banu Sulaym called Khawlah seeking to borrow dates, so she sent dates to him and said: If you want more of this, we have what you want. He said: Do you want from this? He said: Yes, go, measure and take in full. Then he said: He was more in need of your support than me, and I am more in need that you command me to fulfill my trust. And he said: Allah the Exalted does not sanctify a nation that does not aid its weak, or he said: does not strengthen its weak."
"3954","3302 - Al-Harith said: Yazid narrated to us, Abu al-Fadl a shaykh in Wasit narrated, Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated, he said: Marwan took a minbar ... the hadith, so Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever sees an innovation, let him change it."
"3955","3303 - Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from Asim, from Abu Wa'il who said: Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I command you with what I do not do, but I hope to be rewarded for it."
"3956","3304 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Qays narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not fitting for a person who witnesses a stand of truth except to speak it, for it will neither hasten his term nor deprive him of provision due to him."
"3957","Chapter 6 - Virtue of piety and God-consciousness. 3305 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Abbad narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata and Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited this verse: And these examples We present to people, but none understands them except the learned. He said: The learned is he who understood from Allah the Exalted and acted in His obedience and avoided His wrath."
"3958","3306 - He said: And Ata said, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), raised: The best of people are the most intelligent of people. Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: And that is your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3959","3307 - Dawud narrated to us, Abbad narrated, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): A Christian man from the people of Jurash came as a merchant, eloquent and dignified. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, how intelligent is this Christian. He rebuked the speaker and said: Hush, the intelligent is he who acts in obedience to Allah the Blessed and Exalted."
"3960","3308 - Dawud narrated to us, Abbad narrated, Suhayl narrated, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah and Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O son of Adam, fear your Lord, be dutiful to your parents and join your kin; your life will be increased, your ease made easy, your hardship lightened, and your provision expanded for you. O son of Adam, obey your Lord and you will be called intelligent, and do not disobey your Lord lest you be called ignorant."
"3961","3309 - Dawud narrated to us, Sallam narrated, from Hisham, from Humayd ibn Hilal who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The death of a thousand worshippers who stand the night and fast the day is easier than the death of an intelligent man who understood Allah the Mighty and Majestic's command, learned what Allah made lawful and unlawful for him, benefited by his knowledge and people benefited by him, even if he did not add much beyond the obligations Allah the Exalted imposed on him. Likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said."
"3962","3310 - Dawud narrated to us, Maysarah narrated, from Hanzalah ibn Wada'ah, from his father, from al-Bara ibn Azib (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah the Exalted has special ones whom He settles in the highest of Gardens; they were the most intelligent of people. He said: They are those whose concern is racing to their Lord and hastening to what pleases Him; they renounced this world, its excess, its leadership, and it became trivial to them, so they were patient a little and rested long."
"3963","3311 - Dawud narrated to us, Maysarah narrated, from Musa ibn Jaban, from Luqman ibn Amir, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The ignorant reveals only disgrace, even if he is clever and refined before people; and the intelligent reveals only virtue, even if he is inarticulate and despised before people. These hadiths from the Book of Intellect of Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar are all fabricated; al-Harith mentioned them in his Musnad from him, and many of them preceded in the chapter of intellect in the Book of Manners."
"3964","3312 - Al-Harith said: Rawh narrated to us, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated, from Humayd ibn Hilal, Abu Qatadah and Abu al-Dahma who used to travel often toward the House narrated to us, they said: We came upon a Bedouin man, and the Bedouin said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and began teaching me of what Allah the Exalted taught him. Among what I memorized is that he said: Do not leave anything out of fear of Allah the Mighty and Majestic except that Allah the Exalted will replace it for you with better than it."
"3965","3313 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abi al-Juwayriyah who said: He heard al-Hakam ibn Mina say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): Gather for me those Quraysh who are here. So he gathered them, then said: O Messenger of Allah, do you go out to them or do they enter? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather I will go out to them. So he went out and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O company of Quraysh, is there among you other than you? They said: No, except the sons of our sisters. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The son of the sister of a people is of them. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Quraysh, know that the most entitled of people to the Prophet are the God-fearing; so see to it that people do not come with deeds on the Day of Resurrection while you come with this world carrying it, and I turn my face away from you. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: Indeed the most entitled of people to Ibrahim are those who followed him, and this Prophet - the verse."
"3966","Chapter 7 - Virtue of fear of Allah the Exalted and weeping from His fear. 3314 /1 - Abu Ya'la said: Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim informed, and al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah and Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated, both from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Bakr ibn Ubaydullah ibn Anas, from his grandfather Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends a good deed, Allah the Exalted records it for him as a good deed; if he does it, ten good deeds are recorded for him. Whoever intends an evil deed, it is not recorded against him until he does it; if he does it, one evil deed is recorded against him, and if he abandons it, a good deed is recorded for him; Allah the Blessed and Exalted says: He abandoned it only out of fear of Me. The wording is that of Mujahid."
"3967","3314 /2 - Al-Harith said: Ya'la narrated to us, Abd al-Hakam narrated, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends a good deed and does it, ten good deeds are recorded for him; if he does not do it, one good deed is recorded for him. If he intends an evil deed and does it, one evil deed is recorded against him; if he does not do it, nothing is recorded against him."
"3968","3315 /1 - Abd said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated, Yazid ibn Khumayr narrated, from Sulayman, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: If you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much, and you would go out crying out, not knowing whether you will be saved or not."
"3969","3315 /2 - Al-Bazzar said: al-Hasan ibn Yahya and Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated the same. He said: We do not know it narrated from Shu'bah except by Muslim ibn Ibrahim. And the daughter of Abu al-Darda is not known."
"3970","3316 - Abd said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Zuhri narrated to us, my father narrated, from Salih ibn Kaysan who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Forbidden for two eyes to be touched by the Fire: an eye that wept from fear of Allah the Exalted, and an eye that spent the night guarding Islam and its people against the disbelievers. And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No servant weeps and his eyes shed tears from fear of Allah the Exalted and Allah puts him into the Fire ever, until the rain returns to the sky. And it is said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the pulpit when the people returned from Mu'tah with a piece of bread in his hand; when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned their affair, his eyes overflowed, so he wiped his face and said: I am only a man; I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan. A man thinks himself many by his brother; who has a promise with me? Salman al-Farisi (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I, O Messenger of Allah, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave it to him. And Barakah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: When the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who was under Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was dying, his eyes overflowed and Barakah (may Allah be pleased with her) wept and tore her hair, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rebuked her. She said: Do you weep, O Messenger of Allah, while we are silent? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What you saw from me is mercy for her; I am only a man. The believer is in a good station with Allah the Exalted in hardship or ease."
"3971","3317 /1 - Abu Ya'la said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Abdullah al-Rumi narrated, Jabir ibn Yazid ibn Rifa'ah narrated to me, from Harun ibn Abi al-Jawza, from al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) under a tree when the wind stirred and the dry leaves fell, while the green leaves remained. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is the likeness of this tree? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its likeness is that of the believer: when he trembles from fear of Allah the Exalted, his sins fall from him and his good deeds remain for him."
"3972","3317 /2 - Al-Bazzar narrated it through another chain from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Umm Kulthum bint al-Abbas, from her father (may Allah be pleased with them), restricting it to his saying: When the skin of the servant shudders from fear of Allah the Exalted, his sins fall from him as leaves fall from the dry tree."
"3973","3318 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Ayyub ibn Shabib al-San'ani narrated to me as presented to Rabah ibn Zayd, Abdullah ibn Bahir narrated to me, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Do not forget the two great things. We said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the two great things? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paradise and Hell. Then he mentioned what he mentioned until he wept so that tears flowed or wet the sides of his beard (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then said: By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, if you knew of the matter what I know, you would walk to the open land and throw dust on your heads."
"3974","Chapter 8 - Retaliation on the Day of Resurrection. 3319 /1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, Ibn Khuthaym narrated, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the sea emigrants returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Will you not inform us of the wonders you saw in the land of Abyssinia? Some youths among them said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. While we were sitting, an old woman of their nuns passed with a water jar on her head; a youth among them passed and put one of his hands between her shoulders, so she fell on her knees and her jar broke. When she rose, she turned to him and said: You will know, O treacherous one, when Allah the Exalted sets the Throne and gathers the first and the last, and hands and feet speak of what they used to earn; you will know how my affair and your affair stand with Him tomorrow. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She spoke truthfully, she spoke truthfully; how can Allah sanctify a people whose weak is not given justice from their strong."
"3975","3319 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated the same. 3319 /3 - Muslim ibn Khalid followed him, from Ibn Khuthaym; Harmalah brought it from Ibn Wahb."
"3976","3320 /1 - Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi Abdullah narrated, from Ibn Jud'an, from his grandmother, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a maidservant of his and she delayed, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not for fear of retaliation, I would have hurt you with this siwak."
"3977","3320 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki' narrated the same."
"3978","3320 /3 - Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated to us, my father narrated, Dawud ibn Abi Abdullah narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Jud'an al-Qurashi, from his grandmother, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in my house with a siwak in his hand, and he called a servant of his until anger became clear on his face (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) went out to the neighbors and found the maid playing among them, and said: Do I see you playing with this sheep while his Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) calls you? She said: By the One who sent you with truth, I did not hear you. Then he mentioned it."
"3979","Book 25 - Book of Adhkar and Supplications. Chapter 1 - Sending blessings on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3321 - Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, Abu Qurrah al-Asadi informed, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: It was mentioned to me that supplication remains between heaven and earth, nothing of it ascends until blessings are sent on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3980","3322 /1 - al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Ma'bad informed me, a man in the mosque of Damascus informed me, from Awf ibn Malik who said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat with him, and said: O Abu Dharr, did you pray Duha? Then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: The most astray of people is he before whom I am mentioned and he does not send blessings on me (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3981","3322 /2 - Al-Harith said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aishah narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Ibn Hilal al-Anazi, a man narrated to me, then he mentioned it."
"3982","3323 /1 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Sa'id ibn Sa'id Abu al-Sabbah narrate to you, Sa'id ibn Umayr ibn Uqbah narrated, from his uncle Abu Burdah ibn Niyar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: No servant sends a prayer on me sincerely from himself except that Allah the Exalted records for him ten prayers for it, records ten good deeds, erases ten evil deeds, and raises him ten degrees for it? So Abu Usamah acknowledged it and said: Yes. 3323 /2 - al-Nasa'i narrated it in the Day and Night from Zakariyya ibn Yahya, from Abu Kurayb with it."
"3983","3324 - Abd said: Ja'far ibn Awn narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from his father who said: Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Do not make me like the cup of the rider; when the rider hangs his belongings, he takes his cup and fills it with water: if he needs ablution he performs ablution, if he needs drink he drinks, otherwise he pours out what is in it. Place me at the beginning of supplication, in its middle, and at its end."
"3984","3325 - Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Munib narrated, from al-Sari ibn Yahya, from a man of Tayy, and he praised him well, who said: I used to ask Allah the Exalted to show me the Name by which if He is called He answers; so I saw it written in the stars in the sky: O Originator of the heavens and earth, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor."
"3985","3326 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Damdam al-Amiri, Imran al-Himyari narrated, he said: I heard Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah the Blessed and Exalted has given me an angel who stands at my grave when I die; no servant sends a prayer on me except that he says: O Muhammad, so-and-so son of so-and-so sends blessings on you, naming him by his name and his father's name; so Allah the Mighty and Majestic sends ten in its place on him."
"3986","3327 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Salamah ibn Wardan narrated, he said: I heard Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to relieve himself and found no man to follow him, so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) hastened and followed him with an earthen vessel and water for ablution. He found him prostrating in his water-shed, so he withdrew and sat behind him until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his head and said: You did well, O Umar, when you found me prostrating and withdrew from me. Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me and said: Whoever sends one prayer on you, Allah sends ten on him and raises him ten degrees."
"3987","3328 - With the same chain, he said: I heard Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended one step of the pulpit and said: Amin, then ascended another step and said: Amin, then ascended the third and said: Amin. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) settled and sat, so his Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) said: O Prophet of Allah, upon what did you say Amin? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me and said: May the nose of a man who reaches his parents or one of them and does not enter Paradise be in dust. He said: I said: Amin. And may the nose of one who reaches Ramadan and is not forgiven be in dust. He said: I said: Amin. And may the nose of one before whom you are mentioned and he does not send blessings on you be in dust. He said: I said: Amin."
"3988","3329 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ma'mar Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit and said: Amin, Amin, Amin. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, when you ascended the pulpit you said: Amin, Amin, Amin. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me and said: Whoever reaches the month of Ramadan and is not forgiven, then enters the Fire, may Allah the Exalted distance him; say: Amin, so I said: Amin. And whoever reaches his parents or one of them and does not honor them, then dies and enters the Fire, may Allah distance him; say: Amin, so I said: Amin. And whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings on you, then dies and enters the Fire, may Allah distance him; say: Amin, so I said: Amin."
"3989","3330 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Hurrah, from al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Send many blessings on me on Friday, for they are presented to me. This is mursal."
"3990","3331 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed, from Abu Dawud al-A'ma, from Buraydah al-Khuza'i who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we know how to greet you, so how do we send blessings on you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: O Allah, place Your prayers and mercy on Muhammad and on the family of Muhammad, as You placed them on the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"3991","3332 - Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Balj al-Fazari narrated, Thuwayr freedman of Banu Hashim narrated, he said: I said to Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): How is sending blessings on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Allah, place Your prayers, blessings and mercy on the chief of the messengers, the imam of the God-fearing, the seal of the prophets, Muhammad Your servant and Messenger, the imam of good and leader of good. O Allah, raise him on the Day of Resurrection to a praised station envied by the first and the last. And send blessings on Muhammad and on the family of Muhammad as You sent blessings on Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"3992","3333 - Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed me, Isra'il narrated, from Abi Yahya al-Qattat, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas who said: There is no one of the nation of Muhammad who sends blessings on Muhammad or greets him except that it reaches him: So-and-so sends blessings on you and so-and-so greets you."
"3993","Chapter 2 - Sending blessings on other than the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3334 /1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, al-Rabadhi Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Muhammad ibn Thabit informed me, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Send blessings on the prophets and messengers of Allah, for Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent them as He sent me; the prayers and peace of Allah upon them all."
"3994","3334 /2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Sa'id freedman of Banu Hashim narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, then he mentioned it."
"3995","3335 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Hasan narrated, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Darraj, from Abi al-Haytham, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who has no wealth in which there is charity, and says: O Allah, send blessings on Muhammad Your servant and Messenger, and send blessings on the believing men and women and the Muslim men and women, then it is purification for him."
"3996","Chapter 3 - Warning against heedlessness of remembrance of Allah the Exalted. 3336 /1 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, al-Ifriqi narrated, from Hudayj ibn Sawmiyy, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Heedlessness is in three things: from remembrance of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, from the time the dawn prayer is prayed until the sun rises, and a man's heedlessness of himself until debt overtakes him."
"3997","3336 /2 - Abd said: Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated the same."
"3998","3337 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, a trustworthy companion of ours called Umar ibn Hafs narrated, from a shaykh of Banu Sulaym called Zubayd who read the Quran for twenty years completing it in a day and night, and twenty years completing it in two days and two nights, he said: By Allah, as if there were light on his face, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he sensed inadvertence or heedlessness among his Companions would call among them in a loud voice: Death has come to you regularly, either wretchedness or felicity. Umar ibn Hafs said: And this shaykh said to us: I am this year better than me last year; last year I had a sheep, and this year I have no sheep. And a man said to him: I wanted to marry a woman, so supplicate Allah the Exalted for me to marry me to a righteous woman. He said: So he supplicated Allah the Exalted, and a righteous woman was made ready for him."
"3999","Chapter 4 - Virtue of supplication. 3338 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi Yazid al-Hamdani narrated to us, Bakr ibn Khunays narrated, from Dirar ibn Amr, from al-Raqashi, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: All deeds of righteousness are half of worship, and supplication is half; so when Allah the Exalted wants good for a servant, his heart inclines to supplication."
"4000","3339 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Kufi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi Yazid al-Hamdani narrated, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Supplication is the weapon of the believer, the pillar of religion, and the light of the heavens and earth."
"4001","3340 /1 - Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Sallam ibn Sulaym narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not guide you to what will save you from your enemy and increase your provisions? You supplicate Allah the Exalted in your night and day, for supplication is the weapon of the believer."
"4002","3340 /2 - Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Sallam ibn Sulaym narrated, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir the same."
"4003","3341 - Al-Harith said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated, al-Hakam narrated, from Muhammad ibn Rabi', from Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day, and he said: The likeness of the believer is like a tree whose leaves do not fall; do you know what it is? They said: No. He said: It is the palm tree, whose leaves do not fall, and no supplication of the believer is lost."
"4004","3342 /1 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated, from Asim, from Abi Uthman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The most miserly of people is he who is miserly with greeting, and the most incapable of people is he who is incapable of supplication."
"4005","3342 /2 - Al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Hassan narrated, Hibban ibn Ali narrated, from Ata ibn Ajlan, from Abi Nadrah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like and added: When you supplicate, let the young and old, the blind and the eloquent among you supplicate, for you do not know which of you will be answered - the hadith."
"4006","3343 - Al-Harith said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated, al-Rabi' ibn Subayh narrated, he said: al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: Allah the Mighty and Majestic may delay the supplication for the servant and give it to him on the Day of Resurrection; he would not like that any fleeting portion of this world had befallen him."
"4007","Chapter 5 - Comprehensive supplications. 3344 /1 - al-Tayalisi said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabr ibn Habib, from Umm Kulthum, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she was praying, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Upon you is supplication with the complete and comprehensive. When she finished, she asked him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: O Allah, I ask You for all good, what I know of it and what I do not know, and I seek refuge with You from all evil, what I know of it and what I do not know, and I seek refuge with You from the Fire and what brings near to it of word or deed. O Allah, I ask You for Paradise and what brings near to it of word or deed. O Allah, I ask You for the good Your servant and Messenger Muhammad asked You for - the hadith. Al-Jurayri followed him from Jabr, and Abu Na'amah differed from him."
"4008","3344 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated, Abu Na'amah narrated, from Jabr ibn Habib, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) came seeking permission while she was praying, so she kept clapping and he did not understand her. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while they were at the door and said: What prevented you from taking the comprehensive and opening words? She said: What are their comprehensive and concluding? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You say: O Allah, I ask You for all good, what I know of it and what I do not know, its immediate and later, and I seek refuge with You from all evil, what I know of it and what I do not know, its immediate and later. O Allah, whatever decree You decree, make its outcome guidance. Its origin is in Muslim through another chain."
"4009","3345 - Abu Yusuf Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Jizi narrated to us, Mu'ammal narrated, Sufyan narrated, a shaykh of the people of Medina narrated, from Salim, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say in his supplication: Protection like the protection of the newborn. Abu Ya'la said: Meaning the child."
"4010","3346 - Shayban narrated to us, Sallam ibn Miskin narrated, Ismah Abu Hukaymah narrated to me, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Shall I not teach you of what Jibril (peace be upon him) taught me? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: O Allah, forgive me my sins, my intentional and my jesting and my serious; do not deprive me of the blessing of what You gave me, and do not test me by what You withheld from me."
"4011","3347 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Ifriqi narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi', from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to often say: O Allah, I ask You for health, chastity, trustworthiness, good character, and contentment with decree."
"4012","3348 /1 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Dawud al-A'ma, from Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Shall I not teach you words: whoever Allah the Exalted wants good for teaches them to him, then he never forgets them ever: O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen my weakness in Your pleasure, take my forelock to good, and make Islam the utmost of my pleasure. O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen me; lowly, so honor me; poor, so provide for me."
"4013","3348 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir narrated, from al-Ala the same."
"4014","Chapter 6 - Prohibition of singling out oneself in supplication. 3349 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with them) who said: A man said: O Allah, forgive me and Muhammad alone. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have restricted it from many people. Ibn Hibban brought it."
"4015","3350 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ubaydah ibn Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Abu Sa'id freedman of Banu Hashim narrated, al-Mas'udi narrated, from Awn ibn Abdullah who said: I met an old man in Sham and said: Did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, I heard him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: O Allah, forgive us and have mercy on us."
"4016","Chapter 7 - Raising hands in supplication. 3351 - Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Ruzayq narrated, Abu Dawud al-A'ma narrated, from al-Bara ibn Azib (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that when hardship befell him and he supplicated, he raised his hands until the whiteness of his armpits was seen (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4017","3352 - al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, Abu Hilal owner of this house narrated to me, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his hands in supplication until the whiteness of his armpits was seen (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4018","3353 - Musaddad said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated, from Abi Qilabah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhayriz (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you ask Allah the Mighty and Majestic, ask Him with the palms of your hands, and do not ask Him with their backs. Khalid said: I said to Abi Qilabah: What is the meaning of this? So he raised between his hands and said: Thus is takbir and tahlil."
"4019","Chapter 8 - What to say when supplicating for people. 3354 /1 - Abd said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) strove for someone in supplication, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah place upon you the prayer of righteous people, neither sinful nor wicked, who stand the night and fast the day."
"4020","3354 /2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Nasr al-Tammar narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When one of them strove for his brother in supplication, he said: Then he mentioned it."
"4021","Chapter 9 - Supplicating with one palm. 3355 - Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Ash'ath ibn Sulaym narrated, from a man of the Ansar: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was supplicating spreading both his palms, and said: Make it one, for He is One."
"4022","3356 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Isra'il narrated, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha, from a man of the Ansar (may Allah be pleased with them) who narrated to him from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was supplicating with his hands, and said: Make it one, for He is One."
"4023","Chapter 10 - Command to say inna lillah in everything and ask Allah everything. 3357 /1 - Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ubaydullah, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let one of you say inna lillah for everything, even for the strap of his sandal, for it is of calamities."
"4024","3357 /2 - Hafs narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ubaydullah, then he mentioned it with the wording: When the strap of one of you breaks, let him say inna lillah, for it is of calamities; and ask Allah the Mighty and Majestic even for the strap, for if He does not make it easy, it will not be."
"4025","3357 /3 - Khalid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ubaydullah the same."
"4026","3358 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn al-Munadi narrated to us, Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated, Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Abi al-Waddah narrated, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Ask Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, for everything, even the strap, for if Allah the Exalted does not make it easy, it will not become easy."
"4027","Chapter 11 - What to say when taking bed. 3359 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father who said: I was with Ammar (may Allah be pleased with him) when a man came to him and said: Shall I not teach you words, as if raising them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: When you take your bed at night, say: O Allah, I submit myself to You, turn my face to You, entrust my affair to You, and lay my back on You. I believe in Your revealed Book and Your sent Prophet. O Allah, my soul You created; to You is its life and to You is its death; if You take it, have mercy on it, and if You delay it, guard it with the guarding of faith. Its chain is good, and it has a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of al-Bara (may Allah be pleased with him) and from the hadith of others."
"4028","3360 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Salamah ibn Wardan narrated, he said: I heard Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) say: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of need, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I not guide you to better than that? You say there is no god but Allah thirty-three times at your sleep, glorify Him thirty-three times, and praise Him thirty-four times; that is one hundred, better for you than this world and what is in it."
"4029","3361 /1 - al-Tayalisi said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Shahr, a man informed us, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sleeps pure and wakes in the night, he does not ask Allah the Exalted for anything of the Hereafter and this world except that He gives it to him. 3361 /2 - Thabit said: The man from whom Shahr narrated to us came to us and narrated this hadith to us."
"4030","3362 - Abu Ya'la said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated, al-Sari ibn Ismail narrated, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would order his bed to be spread for him, facing the qiblah; when he retired to it, he pillowed his right hand, then whispered, we do not know what he said. At the end of that he raised his voice and said: O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens, Lord of the Mighty Throne, God and Lord of everything, Revealer of the Torah, the Gospel and the Furqan, Splitter of grain and seed, I seek refuge with You from the evil of everything whose forelock You hold. O Allah, You are the First, there is nothing before You; You are the Last, there is nothing after You; You are the Manifest, there is nothing above You; You are the Hidden, there is nothing below You. Settle our debt for us and enrich us from poverty."
"4031","Chapter 12 - What to say when waking. 3363 /1 - Al-Harith said: Khalid ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Layth narrated, from Ishaq ibn Abi Farwah, from Musa ibn Wardan, from Nabil owner of the cloak, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says when he wakes, after Allah the Exalted has returned his soul to him: There is no god but Allah alone without partner, to Him is dominion and praise, in His Hand is good, and He is over everything Capable, his sins are forgiven even if like the foam of the sea. I said: Its chain is weak because of Ishaq. 3363 /2 - Ibn al-Sunni brought it in the Day and Night from the chain of Ismail ibn Ayyash from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Musa ibn Wardan. I think Ismail erred in it; it is rather the hadith of Ishaq ibn Abi Farwah."
"4032","Chapter 13 - What to say when sleepless. 3364 - Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to me, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) was kept awake or sleeplessness befell him, so he complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered him to seek refuge at his sleep with the perfect words of Allah from His anger, punishment, the evil of His servants, the whispers of devils and their presence."
"4033","3365 - Abu Ya'la said: Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Ibn Ulasah narrated, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan who said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrate from his father Marwan ibn al-Hakam, from Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of sleeplessness that befell me, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: O Allah, the stars have set and eyes have rested, and You are Living, Sustaining; neither slumber nor sleep overtakes You. O Living, O Sustaining, calm my night and make my eye sleep. So I said it, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic removed what I felt. Ibn Adi said: Amr ibn al-Husayn is alone in it and his hadith is dark. Abu Zur'ah weakened him, Abu Hatim abandoned him, and al-Khatib declared him a liar."
"4034","Chapter 14 - What to say when leaving house. 3366 - al-Tayalisi said: Abu Bakr al-Hudhali narrated to us, from Amir, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, from Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left my house, he raised his head to the sky and said: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from slipping, straying, wronging, being wronged, acting ignorantly, or being treated ignorantly."
"4035","Chapter 15 - What one whose ear rings says. 3367 /1 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us, Hibban ibn Ali narrated, Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi' narrated, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the ear of one of you rings, let him remember me, send blessings on me, and say: May Allah mention with good who mentioned me."
"4036","3367 /2 - Al-Bazzar said: Ziyad ibn Yahya Abu al-Khattab narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Ubaydullah narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abi Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned it without his saying: and send blessings on me."
"4037","Chapter 16 - What one who boards ship says. 3368 /1 - Abu Ya'la said: Jabbarah ibn al-Mughallis narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated, from Marwan ibn Salim, from Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, from al-Husayn ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Protection for my nation from drowning when they ride the sea is to say: In the Name of Allah is its course and anchorage; indeed my Lord is Forgiving, Merciful. And: They did not esteem Allah as He deserves - the verse. 3368 /2 - Sayf ibn al-Hajjaj al-Kufi followed him, from Yahya ibn al-Ala. And Yahya is very weak."
"4038","Chapter 17 - What calamity is repelled by supplication. 3369 /1 - Ishaq said: Shabbabah ibn Sawwar Abu Amr al-Mada'ini informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Mulayki narrated, from Ibn Abi Husayn, from Makhul, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Caution does not avail against decree, but supplication repels. Al-Mulayki is weak, and Makhul did not hear from Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him). 3369 /2 - Ahmad narrated it from the hadith of Ismail ibn Ayyash, from Ibn Abi Husayn, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) with another wording."
"4039","3370 - Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, al-Awza'i narrated, from Hassan ibn Atiyyah who said: There is no harm in saying amin to the supplication of a monk when he supplicates for you. He said: They are answered for us and not answered for themselves."
"4040","Chapter 18 - Supplication of the sick. 3371 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Nasr narrated to us, Amir ibn Yasaf narrated, from Yahya, from al-Hasan, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Shall I not inform you of a matter that is truth: whoever speaks it at the first of his bed in his illness, Allah the Exalted will save him from the Fire? He said: Yes, by my father and mother. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Know that when you wake you may not reach evening, and when you evening you may not reach morning; and if you say that at the first of your bed in your illness, Allah the Exalted will save you by it from the Fire: that you say: There is no god but Allah, He gives life and death, and He is Living who does not die. Glory to the Lord of servants and lands. Praise to Allah, much, good, blessed praise in every state. Allah is Greatest; the pride, majesty and power of our Lord in every place. O Allah, if You made me ill to take my soul in this illness, place my soul among the souls of those for whom good has preceded from You. If you die in that illness of yours, then to the pleasure of Allah the Exalted and Paradise; and if you committed sins, Allah the Mighty and Majestic will turn to you."
"4041","Chapter 19 - Best supplication. 3372 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Awn narrated to us, Abu Ubaydah narrated, Umar narrated, he said: I heard al-Fadl ibn Thawr say: So-and-so narrated to me that the Prophet of Allah the Exalted (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing was said, and no one before said a word better than There is no god but Allah; and questioners did not ask their Lord for anything better than forgiveness."
"4042","Chapter 21 - Prohibition of supplicating against oneself and children. 3373 - Abu Ya'la said: Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated, Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not supplicate against your children, lest that coincide with an answering from Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"4043","Chapter 22 - What the traveler says. 3374 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, al-Muharibi narrated, from Umar ibn Musawir al-Ijli, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never intended a journey except that he said when rising from his sitting: O Allah, by You I spread, to You I turn, by You I hold fast. O Allah, You are my trust and my hope. O Allah, suffice me what concerns me and what I do not concern myself with and what You know better than me. Provide me piety, forgive me, and direct me to good wherever I turn."
"4044","3375 - Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Ala informed, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you intends travel, let him greet his brothers, for they add to his supplication by their supplication good."
"4045","3376 - Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad narrated to us, Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated, Umarah ibn Zadhan narrated, from Ziyad al-Numayri, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended a height of land, he would say: O Allah, to You is loftiness over every loftiness, and to You is praise in every state."
"4046","Chapter 23 - Beware the supplication of the oppressed. 3377 - Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Shayban, from Firas, from Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him), raised: Fear the supplication of the oppressed."
"4047","Chapter 24 - What to say when wind blows. 3378 /1 - Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Husayn ibn Qays narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When the wind blew, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would face it, kneel on his knees, then say: O Allah, make them winds and do not make it a wind; O Allah, make it mercy and do not make it punishment."
"4048","3378 /2 - Abu Ya'la said: Wahb narrated to us, Khalid narrated the like."
"4049","3379 - Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, from Rashidin ibn Kurayb, from his father, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from the evil the wind brings and the evil the messengers bring."
"4050","3380 - Abd said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wind is from the breath of Allah the Mighty and Majestic; when you see it, ask Allah the Exalted for its good and seek refuge with Allah from its evil."
"4051","3381 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, Yazid ibn Abi Ubayd narrated to me, he said: I heard Salamah ibn al-Akwa' (may Allah be pleased with him), raising it: When the wind was strong, he would say: O Allah, fertile and not barren."
"4052","Chapter 25 - What to say when beast escapes. 3382 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Umar narrated to us, Ma'ruf ibn Hassan narrated, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: When the beast of one of you escapes in a desert land, let him call: O servants of Allah, hold it; O servants of Allah, hold it; for Allah the Blessed and Exalted has one present in the land who will hold it."
"4053","Chapter 26 - Ending the gathering. 3383 - Abu Bakr said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abi Farwah, from Abi Ma'shar, a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us that he sat in a gathering, and when he wanted to rise said: Glory to You O Allah and with Your praise, I bear witness there is no god but You, I seek Your forgiveness and turn to You. A man of the people said: What is this, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Words Jibril (peace be upon him) taught me, expiations for the sins of the gathering. Its chain is sahih, and Abu Ma'shar is Kufan, his name Ziyad ibn Kulayb."
"4054","Chapter 27 - Praise. 3384 - Musaddad said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated, from Abi al-Ward, from Abi Muhammad al-Hadrami, from Abi Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man said before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Praise to Allah, much, good, blessed praise. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is the owner of the words? The man fell silent, thinking he had come upon something from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he disliked. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is he, for he said only what is right? The man said: I said it, O Messenger of Allah, hoping for good by it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, I saw thirteen angels racing for your words, which of them raises them to Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"4055","3385 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Thabit, from Uqbah ibn Abd al-Ghafir, Hammad said: I only know he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever says: Glory to your Lord, Lord of Might, above what they describe; peace on the messengers; and praise to Allah Lord of worlds - has measured with the fullest measure."
"4056","Chapter 28 - Virtue of remembrance. 3385 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, Abu al-Mughirah narrated to me, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me, al-Ahwas ibn Hakim ibn Umayr and Habib ibn Ubayd narrated to me, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let no man of you leave acting for Allah a thousand good deeds, that is glorifying a thousand times, for he will not do the like of that of sins in his day, and what he did of good besides that will remain abundant."
"4057","3386 /1 - Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Isra'il narrated, from Abi Yahya, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever of you is unable for the night to endure it, miserly with wealth to spend it, and cowardly before the enemy to fight him, let him increase remembrance of Allah the Exalted."
"4058","3386 /2 - Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, Ubaydullah narrated the same. He said: We know it only through this chain."
"4059","3387 /1 - Musaddad said: Bishr narrated to us, Umar ibn Abdullah freedman of Ghufrah narrated, he said: I heard Ayyub ibn Khalid ibn Safwan al-Ansari say: Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: O people, Allah the Exalted has troops of angels who alight and stand at gatherings of remembrance in the earth, so graze in the meadows of Paradise. They said: Where are the meadows of Paradise, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gatherings of remembrance. Go and return in remembrance of Allah the Exalted, and remind Him by yourselves. Whoever loves to know his station with Allah the Exalted, let him see how the station of Allah the Mighty and Majestic is with him, for Allah the Exalted places the servant where he placed Him in himself."
"4060","3387 /2 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated, from Umar ibn Abdullah the like."
"4061","Abd said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to me, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated, with the same report."
"4062","Abu Yala said: Ubaydullah — he is al-Qawariri — narrated us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated, with the same report."
"4063","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated us, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated, and he mentioned it. Al-Bazzar said: We do not know it narrated from Jabir except with this chain."
"4064","Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan — he is Ibn Muawiyah — narrated us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no spot on which Allah the Exalted is mentioned for prayer or remembrance except it rejoices at the remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic, to its furthest extent of seven earths, and except it boasts over the spots around it."
"4065","Abu Yala said: Zuhayr narrated us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated, Musa narrated with it, and he added: And there is no servant who stands in a desert land intending prayer except the earth is adorned for him."
"4066","Musaddad said: Al-Mutamir narrated us, my father narrated, from Qatadah, from al-Ala ibn Ziyad, who said: It reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hasten to the meadows of Paradise. They said: O Prophet of Allah, what are the meadows of Paradise? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Circles of remembrance."
"4067","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bahr narrated us, Adi ibn Abi Umarah narrated, Ziyad al-Numayri narrated, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Satan places his snout upon the heart of the son of Adam; if he remembers Allah, mighty and majestic, he withdraws, and if he forgets, he swallows his heart; that is the whispering withdrawer."
"4068","Musaddad said: Isa narrated us, Harun ibn Antarah narrated, from his father, who said: We entered upon Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and a man said to him: Which deed is best? He said: Remembrance of Allah; Allah is Greatest, three times. Then he said: No people sit in a house of the houses of Allah, blessed and exalted, studying the Book of Allah the Exalted and exchanging it among themselves, except the angels shade them with their wings, and except they are guests of Allah, blessed and exalted, until they rise."
"4069","Ishaq said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi informed us, saying: I heard Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrating from Abu Abdillah al-Qarraz, from Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveling at al-Duff from Jumdan, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) halted and said: O Muadh, where are the forerunners? I said: Some people have gone ahead and some have stayed behind. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where are the forerunners who are infatuated with the remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic; whoever loves to graze in the meadows of Paradise, let him increase in remembrance of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"4070","Ishaq ibn Sulayman said: And I heard Hariz ibn Uthman narrating from Abu Bahriyyah, from Muadh, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: No son of Adam does a deed more saving for him from the punishment of Allah, mighty and majestic, than remembrance of Allah the Exalted. They said: Not even striving in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic? He said: No, even if he struck with his sword. Allah says: And the remembrance of Allah is greater. I say: Ahmad narrated the last portion of this with a different, interrupted chain, and its basis is in al-Tirmidhi and others."
"4071","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from Yahya ibn Ubaydillah, who said: I heard my father saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gatherings are three: a gainer, a safe one, and a ruined one. The gainer is the one who increases the remembrance of Allah the Exalted in his gathering; the safe one is the one who is silent, neither against him nor for him; and the ruined one is the one whose speech and deed are in disobedience to Allah, blessed and exalted."
"4072","Yahya narrated us, from Musa al-Juhani, Mikhraq narrated, saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Gatherings are three: among them the gainer, among them the safe, and among them the ruined. The gainer is a servant who remembered Allah, mighty and majestic, so Allah the Exalted remembered him; the safe is a servant who did not dictate upon his recorder good or evil; and the ruined is the one who engaged in falsehood, so he incurs it upon himself."
"4073","Chapter: the excellence of remembrance after the dawn and afternoon prayers. Al-Tayalisi said: Muhammad ibn Mihzam narrated us, Yazid ibn Aban narrated, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That I sit with people remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, from the dawn prayer until sunrise is dearer to me than that over which the sun has risen; and that I remember Allah from the afternoon prayer until sunset is dearer to me than freeing eight of the children of Ismail, the blood-money of each one of them twelve thousand. So we calculated their blood-moneys while in a gathering and it reached ninety-six thousand; and here is one who says: four of the children of Ismail; by Allah, he said nothing but eight, the blood-money of each one of them twelve thousand."
"4074","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated us, Hammad narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That I sit with people remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, after the dawn prayer until the sun rises is dearer to me than freeing four of the children of Ismail; and that I remember Allah, mighty and majestic, after the afternoon until the sun sets is dearer to me than freeing eight slaves of the children of Ismail."
"4075","Al-Harith said: Abdillah ibn Awn narrated us, Abu Ubaydah narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it with the wording: That I pray the dawn prayer and sit with people remembering Allah the Exalted until sunrise is dearer to me than that over which the sun has risen; and that I pray the afternoon prayer and sit with people remembering Allah, mighty and majestic, until sunset is dearer to me than freeing eight slaves of the children of Ismail, the blood-money of each slave twelve thousand."
"4076","Abu Yala said: Khalid ibn al-Qasim and Abu al-Rabi — he separated them — narrated us, both saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated us, Yazid al-Raqashi narrated, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That I sit with people remembering Allah the Exalted from the morning until the sun rises is dearer to me than that over which the sun has risen."
"4077","Abu al-Rabi narrated us, Hammad narrated, al-Mualla ibn Ziyad narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That I sit with people remembering Allah the Exalted from the afternoon prayer until the sun sets is dearer to me than freeing eight of the children of Ismail."
"4078","And Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated us, Hammad narrated, with this, similar, and he added: all of them Muslim."
"4079","Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated us, al-Tayyib ibn Sulayman narrated, saying: I heard Amrah saying: I heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever prays the dawn prayer, or he said the morning prayer, then sits in his sitting place and does not engage in any worldly matter, and remembers Allah the Exalted until he prays four cycles of the forenoon prayer, he emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him, with no sin upon him."
"4080","Chapter: remembrance in prayer. Abu Yala said: Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad narrated us, Bishr ibn Mansur narrated, from al-Khalil ibn Murrah, from al-Furat ibn Salman, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Will not one of you stand and pray four cycles, saying in them what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Your light is perfected so You guided, so praise belongs to You; Your forbearance is great so You pardoned, so praise belongs to You; You extended Your hand so You gave, so praise belongs to You; Your face is the greatest of faces, Your majesty the greatest of majesties, Your gift the best and most wholesome of gifts; our Lord, You are obeyed so You thank, and You are disobeyed, our Lord, so You forgive; You answer the distressed, dispel grief, heal the sick, forgive sin, accept repentance; none can requite Your blessings, and no speaker's words can reach Your praise."
"4081","Chapter: remembrance in the morning and evening. Abd said: Abdillah ibn Bakr narrated us, Faid Abu al-Warqa narrated, from Abdillah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when we entered morning saying: We have entered morning and sovereignty belongs to Allah, and pride, and greatness, and creation, and the night and day, and what rests therein belongs to Allah alone with no partner; O Allah, make the beginning of this day righteousness, its middle success, and its end triumph, and I ask You the good of this world and the Hereafter."
"4082","Al-Harith said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated us, Maan Abu Abdillah narrated, a man narrated me, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were sitting with a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with him, when he was approached and told: Catch up with your house, it has burned. He said: My house has not burned. He went, then came, and was told: Catch up with your house, it has burned. He said: By Allah, my house has not burned. He was told: You are told your house has burned, yet you swear by Allah it has not burned? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever says in the morning: My Lord is Allah, there is no god but He, upon Him I rely, and He is Lord of the Mighty Throne; what Allah wills is, and what He does not will is not; there is no power nor strength except with Allah, the High, the Mighty; I bear witness that Allah is over all things Capable, and that Allah has encompassed all things in knowledge; I seek refuge with Allah who holds the sky lest it fall upon the earth except by His permission, from the evil of every creature whose forelock my Lord holds; indeed my Lord is upon a straight path — he will not see in that day in himself, nor his family, nor his wealth anything he dislikes; and I have said it today. I say: al-Tabarani narrated it in al-Dua from another route, naming the Companion Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him; and it is not permitted to interpret the obscure one here by him, for al-Hasan did not sit with Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4083","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Zanjawayh narrated us, Abu al-Mughirah narrated, Abu Bakr narrated, Damrah ibn Habib narrated me, from Abu al-Darda, from Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him a supplication and commanded him to entrust his family with it every day. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say in the morning: At Your service, O Allah, at Your service; at Your service and felicity, and good is in Your hands, from You, to You, and toward You; whatever word I spoke, deed I did, vow I vowed, or oath I swore, Your will precedes all of that; what You willed is, and what You did not will is not; there is no power nor strength except with You; indeed You are over all things Capable. O Allah, whatever prayer I prayed, it is upon whom You prayed, and whatever curse I invoked, it is upon whom You cursed; You are my guardian in this world and the Hereafter; cause me to die as a Muslim and join me with the righteous."
"4084","Abu al-Rabi narrated us, Yusuf ibn Atiyyah narrated, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate with these supplications in the morning and evening: O Allah, I ask You the sudden occurrences of good, and I seek refuge with You from the sudden occurrences of evil, for the servant does not know what will suddenly befall him in the morning and evening."
"4085","Musaddad said: al-Mutamir narrated us, saying: I heard Mansur narrating from Ribi ibn Hirash, from a man of al-Nakha, from his father, from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the servant says in the morning: O Allah, You are my Lord, You have no partner, and I have entered morning and sovereignty belongs to Allah, with no partner to Him — when the servant says it in the morning and evening, I expiate from him what he newly did between them, or he said: what he incurred between them."
"4086","Abu Awanah narrated us, Husayn narrated, from Abdillah ibn Sabrah, who said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when he entered morning would say: O Allah, make me of the greatest of Your servants in share of every good You apportion in the morning — of light You guide with, mercy You spread, provision You extend, harm You remove, affliction You repel, trial You avert, and evil You ward off."
"4087","Chapter: urging adherence to glorification. Ishaq said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid informed us, al-Hakam ibn Abdillah al-Ayli narrated, al-Zuhri narrated, saying: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a crow with full wings, and he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No game is hunted, no thorny tree is lopped, nor any branch cut, except due to scantiness of glorification. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, released the crow. This is problematic or sent; and al-Hakam is utterly weak. The hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, on the excellence of Glory be to Allah and with His praise, in censuring pride, is in the book of manners."
"4088","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from al-Masudi, from Abdillah ibn Mukhariq, from his father, who said: Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When I narrate you a hadith I inform you of its confirmation from the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic. He said: No servant says: Glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, there is no god but Allah, Allah is Greatest, and blessed is Allah, except Allah, mighty and majestic, appoints over them an angel who lays them beneath his wing and ascends with them to heaven; he does not pass any assembly of angels except they seek forgiveness for their speaker, until he greets with them the face of the Most Merciful, mighty and majestic. Then Abdillah recited: To Him ascends the good word, and righteous deed raises it. Al-Hakim transmitted it."
"4089","Yahya narrated us, Shubah narrated, Mansur narrated me, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: That I say: Glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest, is dearer to me than spending the like thereof in the path of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"4090","Ismail narrated us, al-Jurayri narrated, a man narrated me, saying: I said to a jurist in Makkah: We have a jurist — meaning al-Hasan — when he is silent his wont is: Glory be to Allah and with His praise, glory be to Allah the Mighty. He said: This companion of yours is indeed a jurist; no servant says it seven times except a house is built for him in Paradise."
"4091","Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated us, Salamah — he is Ibn Wardan — narrated, saying: I heard Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever declares unity one hundred times, magnifies one hundred times, and glorifies one hundred times, it is better than ten slaves he frees and seven sacrificial camels he slaughters."
"4092","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated us, Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated, Hizam ibn Ismail narrated, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Hakim, freedman of al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No morning dawns upon the servants except a caller cries: O creatures, glorify the King, the Most Holy."
"4093","Chapter: the excellence of hidden remembrance. Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated us, Ishaq narrated, Muawiyah narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hidden remembrance, which the guardian angels do not hear, is favored seventyfold. And he says: When the Day of Resurrection comes and Allah the Exalted gathers the creatures for their reckoning, and the guardians come with what they guarded or wrote, Allah the Exalted says to them: See, does anything of his remain? They say: Our Lord, we left nothing of what we knew and guarded except we enumerated and wrote it. Then Allah, blessed and exalted, says: You have with Me a hidden store you do not know, and I shall recompense you for it, and it is hidden remembrance."
"4094","Chapter: the greatness of the remembrance of Allah the Exalted. Abu Bakr said: al-Hasan narrated us, Yahya narrated, from Abu Harun, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you strikes his servant and he mentions Allah, mighty and majestic, lift your hands."
"4095","Chapter: magnifying Allah. Abu Yala said: Abdan narrated us, Abu al-Nadr narrated, from Kathir, Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated, from his father, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see a fire, magnify Allah. This is sent, fair."
"4096","Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from Anbasah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Zadham, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a grave matter occurs or a dark wind rages, upon you is magnifying Allah, for it clears away the black dust."
"4097","Chapter: the regret of one who disperses without remembrance. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yusuf ibn Atiyyah narrated us, from al-Ala ibn Kathir, from Makhul, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any people who sit in a gathering then disperse before remembering Allah the Exalted and sending blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that gathering will be regret upon them on the Day of Resurrection."
"4098","Abu Yala said: al-Qawariri narrated us, Yusuf ibn Yazid Abu Mashar al-Barra narrated, Shaddad ibn Said narrated, from Abu al-Wazi Jabir ibn Amr, from Abdillah ibn Mughaffal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No people ever sit in a gathering and do not remember Allah the Exalted except it is regret upon them on the Day of Resurrection."
"4099","Chapter: seeking refuge. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad — he is Ibn Salamah — narrated, from Mabad, a certain man in the mosque of Damascus informed me, from Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the hadith, saying: Seek refuge with Allah from the devils of men and jinn. And he mentioned like a hadith before it, its text: I said: O Messenger of Allah, are there devils among men? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. I say: al-Nasai narrated it from the hadith of Ubaydillah ibn Abi al-Khashkhash, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him; and this, if Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, witnessed the story, is of his musnad."
"4100","Al-Tayalisi said: Shubah narrated us, from Yala ibn Ata, who said: I heard Abu Alqamah; Shubah said: And Yunus ibn Khabbab narrated me that he heard Abu Alqamah narrating from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — and Yala did not raise it to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — who said: Whoever says: I ask Allah Paradise seven times, Paradise says: O Allah, admit him to Paradise; and whoever seeks refuge from the Fire seven times, the Fire says: O Allah, protect him from the Fire."
"4101","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated us, Jarir narrated, from Yunus — he is Ibn Khabbab — from Abu Alqamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, similar to it."
"4102","Al-Bazzar said: Yusuf ibn Musa narrated us, Jarir narrated, from Layth, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, and he mentioned it."
"4103","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from Abu Hayyan, Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir narrated me, saying: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to me, then told me face to face afterward, saying: Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated us that he said of what he says from the Torah, we find it written: Satan cannot bear a servant from evening until morning saying: O Allah, I seek refuge with Your name and Your perfect word from evil in the poisonous and the common, and I ask You by Your name and Your perfect word for the best of what is asked, the best of what is given, the best of what is hidden, and the best of what is manifest; O Allah, I ask You by Your name and Your perfect word against the evil with which the day is unveiled, if it is day; and if it is night, he said: against the evil with which the night darkens. And the hadith of Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, is at the beginning of the reports of the Prophets, peace be upon them."
"4104","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Ismail narrated us, Ayyub informed, from Muhammad, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I bear witness that Allah is true, His meeting is true, the Hour is true, Paradise is true, and the Fire is true; O Allah, I seek refuge with You from the trial of life and death, from the punishment of the grave, and from the punishment of Hell. Suspended, sound."
"4105","Al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated us, Abu Mashar narrated, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from deafness and muteness, I seek refuge with You from sin and debt, and I seek refuge with You from death by collapse — the hadith."
"4106","Al-Bazzar said: Amr — he is Ibn Ali — narrated us, Jabir ibn Ishaq narrated, Abu Mashar narrated, and he mentioned similar and added: And I seek refuge with You from drowning — meaning submersion — and I seek refuge with You from grief. And he said: We know it only with this chain."
"4107","Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai narrated us, from al-Muharibi, from Layth, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever seeks refuge with Allah the Exalted ten times in the day from Satan, Allah the Exalted appoints over him an angel repelling Satan from him."
"4108","Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated us, Rawh ibn Aslam and Fahd narrated us, both saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated, Layth narrated, from Abu Muhammad, from Maqil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Association is among you more hidden than the creeping of the ant — the hadith. And it has preceded with its routes in the chapter warning against ostentation in the book of softeners of hearts."
"4109","Chapter: the excellence of there is no power nor strength except with Allah. Ishaq said: al-Nadr informed us, Hammad narrated, Mabad narrated, a certain man informed me, from Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the hadith like a hadith before it, in which, then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I guide you to a treasure of the treasures of Paradise? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: There is no power nor strength except with Allah."
"4110","Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, from Kathir ibn Zayd al-Madani, al-Muttalib ibn Abdillah ibn Hantab narrated me, from Amir ibn Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I met Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: Shall I command you with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me: to increase in there is no power nor strength except with Allah, for it is of the treasures of Paradise. Its chain is fair."
"4111","Abd said: Abu Nuaym narrated us, Abdillah ibn Amir al-Aslami narrated, from Abu al-Zinad, from Said ibn Sulayman, from Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Shall I guide you to treasures of the treasure of Paradise? Increase in saying: There is no power nor strength except with Allah."
"4112","Abu Yala said: Hudhayl narrated us, Salih ibn Bayan al-Sahili narrated, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day and I said: There is no power nor strength except with Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know its meaning? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No averting from disobedience to Allah except by Allah's protection, and no strength for obedience to Allah except by Allah's strength; thus Jibril, peace and blessings be upon him, informed me."
"4113","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Waqid narrated us, Hafs ibn Abdillah al-Ifriqi narrated, Hakim ibn Nafi narrated, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no power nor strength except with Allah is of the treasures of the Throne."
"4114","Book of the beginning of creation. Ishaq said: Sufyan informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yazid ibn Judubah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mikhraq, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah the Exalted created in Paradise a wind after wind by seven years, without it a closed door; the wind comes to you only through the gaps of that door; were that door opened, it would scatter what is between heaven and earth; with Allah, mighty and majestic, it is al-Azyab, and with you it is the south wind."
"4115","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated us, with it. I say: Yazid ibn Judubah is Ibn Iyad; abandoned."
"4116","Ishaq said: Abu Muawiyah informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Nasr, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: What is between the lowest heaven and the earth is a journey of five hundred years, the thickness of each of them is a journey of five hundred years, what is between each heaven and the one following it is a journey of five hundred years, to the seventh heaven; the earths are like that; and what is between the seventh heaven and the Throne is like all of that."
"4117","Hakkam ibn Salm al-Razi informed us, Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated, from al-Rabi ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The heavens: the first is restrained wave, the second of rock, the third of iron, the fourth of copper, the fifth of silver, the sixth of gold, and the seventh of ruby."
"4118","A report from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Sad al-Tai, who said: I was told that the Throne is a red ruby. The hadith: Allah created creation and decreed the decree — is in the book of faith, chapter of decree."
"4119","Chapter: what suits the days of the week. Abu Yala said: Amr ibn al-Husayn narrated us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated, Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Sunday is a day of planting and building, Monday a day of travel, Tuesday a day of blood, Wednesday a day of taking with no giving in it, Thursday a day of entering upon the ruler, Friday a day of marriage and intercourse, and Saturday —."
"4120","Chapter: the creation of the earth. Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I said to Kab: What holds this earth we are upon? He said: The command of Allah the Exalted. He said: I said: I know it is the command of Allah, mighty and majestic, that holds it; what is that command of Allah? He said: A green tree in the hand of an angel of the angels standing upon the back of the whale, coiled, and the heavens are beneath the Throne. I said: What inhabits the second earth? He said: The barren wind; when He intended to destroy Ad, He revealed to its keepers to open a door of it. They said: Our Lord, like the nostril of a bull? He said: Then it would hurl the earth and those upon it. So it was made like the place of a ring. He said: I said: Who inhabits the third earth? He said: The stones of Hell. He said: I said: Who inhabits the fourth earth? He said: The brimstone of Hell. I said: Does it indeed have brimstone? He said: Yes, by Him in whose hand is my soul, and seas into which if mountains were cast they would crumble from their heat. He said: I said: Who inhabits the fifth earth? He said: The snakes of Hell. I said: Does it indeed have snakes? He said: Yes, by Him in whose hand is my soul, like valleys. I said: Who inhabits the sixth earth? He said: The scorpions of Hell. I said: Does it indeed have scorpions? He said: Yes, by Him in whose hand is my soul, like wheels, with tails like lances; one of them meets the disbeliever and stings him a sting so his flesh scatters upon his feet. He said: I said: What inhabits the seventh earth? He said: That is Sijjin, in it Iblis is bound; the angels sought aid against him, so Allah the Exalted confined him therein, a hand before him and a hand behind him, a leg before him and a leg behind him, and his troops come to him with news, and he has a time in which he is released."
"4121","Chapter: souls. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from Shubah, Abu Ishaq narrated me, from Amr ibn Murrah, who said: Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Souls are grouped troops; what recognized one another united, and what disavowed one another differed. Suspended, sound."
"4122","Chapter: the angels, peace be upon them. Abu Yala said: Amr al-Naqid narrated us, Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated, Israil narrated, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was permitted to speak of an angel whose feet have pierced the seventh earth, with the Throne upon his shoulder, saying: Glory be to You, where were You and where will You be. Sound."
"4123","Chapter: the jinn. Abu Yala said: Husayn ibn al-Aswad narrated us, Abu Usamah narrated, Yazid ibn Sinan Abu Farwah al-Ruhawi narrated, Abu Munib al-Himsi narrated me, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted created the jinn in three kinds: a kind snakes, scorpions, and vermin of the earth; a kind like wind in the air; and a kind upon whom is reckoning and punishment. And Allah, mighty and majestic, created men in three kinds: a kind like beasts — Allah, mighty and majestic, says: They have hearts wherewith they understand not — the verse; a kind whose bodies are the bodies of the sons of Adam and whose spirits are the spirits of devils; and a kind in the shade of Allah the Exalted on a day when there is no shade but His shade."
"4124","Ahmad ibn Isa narrated us, Ibn Wahb narrated, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted created the jinn in three thirds: a third with wings flying in the air, a third snakes and dogs, and a third settling and departing."
"4125","Chapter: the veils before Allah the Exalted. Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Zimmani narrated us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, from Umar ibn al-Hakam, from Abdillah ibn Amr; and from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Before Allah, blessed and exalted, are seventy thousand veils of light and darkness; no soul hears anything of the sense of those veils except its soul departs."
"4126","Book of the reports of the Prophets, peace and blessings be upon them. Muhammad ibn Abi Umar said: Hisham ibn Sulayman narrated us, Abu Rafi narrated, from Yazid ibn Ruman, from one who informed him, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I entered the mosque and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting alone, so I stood looking at him while he did not see me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not alone like this except for a need or revelation, so I debated with myself whether to come to him, but my soul refused except to come to him, so I came, greeted, then sat; I sat long while he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) neither turned to me nor spoke to me. He said: I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has disliked my sitting. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me and said: O Abu Dharr. I said: At your service and felicity. He said: Have you bowed today? I said: No. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand and bow. So I stood and bowed what Allah the Exalted willed, then returned and sat; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained long not speaking to me, and I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has disliked my sitting. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me and said: O Abu Dharr. I said: At your service and felicity. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek refuge with Allah from the evil of the devils of men and jinn. I said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, are there devils among men? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does not Allah, mighty and majestic, say: The devils of men and jinn, inspiring one another — the verse. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me and said: O Abu Dharr. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I teach you a word that is a treasure of the treasures of Paradise? I said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransom for you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Say: There is no power nor strength except with Allah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) persisted without speaking until that was long from him, so I began speech and said: O Messenger of Allah, you commanded me with prayer, what is prayer? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Good placed; whoever wills, let him do little, whoever wills, let him do much. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is fasting? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An obligatory, discharging duty. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is charity? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Multiples multiplied, and with Allah is increase. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which deed is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Faith in Allah, mighty and majestic, and striving in the path of Allah the Exalted. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which martyrs are best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He whose blood is shed and whose horse is hamstrung. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which slaves are best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dearest in price and most precious to their owners. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which charity is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Effort of one of little means, and a secret to a poor one. I said: O Messenger of Allah, if I find nothing to give? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You aid a weak one, or make for one lacking skill. I said: O Messenger of Allah, if I cannot? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You withhold this — and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed to his tongue — for it is a fine charity a man gives upon himself. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of what was sent upon you of the Quran is greatest? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The verse of the Throne; and do you know what is the likeness of the heavens and earth in the Footstool? I said: No, except you teach me of what Allah the Exalted taught you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of the heavens and earth in the Footstool is but as a ring cast in a desert; the excellence of the Footstool over the heavens and earth is as the excellence of the desert over that ring. I said: O Messenger of Allah, how many were the Prophets, peace be upon them? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They were one hundred twenty-four thousand. I said: O Messenger of Allah, were all of them messengers? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, the messengers among them were three hundred fifteen men. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of them was first? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Adam, peace be upon him. I said: And was Adam a prophet? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, Allah shaped his clay, created him with His hand, breathed into him of His spirit, and spoke to him face to face. Then people crowded around the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I inform you of the most miserly of people? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One before whom I am mentioned and he does not send blessings upon me."
"4127","Chapter: Adam and the number of the Prophets, peace and blessings be upon them. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad — he is Ibn Salamah — informed, Mabad informed, a certain man in the mosque of Damascus informed me, from Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned like a hadith before it, in which: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of the Prophets was first? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Adam. I said: And was he a prophet? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, a prophet addressed. I said: O Messenger of Allah, how many are the Prophets? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three hundred fifteen, a great multitude."
"4128","Abu Haywah al-Himsi Shurayh informed us, Maan ibn Rifaa al-Sulami narrated, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman — freedman of Yazid ibn Muawiyah al-Shami — from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): How many are the Prophets? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One hundred twenty-four thousand. He said: How many of them are messengers? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three hundred fifteen, a great multitude."
"4129","Abu Bakr said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated us, from al-Masudi, from Ubayd ibn al-Khashkhash, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, similar to it."
"4130","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq Abu Abdillah al-Jawhari narrated us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah sent eight thousand prophets: four thousand to the Children of Israil and four thousand to the rest of mankind."
"4131","Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated us, Muhammad ibn Thabit al-Abdi narrated, Mabad ibn Khalid al-Ansari narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There were of my passed brothers of the Prophets, peace and blessings be upon them, eight thousand prophets, then there was Isa son of Maryam, peace and blessings be upon them both, then I was."
"4132","Chapter: the life of the Prophets, peace and blessings be upon them. Abu Yala said: Abu al-Jahm al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated."
"4133","Al-Bazzar said: Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated us, al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated — both saying: al-Mustalim ibn Said narrated, from al-Hajjaj, from Thabit, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Prophets are alive in their graves, praying. Al-Bazzar said: We know none narrated it from Thabit except Hajjaj, nor from Hajjaj except al-Mustalim; and al-Hajjaj narrated from Thabit only this."
"4134","And he transmitted it from Muhammad, from Abd al-Rahman al-Harrani, from al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah, from Hammad, from Abd al-Aziz, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: We know none who followed al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah in his narration of it from Hammad."
"4135","Chapter: the creation of Adam, peace and blessings be upon him. Abu Yala said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated us, Umar ibn Muhammad narrated, from Ismail ibn Rafi, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah, blessed and exalted, created Adam from dust, then made him clay, then left him until he was black mud, then created and formed him, then left him until he was dry clay like pottery; Iblis would pass by him saying: You have been created for a great matter. Then Allah the Exalted breathed into him of His spirit; the first thing the spirit reached was his sight and nostrils, so he sneezed, and Allah, blessed and exalted, taught him the praise of his Lord, so the Lord, mighty and majestic, said: May your Lord have mercy on you. Then Allah the Exalted said: O Adam, go to those group and say to them, and see what they say. So he came and greeted them, and they said: And upon you peace and the mercy of Allah. Then he came to his Lord, mighty and majestic, and He, majestic and high, said: What did they say to you — while He knows best what they said? He said: My Lord, I greeted them and they said: And upon you peace and the mercy of Allah. He, blessed and exalted, said: O Adam, this is your greeting and the greeting of your offspring. He said: My Lord, what is my offspring? He, majestic and high, said: Choose one of My two hands, O Adam. He, peace and blessings be upon him, said: I choose the right of my Lord, and both hands of my Lord are right; so Allah the Exalted extended His palm, and all that is to be in his offspring was in the palm of the Most Merciful, blessed and exalted."
"4136","Chapter: Salih and Thamud, peace and blessings be upon them both. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated us, Jafar ibn Burqan narrated, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by al-Hijr of the valley of Thamud and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Hasten the march, and do not alight in this village whose people were destroyed."
"4137","Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated us, Mubarak narrated, from al-Hasan, saying: I heard him saying: Abdillah ibn Qudamah narrated me, from al-Sadi — and al-Sadi was a truthful man. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon the valley of Thamud and said to his Companions: Go out, go out, for it is an accursed evil valley; if you do not go out, such-and-such will befall you."
"4138","Chapter: Ayyub, peace and blessings be upon him. Abu Yala said: Humayd ibn al-Rabi narrated us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated."
"4139","Al-Bazzar said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, Muhammad ibn Miskin, and Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Askar narrated us, saying: Said narrated, Nafi ibn Yazid narrated, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ayyub, the Prophet of Allah, peace be upon him, was in his affliction eighteen years, so near and far rejected him except two men of his brothers — al-Bazzar added: they were of his most special brothers — who would come to him morning and evening. One of them said to his companion: Know, by Allah, Ayyub has committed a sin none committed. His companion said to him: What is that? He said: For eighteen years Allah the Exalted has not had mercy on him, so He has not removed what is with him. When they went to him in the evening, the man could not restrain until he mentioned that to him. So Ayyub, peace be upon him, said: I do not know what you say, save that Allah, blessed and exalted, knows I would pass by two men disputing, and they would mention Allah the Exalted, so I would return to my house and expiate for them, lest Allah the Exalted be mentioned except in truth. He said: He, peace and blessings be upon him, would go out for his need, and when he relieved his need his wife would hold his hand until he reached. One day he was late for her, and it was revealed to Ayyub, peace be upon him, in his place: Strike with your foot; this is a cool washing and drink. She awaited him, looked around, and he came to her with Allah, blessed and exalted, having removed his affliction, in the finest state; when she saw him she said: May Allah bless you, have you seen this afflicted Prophet of Allah? By Allah, I have seen none resembling him since he was sound like you. He, peace be upon him, said: I am he. He had two threshing floors: a threshing floor of wheat and a threshing floor of barley; so Allah the Exalted sent two clouds, and when one was over the threshing floor of wheat it poured gold into it until it overflowed, and the other poured silver upon the threshing floor of barley until it overflowed. Al-Bazzar said: We know none narrated it from al-Zuhri, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, except Uqayl, nor from him except Nafi. Ibn Hibban declared it sound from the route of Ibn Wahb, from Nafi ibn Yazid."
"4140","Chapter: Yaqub and Yusuf, peace and blessings be upon them both. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shubah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, who said: Abu al-Ahwas said, from Abdillah: Yusuf and his mother were given a third of beauty. This is a sound chain, suspended."
"4141","Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham al-Rifai Muhammad ibn Yazid narrated us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, from Yunus ibn Amr, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he honored him and said to him: Come to us. So he came to him and he said: Ask your need? He said: A she-camel to ride, and goats his family milks. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were you unable to be like the old woman of the Children of Israil? They asked him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Musa, peace and blessings be upon him, when he journeyed with the Children of Israil from Egypt, they lost the way. He said: What is this? Their scholars said: Yusuf, peace and blessings be upon him, when death approached took a covenant with us from Allah the Exalted not to leave Egypt until we carry his bones with us. He said: Who knows the place of his grave? He said: An old woman of the Children of Israil; so he sent to her and she came to him and he said: Show me the grave of Yusuf. She said: Until you give me my judgment. He said: What is your judgment? She said: To be with you in Paradise; he disliked giving her that, so Allah the Exalted revealed to him to give her her judgment. She set out with them to a lake — a place of stagnant water — and said: Drain this water; so they drained it. She said: Dig; so they dug and extracted the bones of Yusuf, peace be upon him; when they lifted them to the ground, there was a road like daylight. Ibn Hibban declared it sound."
"4142","Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan — he is Ibn Muawiyah — narrated us, Yahya ibn Humayd narrated, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it, saying: A man said to Yaqub, peace be upon him: What took your sight and bent your back? He said: As for what took my sight, weeping over Yusuf; and as for what bent my back, grief over his brother Binyamin. He said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him and said: O Yaqub, do you complain of Allah the Exalted? He said: I complain only my anguish and grief to Allah. Jibril, peace and blessings be upon him, said to him: Allah knows what you said better than you; then Jibril, peace be upon him, departed, and Yaqub entered his house and said: My Lord, You took my sight and bent my back, so return to me my two sweet basils that I may smell them a smell, then do with me after that what You will. So Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him and said: O Yaqub, Allah, blessed and exalted, greets you and says: Rejoice, for had they been dead I would have revived them for you and delighted your eyes with them; and He says to you: O Yaqub, do you know why I took your sight and bent your back, and why the brothers of Yusuf did what they did? He said: Because there came to you a destitute orphan, fasting, hungry, while you and your family had slaughtered a sheep and ate it and did not feed him. And He says: I love nothing of My creation as I love orphans and the destitute. Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yaqub, peace and blessings be upon him, whenever evening came would call his caller: Whoever is fasting, let him attend the food of Yaqub; and when morning came would call his caller: Whoever is breaking fast, let him attend the food of Yaqub."
"4143","Chapter: reports of Musa and Harun, peace and blessings be upon them both. Ishaq said: Abu Muawiyah informed us, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdillah ibn Masud, from Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When Allah the Exalted sent Musa, peace and blessings be upon him, to Pharaoh, he said: What shall I say? He said: Say: Ahya shar ahya. Al-Amash said: So they explained it: The Living before all things, and the Living after all things."
"4144","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated us, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated, from al-Hakam, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying, mighty and majestic: Be not like those who harmed Musa, so Allah cleared him. He said: Musa and Harun ascended the mountain and Harun died; the Children of Israil said: You killed him, for he was more beloved to us than you and gentler to us than you; so they harmed him thereby. So Allah the Exalted commanded the angels who carried him until they passed over the Children of Israil; the angels, peace be upon them, spoke of his death until the Children of Israil knew he had died; so they set out with him and buried him; and none of Allah's creation saw his grave except the vultures; so Allah, mighty and majestic, made it deaf and dumb. This is a sound chain."
"4145","Abd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam narrated us, my father narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of the companions of Musa, peace and blessings be upon him, who crossed the sea were twelve tribes; in each path were twelve thousand, all of them of the offspring of Yaqub, peace and blessings be upon him. And there will come, Allah willing, something of this in the interpretation of al-Baqarah."
"4146","Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated us, Sallam narrated, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The sea was split for the Children of Israil on the day of Ashura."
"4147","Chapter: mention of Dawud, peace and blessings be upon him. Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, had a house near the mosque, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked him: Give it to me — or sell it to me — that I may include it in the mosque; he refused. And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Set between me and you a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so he set Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, who judged against Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. So Umar, may Allah's pleasure be upon him, said: You are of the boldest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Or of the sincerest to you, O Commander of the Faithful. Then he said: If you knew that Dawud, peace be upon him, was commanded to build the Sacred House, he included houses without the permission of their owners; when the building reached men's waists he was prevented from building it. He said: My Lord, then in my progeny after me."
"4148","Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed, Zayd ibn Aslam informed, with this hadith similar, and in it he said: Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When He intended — the hadith."
"4149","Musaddad said: Khalid narrated us, al-Jurayri narrated me, from Abu Attaf, who said: Dawud, peace and blessings be upon him, when he brought the vessel near his mouth to drink and remembered his sin, would weep until the vessel overflowed from his tears."
"4150","Chapter: Uzayr. Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Kurayb, from Kurayb, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not know whether Uzayr was a prophet or not."
"4151","Chapter: mention of Isa. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed, Hammad — he is Ibn Salamah — narrated, Amr ibn Dinar informed, from Yahya ibn Jadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatimah: No prophet ever lived long except the prophet after him lived half the age of his companion; and Isa lived forty, and I twenty. I say: Its meaning is he lived in prophethood."
"4152","Chapter: the story of Kursuf. Abu Yala said: Abu Talib Abd al-Jabbar ibn Asim narrated us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated, from Muawiyah ibn Yahya, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Makhul, from Ghudayf ibn al-Harith, from Atiyyah ibn Busr al-Mazini, who said: Akkaf ibn Wadaah al-Hilali came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Akkaf, have you a wife? He said: No — the hadith; and in it: They are the companions of Yusuf, Dawud, and Kursuf. He said: What is Kursuf, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man in the Children of Israil on a seashore, fasting the day, standing the night, not flagging from prayer and fasting; then he disbelieved afterward in Allah the Mighty due to a woman he loved, abandoning what he was upon of worship of his Lord; so Allah the Exalted retrieved him with what preceded from him and turned to him."
"4153","Chapter: al-Khadir and al-Yasa, peace be upon them both. Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated us, al-Qasim ibn Bahram narrated, Aban narrated, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Al-Khadir, peace be upon him, is in the sea, and al-Yasa, peace be upon him, is on land; they meet every night at the dam built by Dhu al-Qarnayn between people and Yajuj and Majuj, and they perform hajj and umrah every year, drinking from Zamzam a draught sufficing them till the next year. Very weak."
"4154","Chapter: what was among the Children of Israil. Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, Shubah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from his father Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There was among the Children of Israil, or among some kings, a man who said: I know none today mightier than me. So Allah the Exalted sent to him the weakest of His creation, which entered his nostril; he kept saying: Strike, strike; so they struck his head with axes until they smashed his head."
"4155","al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Wail Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man killed ninety-nine souls, then intended repentance, so he came to a monk in a barren land and said: O monk, I killed ninety-nine souls; is there repentance for me? He said: No. He said: Inevitably, by Allah I shall complete them with you as a hundred. Then he came to another monk, saying: I killed ninety-nine souls and completed them as a hundred with a monk; is there repentance for me? He said: You have exceeded upon yourself and embarked upon enormity; whoever repents, Allah turns to him. He said: He cast the sword and said: By Allah I shall serve you until death parts us. He said: He covenanted with him not to disobey him. He said: People came, traveling or in drought, and he practiced medicine; so the man said: Do you command me with anything? He said: Go heat the oven. He said: He went and heated it until it grew hot. He said: It is hot; what do you command me? He said: Go fall into it. He said: He went and fell into it; then the monk remembered, so he rose with those with him, and there he was in the oven sweating, the Fire not harming him. So the monk said: I knew your repentance has been accepted; I shall serve you ever till you leave me. Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Children of Israil, when one of them sinned, would awake with the expiation of his sin written upon his door threshold; so Allah the Exalted favored you over them, commanding you with seeking forgiveness, so you seek forgiveness of Allah the Exalted. He said: And He gave this nation a verse I would not love them to have the world and what is in it in exchange: And those who, when they commit indecency or wrong themselves, remember Allah and seek forgiveness for their sins — the verse. Its chain is sound, and it has a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4156","Jarir informed us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abdillah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There was a man of those before you among a disbelieving folk, with a righteous folk between them; the man said: Long I was in my disbelief, so I shall come to this righteous village and be a man of them; so he set out, his term overtook him on the road, and the angel and devil disputed; this said: I am more entitled, this said: I am more entitled; so Allah, blessed and exalted, appointed for them one of His troops, saying: Measure what is between the two villages; to whichever it is nearer, he is of it. So they measured between them and found him nearer to the righteous village, so he was of them. This is a sound chain; it has a witness in the Sahih."
"4157","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated us, from al-Rabi ibn Hassan al-Jufi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Narrate from the Children of Israil without harm, for among them were wonders. This is sent."
"4158","Ishaq said: Abu Dawud al-Hafari Umar ibn Sad informed us, Sufyan narrated, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Zara, from Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A monk worshipped Allah the Exalted in his cell sixty years; a woman descended beside him, so he descended to her and was with her six nights; then he regretted, fled, came to a mosque and stayed in it three days without eating; then a loaf was brought, so he broke it in two, giving a destitute on his right its half and another on his left its half; then Allah the Exalted took him; the sixty years were weighed in a scale and the six nights in a scale, so the six outweighed; then the six were weighed with the loaf, so the loaf outweighed."
"4159","al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Wail, from Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man worshipped sixty years. And he mentioned similar. This is a sound chain, suspended."
"4160","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid narrated us, Warqa narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Five of the Children of Israil would enter the split of a pomegranate."
"4161","Ishaq said: Abu al-Walid Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik informed us, Shubah narrated, Abu Ishaq informed us, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A prophet supplicated against his nation, and it was said to him: Do you love that I loose hunger upon them? He said: No. It was said to him: Do you love that I cast their might between them? He said: No. He said: So plague was loosed upon them, a fine death, burning hearts and reducing number."
"4162","Book of the excellences of the Quran. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi informed me, from Yuhannas Abu Musa, from Rashid ibn Sad, brother of Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites a hundred verses in a night is not written among the heedless; whoever recites two hundred verses is written among the devout; and whoever recites from five hundred to a thousand verses, there dawns for him a qintar of reward, a qirat of it like a great mound. This is a weak chain."
"4163","Abu Bakr and Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki narrated us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from a man called Yuhannas, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with them, raising it: Whoever recites in a night five hundred verses to a thousand, there dawns for him a qintar — the hadith."
"4164","Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, with it."
"4165","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated us, Waki narrated, with it."
"4166","Al-Harith said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated us, Hammad narrated, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites a hundred verses in a night, the Quran will not dispute him; whoever recites two hundred, Allah the Exalted writes for him the devotion of a night; whoever recites from a hundred to a thousand, there dawns for him a qintar, and the qintar is the blood-money of one of you, twelve thousand. He said: The smallest of houses in good is the house in which the Quran is not recited."
"4167","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Marwan Abu Sakhr narrated us, Bakr ibn Yunus narrated, from Musa ibn Ulayy, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites a thousand verses, Allah the Exalted writes for him a qintar; the qintar is a hundred ratls, the ratl twelve ounces, the ounce six dinars, the dinar twenty-four qirats, and the qirat like Uhud. Whoever recites three hundred verses, Allah the Exalted says to His angels: O My angels, I have appointed My servant; I call you to witness, O My angels, that I have forgiven him. Whoever is conveyed an excellence from Allah, mighty and majestic, and acts upon it in faith hoping its reward, Allah gives him that, even if it is not so."
"4168","Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, Muhammad ibn Kab al-Qurazi narrated, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashjai, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites a letter of the Book of Allah the Exalted, Allah the Exalted writes for him thereby a good deed; I do not say: Alif Lam Mim is a letter, but the letters are separate: alif, lam, mim."
"4169","Al-Bazzar narrated it, saying: Ahmad ibn Aban narrated us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, with it."
"4170","Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated me, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, I have left among you what if you hold to it you will never stray: the Book of Allah — the hadith."
"4171","Abd said: Abu Bakr narrated us, with it. And it has preceded in the chapter of the sanctity of Makkah."
"4172","Abu Yala said: Sufyan — he is Ibn Waki — narrated us, my father narrated, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd, from Abu al-Malih, saying: Maqil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Act upon the Quran, declare its lawful lawful, declare its unlawful unlawful, follow it, disbelieve not in anything of it; what is obscure to you, refer it to Allah, mighty and majestic, and to those of knowledge after me that they may inform you; believe in the Torah and Gospel, reject not what the prophets were given from their Lord; let the Quran and what is in it of exposition suffice you, for it is an intercessor granted intercession, and an adversary believed; in every verse of it is a light on the Day of Resurrection; I was given the chapter al-Baqarah of the first remembrance, given Ta Ha and the Tawasin of the tablets of Musa, given the Opening of the Book and the seals of the chapter al-Baqarah from a treasure beneath the Throne, and given the Mufassal as supererogation."
"4173","Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated us, Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated, Abdillah ibn Buraydah narrated, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him saying: The Quran will meet its companion on the Day of Resurrection when his grave splits, like a pale man, saying: Do you know me? He says to him: I do not know you. He says: I am your companion the Quran, who made you thirst in midday heats and kept your night wakeful; every trader is behind his trade, and today you are behind every trader. He said: He is given dominion in his right and eternity in his left, a crown of dignity is placed upon his head, and his parents are clothed two garments unrivaled by the people of the world, so they say: How did we earn this? It is said: By your child's taking the Quran. Then it is said: Recite and ascend in the degrees of Paradise and its chambers; he remains ascending as long as he used to recite, fast or measured. This is a fair chain; Ibn Majah narrated from its beginning to his words: kept your night wakeful, fair."
"4174","Abu Yala said: Abu Hammam narrated us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Haywah informed me, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Salamah ibn Abi Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The first Book would descend from a single door upon a single mode; and the Quran was sent from seven doors upon seven modes: restraining, commanding, lawful and unlawful, decisive and ambiguous, and similitudes. So believe its lawful and declare its unlawful unlawful, do what you are commanded, refrain from what you are forbidden, take lesson from its similitudes, act upon its decisive, believe its ambiguous, and say: We believe in it, all is from our Lord."
"4175","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Ismail al-Bukhari narrated us, Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated, Ibn Abi Uways — meaning Abu Bakr — narrated, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran was sent upon seven modes; for each mode thereof is an outward and inward. And he said: None narrated it from al-Hajari except Ibn Ajlan, nor narrated it thus except al-Hajari."
"4176","Abu Yala said: Sahl ibn Zanjalah al-Razi narrated us, Ibn Abi Uways narrated, from his brother, from Sulayman, with it."
"4177","Al-Harith said: Hawdhah narrated us, Awf narrated, saying: It reached me that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said upon the pulpit: I adjure by Allah a man who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The Quran was sent upon seven modes, all healing, sufficing — that he stand. So they stood till they could not be counted, bearing witness to that; so Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And I bear witness with you, thrice; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying that."
"4178","Abu Yala said: Musa narrated us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated, Awf narrated, from Abu al-Minhal, saying: It reached us that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said one day upon the pulpit: I adjure by Allah, mighty and majestic, a man who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and he mentioned it."
"4179","Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from his brother Abdillah ibn Ubaydah, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were reciting the Quran, some teaching others, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Praise belongs to Allah; the Book of Allah is one; among you are the righteous, the red and the black; recite the Quran before there come a people reciting it, straightening the letters of the Quran as an arrow is straightened, not passing their throats, hastening its reward and not deferring it."
"4180","Chapter: when the Quran descended. Abu Yala said: Sufyan ibn Waki narrated us, my father narrated, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd, from Abu al-Malih, from Jabir ibn Abdillah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Allah the Exalted sent the scrolls of Ibrahim in the first night of Ramadan, sent the Torah upon Musa six nights gone of Ramadan, sent the Psalms upon Dawud twelve nights gone of Ramadan, and the Furqan was sent upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on twenty-four nights gone of Ramadan. I say: This is inverted; it is rather from Wathilah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4181","Chapter: writing the codex. Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Abu Wail al-Qass al-Muradi al-Sanani narrated, saying: I heard Hani al-Barbari, freedman of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was writing the codices, they doubted three verses, so they wrote them upon a sheep's shoulder and sent me to Ubayy ibn Kab and Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them both; I entered upon them and handed it to Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him, so he read it and found in it: There is no changing the creation, that is the upright religion, so he, may Allah be pleased with him, erased with his hand one of the two lams and wrote it: There is no changing the creation of Allah. He said: And he, may Allah be pleased with him, found in it: Look at your food and drink, it has not rotted, so he erased the nun and wrote it: it has not rotted. And he, may Allah be pleased with him, read from it: So respite the disbelievers, so he erased the alif and wrote it: So grant respite; he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And I know not but he said in it; then Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, looked in it, then I set out with it to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, so they established it in the codices thus. This is a weak chain."
"4182","Chapter: Uthman's gathering people, may Allah be pleased with him, upon a single mode. Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, heard a man saying: The first mode; so Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: What is the first mode? A man said to him: O Ibn Abbas, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, as teacher to the people of Kufah, so they memorized his recitation; then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, changed the recitation, so they call it the first mode. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: It is the last mode Jibril, peace and blessings be upon him, presented to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4183","Chapter: reciting with melodies. Abu Yala said: Nasr ibn Ali narrated us, al-Harith ibn Murrah narrated, from Isl, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does not chant the Quran is not of us."
"4184","Al-Bazzar said: Ishaq ibn Ziyad narrated us, Maqil ibn Malik narrated, Abu Umayyah ibn Yala narrated, from Ayyub and Isl, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, with this."
"4185","And he said: Abdillah al-Sadusi narrated us, Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated, Shubah narrated, from Isl, with it. Al-Bazzar said: Shubah narrated from Isl only this, and none narrated it from Shubah except Rawh ibn Ubadah and Muadh ibn Muadh."
"4186","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid narrated us, Hammad narrated, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, would recite one night while the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) listened; so it was said to him, and he said: Had I known, I would have beautified it in beautifying and allured in alluring. Sound."
"4187","Abu Yala said: Ismail ibn Sayf narrated us, Awn ibn Amr narrated, al-Jurayri narrated, from Abdillah ibn Buraydah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite the Quran with sorrow, for it descended with sorrow."
"4188","Chapter: the excellence of reciters. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated us, Hafs Abu Umar al-Qari narrated, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, who said: I was with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he heard their clamor in the mosque reciting the Quran, so he said: Glad tidings to these; these were the most beloved of people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4189","Ishaq said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz informed us, Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir narrated, from Ismail ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi al-Muhajir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever recites the Quran, acts upon what is in it, and dies in the congregation, he is raised on the Day of Resurrection with the noble scribes; whoever recites the Quran while it slips from him, Allah the Exalted gives him his reward twice; whoever is keen upon it yet cannot master it nor abandons it, Allah the Exalted raises him with the nobles of his family, favored over creatures as eagles are favored over other birds, and as an eye in a meadow over what is around it. Then a caller calls: Where are those whom tending livestock did not divert from reciting My Book? So they rise; one of them is clothed the crown of honor, given eternity in his right and everlasting in his left. Then his parents, if Muslim, are clothed a garment better than the world and what is in it, so they say: How is this for us, when our deeds did not reach it? It is said: Your child used to recite the Quran. This is a connected chain, but Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz is weak in hadith."
"4190","Abu Yala said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated, Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No envy except in two — the hadith."
"4191","Abu Bakr said: Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The Quran will appear on the Day of Resurrection and the man who had carried it is brought, and it appears as an adversary against him, saying: My Lord, You entrusted him with me, and he was an evil bearer; he transgressed my bounds, neglected my obligations, rode my disobedience, and abandoned my obedience; it keeps hurling proofs against him until it is said: Your affair with him; so it takes his hand not releasing him till it casts him upon a rock in the Fire. And the righteous servant who had carried it, guarding its command, is brought, and it appears as an adversary for him, saying: My Lord, You entrusted him with me, and he was an excellent bearer; he guarded my bounds, acted upon my obligations, avoided my disobedience, and acted upon my obedience; it keeps hurling proofs for him until it is said: Your affair with him; so it takes his hand, not releasing him till it clothes him the garment of brocade, binds upon him the crown of dominion, and gives him to drink the cup of wine. This is a fair chain."
"4192","Al-Harith said: al-Khalil ibn Zakariyya narrated us, Mujalid narrated, from al-Shabi, from al-Numan ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, blessed and exalted, has people of His among men. They said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are the people of the Quran."
"4193","Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated us, Hammad narrated, from Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Of magnifying Allah the Exalted is honoring the gray-haired Muslim, the bearer of the Quran, and the just ruler."
"4194","Ahmad narrated us, Hammad narrated, from Said al-Jurayri, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Fihri, who said: A man obtained from spoils twenty-five ounces of gold, so he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he supplicate for him, but he turned away from him; then he returned and he turned away from him; then he returned and he turned away from him, saying: What such-and-such gained is not better than what you gained; learn five verses."
"4195","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated, my father narrated, from Ibn Ishaq, with it."
"4196","Abu Bakr said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuzai, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us and said: Rejoice; do you not bear witness there is no god but Allah and I am the Messenger of Allah? They said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This Quran is a rope; one end in the hand of Allah the Exalted and one end in your hands; so hold to it, for you will never stray nor perish after it ever."
"4197","Abd said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated us, with it."
"4198","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu al-Nadr narrated us, Layth narrated, Said ibn Abi Said narrated me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, while sitting awaiting him; when he emerged he stood over them and sat, saying: Do you not bear witness there is no god but Allah alone with no partner, bear witness I am the Messenger of Allah, and bear witness this Quran is from Allah, mighty and majestic? They said: Yes, we bear witness to this. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rejoice, for this Quran is a rope from Allah the Exalted, one end in the hand of Allah the Exalted and one end in your hands; so hold fast to it, you will not stray nor perish after it ever."
"4199","Abu Bakr said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated us, Isa — he is Ibn al-Mukhtar — narrated, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what of a man who recites the first of the night then steals its last? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: When he recites the first of the night, its last restrains him from stealing."
"4200","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated, Sharik narrated, from al-Amash, from Yazid ibn Aban, from al-Hasan, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran is wealth with no poverty after it, and no wealth besides it."
